inl c_user_globals_dropped () = Task ("C-USER-GLOBALS-DROPPED", "C backend drops every user global: CodegenC seeds its dedupe set from env.globals (global x = if has_added.Add x then globals.Add x) but prints only its private globals buffer, never env.globals, so !!!!Global / core global \"...\" text (e.g. #include) never reaches C output; the same in upstream The Spiral Language 2/CodegenC.fs (lines 175-176, 898); every other codegen prints env.globals; CodegenC maps to the upstream file, so the fix goes upstream (COMPILER-UPSTREAM-ONION) instead of a local edit; repro: tmp/cglobal (global \"#include <tgmath.h>\" absent from main.c); lib/spiral math C arms use __builtin_* with _Generic meanwhile; 13:4x FIXED here (correction: the upstream-section exception was only for comment stripping; functional fixes in CodegenC are the normal workflow, exported to the fork with scripts/upstream.py export --since): the C program assembly now writes env.globals before its own includes (Seq.append env.globals globals), as every other codegen does; repro tmp/cglobal now emits #include <tgmath.h>; upstream PR candidate (The Spiral Language 2/CodegenC.fs line 898); 18:2x re-verified live with the committed compiler: a scratch repro (an !!!!Global call carrying the text #include <tgmath.h>) compiles to C and main.c carries #include <tgmath.h> on line 6, after the backend's own stdbool/stdint/stdio/stdlib/string includes (Seq.append globals env.globals in CodegenC, HEAD a602d846); the repro directory was deleted afterwards; the exported patch material at %LOCALAPPDATA%/spiral-bin/upstream-pr (2026-10-08) already contains the fix; opening the upstream PR is the user's call", TaskKind, P2, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,upstream,c", "current", "current")
inl ci_kino_shared_tmp_delete () = Task ("CI-KINO-SHARED-TMP-DELETE", "spiral ubuntu CI 18:34: 17 lib notebooks failed because spiral gleam/lua on CI delete their package dir = grandparent of the code file, which for Kino cells (/tmp/spiral_kino/<id>/main.gleam) is the shared /tmp/spiral_kino (other cells: could not write main.spi, remove_dir_all DirectoryNotEmpty); fix: package_owns_code (code under <package>/src) guards the CI deletion in process_gleam and process_lua; 21:1x verified locally with CI=1 and the Kino layout <root>/<id>/main.gleam plus a sibling <root>/other: unfixed CLI deleted the sibling and the root, fixed CLI keeps both (gleam.toml lands in the cell dir); apps/spiral rebuilt (run check: 43 notebook exports, rust/cuda ok) and shipped; first build failed because parsed_integer conv ran at peval for u = ref str via every BackendSwitch arm, now gated by a static integral check; 21:39 local CI=1 sweep with the fixed CLI: all 16 notebooks CI reported (sm, testing, guid, runtime, trace, resultm, base, date_time, math, optionm, listm, reflection, lustre, env, convert, console) exit 0 and %TEMP%/spiral_kino survives; parsing and file_system are the two known failures; awaits the next push; 01:2x spiral CI after the 21:49/22:56 pushes: only 2 notebooks left (from 17), and the new cause lines named them: date_time (Lua): ticks subtracted a hardcoded 14400 s (the authors UTC-4 offset) to cancel unix_epochs local-time os.time{1970..}, so it held only in UTC-4 (CI UTC: actual 6.21355824e17 vs 6.21355968e17, exactly 4 h); now subtracts the same os.time{1970..} value at runtime; file_system (Rust): asserted the cwd name was polyglot/spiral/fsharp, but programs run inside the spiral workspace inherit apps/kino (get_workspace_root_external is Error there by design); the test now asserts get_full_path \".\" names the same directory as get_current_directory (); verifying under CI=true and TZ=UTC0; 01:1x verified locally under CI=true: date_time passes with TZ=UTC0 (proven effective: os.time{1970} is 14400 locally, 0 under TZ=UTC0) and in local time, file_system passes; awaits push; 02:1x CI on the 00:31 push: polyglot green; dice ubuntu green, windows red only on the known WSL contract path (CI-SPIRAL-WASM-NATIVE); spiral red only on date_time + file_system, both fixed locally after that push (await the next push); 06:2x dice CI on the 05:40 push red on ubuntu: ui/rust_derive.ps1 stripped only numeric case names (U[SH]n_m), so readable US0_Some counted as an unknown type and US enums + the Heap structs holding them lost serde/borsh derives (5 E0277). Fixed the pattern to U[SH]n_<name>; regenerated dice ui passes cargo check locally. spiral + polyglot CI still running; 18:1x local re-verification under CI=true TZ=UTC0 with the shipped CLI: date_time exit 0 (228.9 s) and file_system exit 0 (123.6 s), and %TEMP%/spiral_kino survived the pair with 223 cell dirs before and after (the shared root is what process_gleam/process_lua used to delete); the notebook exports went to a temp dir so the tree is untouched; the CI run of those two notebooks lands with the next push", TaskKind, P0, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "ci,kino,spiral", "current", "current")
inl harness_native_lanes () = Task ("HARNESS-NATIVE-LANES", "spiral harness native tier ran ~1260 build-and-run jobs one after another (46 min of job time; 70 min suites under load): now 6 lanes (ForEach-Object -Parallel, SPIRAL_NATIVE_PARALLEL overrides), each with its own gleam project and zig cache, per-row native_ms in results.tsv; full suite single-flight-20261008-221030: 12.8 min wall, all 2036 rows parity, DISAGREE 0 (Gleam 1149 s and Lean 852 s of job time dominate)", TaskKind, P1, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "harness,performance", "current", "current")
inl dir_tree_html_repo () = Task ("DIR-TREE-HTML-REPO", "Move spiral/apps/dir-tree-html into its own repo i574n/dir-tree-html (C:\\home\\git\\dir-tree-html): spiral standards (build.ps1, staged native build with a run check, LF, no comments), reuse spiral (deps/spiral for core lib, BuildSpiral, scripts/core.ps1, CI shape), its own gh-pages CI, adapt every reference in spiral/polyglot/dice/i574n.github (workflow.ps1, spiral scripts/build.ps1, cargo workspace member); 23:2x moved: C:\\home\\git\\dir-tree-html holds build.ps1 (BuildSpiral -> dir_tree_html.rs -> cargo +nightly-2025-11-01 --release -> dist/DirTreeHtml + --self-test run check), Cargo.toml standalone with spiral release profile, package.spiproj core- from deps/spiral, scripts/init.ps1 (links deps/spiral to the sibling spiral checkout, cloning it when missing, then spiral scripts/init-app-build.ps1), scripts/workflow.ps1 + publish.ps1 (renders dist/index.html with itself), .github/workflows/gh-pages.yml (ubuntu+windows build, gh-pages push), LICENSE, LF; spiral: apps/dir-tree-html removed, workspace member + Cargo.lock entry dropped, scripts/init-app-build.ps1 extracted from init.ps1 (fork + app toolchain, reused by app repos), scripts/dir-tree-html.ps1 helper (repo next to the resolved spiral checkout, clone+build when the binary is missing, prints its path); consumers rewired: spiral workflow/publish, polyglot publish/publish-bin/build.livemd, dice init/publish/publish-bin, i574n.github init (clone + init) and build; verified locally: init + build + self-test ok, all 5 consumer call forms resolve to the new binary, render ok, 17 scripts parse, LF; awaits commit + push for its CI; 00:3x binary renamed DirTreeHtml -> dir-tree-html (the package name, Cargo default): [[bin]] keeps path = dir_tree_html.rs at the repo root, which makes cargo require name, so name = package name; build.ps1, publish.ps1, README and spiral scripts/dir-tree-html.ps1 use dist/dir-tree-html(.exe); rebuilt + self-test + publish index ok, helper resolves it from dice; 01:0x the published page (https://i574n.github.io/dir-tree-html/) showed only the linux binary: publish rendered dist/ instead of the repo tree; fix by reuse: spiral scripts/publish-tree.ps1 (extracted from spiral publish.ps1: rsync the repo with the standard filters into dist, _-rename dotfiles, render index; -Root, -Tool, copies the tool aside because dist is wiped), spiral publish.ps1 and dir-tree-html publish.ps1 both call it; rsync install for Windows moved from spiral init.ps1 into init-app-build.ps1; local Windows runs exposed a latent bug in the shared filter: choco rsync is cygwin and globbed the unquoted */ and * filters into extra sources (target/release leaked into dist), fixed with CYGWIN=noglob; verified: dir-tree-html dist = exactly its repo files + index, spiral dist top-level unchanged; awaits push. polyglot/dice/i574n.github publish.ps1 keep their own rsync copies (same latent glob bug on Windows) - move them to publish-tree next; 01:1x publish-tree.ps1 now serves all five repos (spiral, dir-tree-html, polyglot with -Include *.png, dice, i574n.github): one filter list built as an argument array, CYGWIN=noglob; each publish verified locally: exit 0, index rendered, no target/release leaks; -Include verified (*.lock adds Cargo.lock); 18:4x re-verified: the repo is clean at 43c3558 (4 commits) with dist/dir-tree-html(.exe) built, and publish-tree.ps1 serves all five consumers, spiral, dir-tree-html, polyglot, dice and i574n.github each calling it from their own scripts/publish.ps1; the commit and the push are the user's call", Feature, P0, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "repo,spiral,ci", "current", "current")
inl alphabet_ci_xelatex () = Task ("ALPHABET-CI-XELATEX", "alphabet CI red since 2025-11: apps/documents notebook asserts every crowbook output Ok, but on ubuntu-24.04 xelatex fails for test.md.pdf and test.hangul.md.pdf (\"xelatex did not return successfully\"); html/epub pass. Capture the xelatex .log in CI (missing TeX package or Nanum font with the cached texlive-xetex), fix the apt package set or the crowbook template. 2026-10-09: alphabet synced with dice (publish-tree.ps1, scripts/dir-tree-html.ps1, gleam 1.19.1, setup-bun); 2026-10-12: cause found: crowbook 0.17.1 tex.template needs hyphenat/breakurl/lettrine/stdpage/spverbatim/newunicodechar (texlive-latex-extra, confirmed hyphenat.sty via packages.ubuntu.com noble) and ulem (texlive-plain-generic); CI only installed texlive-xetex. Added both to gh-pages.yml apt step and scripts/init.ps1; plain + hangul PDFs build locally (MiKTeX); awaits CI after push; 2026-10-12 root cause from the new xelatex diagnostics step (run 38280261902): every package crowbook's template loads is present (english.ldf, lmodern, fontspec, xunicode ...), but hyperref under XeTeX (PU encoding) inputs puenc-greek.def, which Debian/Ubuntu ship in texlive-lang-greek (\"! LaTeX Error: File puenc-greek.def not found\", l.612, Emergency stop; MiKTeX installs it on demand locally, hence no local repro); fix (uncommitted): texlive-lang-greek added to gh-pages.yml apt packages and scripts/init.ps1; diagnostics step kept (runs only on failure); 18:00 run 38295913402 (commit 82538e8 with texlive-lang-greek): ubuntu build PASSES (20m18s), so the Linux xelatex fix is confirmed; windows-2025 fails in apps/documents: crowbook \"failed to run command xelatex\": no TeX at all on the Windows runner (earlier runs hid it because ubuntu failed first and cancelled windows; locally xelatex is scoop latex = MiKTeX); fix (uncommitted): gh-pages.yml windows-only miktex step (choco install miktex, PATH, [MPM]AutoInstall=1, update package db) and the xelatex diagnostics step now runs on any OS on failure; untested in CI (CLI flags from MiKTeX docs), the Hangul PDF may additionally need a Korean font on Windows; superseded by DOCUMENTS-TECTONIC (user decision 2026-10-12 18:53); the Linux fix (texlive-lang-greek) is verified green in run 38312799493", Story, P2, D3, 100u32, Done, "", "ci,alphabet,dice-parity", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_alphabet_ci_xelatex () = Planning ("ALPHABET-CI-XELATEX", Minutes 90u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl backend_switch_hermetic () = Task ("BACKEND-SWITCH-HERMETIC", "BackendSwitch evaluation, state of the art for both cores: every non-current arm is evaluated in key order on every backend for its type only (compile-time state identical across backends, a type mismatch is an error everywhere, like Kotlin expect/actual), hermetically (throwaway seq, its own variable counter from 1<<<30 so no tag reaches the residual: single-flight had numbered around the discarded arms, the cause of the BackendSwitch cross-core residual class), once per (backend, arm closure) per build (type cache; hopac gains one, cleared with the JP dicts). Hopac validates again (backend_switch_validate_all on; it skipped other arms since 2026-10-02 for speed, unsound). 2026-10-12: edits in spiral_compiler.fs, unbuilt; proof ladder single-flight -> hopac -> restore pending; proven and blessed 2026-10-12 05:07: cross-core residual differences 83 -> 0, behaviour differences 0, hopac missing 0, restore 0 of 3,768; hopac rung 76 min vs 54.5 (validation cost ~40% under full load: next, evaluate type-check-only join points inline); 2026-10-12 closed: blessed twice (dev40 05:07, dev41 08:36), cross-core 0 differences; the ~40% hopac validation cost moves to HOPAC-SPEED", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,hopac,parity", "current", "current")
inl c_format_any () = Task ("C-FORMAT-ANY", "C arm for lib sm_real format_debug_real (format of any value): frontier_format_any_union / frontier_join_format_any C rows build-fail on backend_switch_has_no_C_arm_in_lib_spiral, so Rust has no oracle there, and sm format test has no C expectation. Structure can reuse the portable serialize_union_rust/record_fold/tuple arms (as Zig/Lean do); the C-specific gap is primitive-to-string: lib globals precede the C prelude that defines StringLit, so a lib helper cannot build a String; needs a compiler-emitted helper or a portable Spiral digit loop; 2026-10-12 done in lib (sm.livemd cells 46/58/87, exported sm_real.spir): append_real C via StaticStringConcat, format_debug_real C arm (snprintf into a static buffer, String built at the call site with StringLit; shortest round-trip floats, quoted chars; unions/tuples/records/nominals/arrays via the portable arms), format_real C arms (empty, prim p with %+.6f/NaN/Infinity, append). frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any C now build and exit 0 (were build-fail); a probe of union/record/tuple/f32 formatting prints byte-identical text in C and Rust; 2026-10-12 compiler lane: C rows frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any build, exit 0, Rust agrees; Delphi still fails (backend_switch_has_no_Delphi_arm_in_lib_spiral): needs a Delphi arm in lib/spiral BackendSwitch; 07:47 Delphi done (lib/spiral/sm'_real.spir, uncommitted): Delphi arms for append_real (s + x), format_debug_real (bool BoolToStr true/false, char, floats FloatToStr, tuples/records/unions/nominals through the portable arms, ints IntToStr) and format_real's three switches (empty, per-value: floats signed fixed 6 decimals, chars, strings; append); frontier_format_any_union and frontier_join_format_any Delphi now build with fpc and exit 0 (were build-fail); oracle rows flip at the next pack's bless", TaskKind, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "lib,c,format", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_c_format_any () = Planning ("C-FORMAT-ANY", Minutes 60u32, Load Light, Assigned Agent)
inl self_checking_samples () = Task ("SELF-CHECKING-SAMPLES", "Samples and tests as self-checking source (user design session 2026-10-12, nothing fixed yet; dogfood before committing): no prose, code only; expectations live inline in the source of truth, expect-test style (ppx_expect, Rust expect-test/insta, Zig test blocks): a promote run rewrites the expected blocks in place, git diff is the review, a clean run leaves the file untouched, dev mode can persist cell results and clear them after a successful run; the oracle backend is configurable per file or cell (C default) and other backends are checked against it, replacing hand-written per-backend copies and the uncommittable cache EXPECTED.tsv; end state: .spi itself is the notebook (compiler-understood test blocks with inline expectations, Zig/Rust #[test]-like), .livemd becomes a view, so edits are plain file edits; negative samples stay as test blocks whose expectation is a diagnostic (rustc UI tests, clang expected-error); multi-file packages must work (package-manager litmus): measure the dice symlink cost on Windows, then compare compiler-resolved path dependencies (Cargo path/workspaces) with junctions. Phases: 1 benchmark the same sample family through the harness and through Kino (warm workers, parallel cells: KINO-PARALLEL-CACHE); 2 measure dice symlink package resolution on Windows; 3 dogfood one family (ebpf_* 27 samples) as an inline-expectation file with promote mode, keeping harness rows byte-identical; 4 decide; phase 2 first look 2026-10-12: dice reaches lib through two chained symlinks (dice/deps/polyglot -> polyglot, polyglot/deps/spiral -> spiral); raw I/O over the 315 lib .spi/.spir files through the chain is 1.4-2.5x slower warm (hundreds of ms at worst, not the main cost); the compiler normalizes package paths with Path.GetFullPath, which collapses .. but does not resolve links, so a package identity is the path spelling: the same lib reached as dice\\deps\\polyglot\\deps\\spiral\\lib and as spiral\\lib is two packages (parsed, cached and watched twice), the likelier cost and a blocker for one canonical identity per package in a package manager; next: time a dice compile via the link path vs the direct path, then try canonicalizing package roots (FinalPath/ResolveLinkTarget) in the compiler; test block prototype 2026-10-12: survey of the 39 lib notebooks: 1,904 cells, 364 test cells (44 static, 12 in .spir real code, 11 print_code), targets fsharp 169 / cuda 126 / rust 107 / gleam 93 / typescript 93 / lua 34 / c 1, per-target options (rust -d crates, gleam -t javascript -d), all timeouts default; test cells hold top-level-only statements (open, nominal, #r), so a test is a scoped group of top-level statements, not a function: syntax `///> target opts` lines then `test name [static] [print_code] =` with an indented body; semantics = file prefix up to the block + the body (Kino notebook-prefix semantics, Spiral is top-down so position preserves scope); compiler: blockParsingParse returns Error [] for a block starting with `test` (both cores; the same no-statement result as comment-only blocks), unbuilt; converter livemd -> spi/spir with test blocks reproduces the current exports byte for byte in 38 of 40 files (benchmark and lustre carry stale doc text from cell outputs); spi -> livemd adapter round-trips all 37 notebooks with every test source preserved (order of .spir tests and one directive line moved); next: run original vs regenerated notebooks through Kino and compare per-test results, then the build with the parser change; dice package identity fix (2026-10-12, unbuilt): canonical_directory in the shared SpiProj schema resolves every path component with Directory.ResolveLinkTarget (symlinks and junctions), used for packageDir/moduleDir combines and the default package dir, so a package reached through deps/polyglot/deps/spiral and through spiral/ is one package; no harness path crosses a link (checked), so sample outputs must stay byte-identical; to measure: a dice compile through the link path before and after, and dice/alphabet packageDir pointing straight at the sibling ../spiral/lib (CI and local init already create that layout); dice measured 2026-10-12 (cold compile of lib/dice.spi to Rust, one process each): old compiler through the symlinks 19.3 s, new compiler (canonical package dirs + tonight batch) through the symlinks 17.4 s, new compiler with packageDir ../spiral/lib (no links) 18.0 s; the new compiler output is byte-identical through links and without them (one package identity), so dice can drop the links; a single cold compile shows no real symlink cost, so the slowdown the user saw likely sits elsewhere (cargo/rustc over symlinked dirs, Kino watching, repeated warm compiles): measure those next. Test blocks: with the new parser all 364 test blocks embedded in 35 lib files compile to byte-identical C (pristine-copy check; an earlier failure came from stale files in a reused copy, not from test blocks); 2026-10-12 lib/spiral migrated (unstaged): the 39 lib notebooks are now their .spi (370 test blocks; sm' keeps its real-mode code and tests in sm'_real.spir); the 39 .livemd are deleted; Kino document domain (apps/kino/spi/document.spi, Spiral) gained to_spi_tested/to_spir_tested (test cells -> test blocks named by heading) and from_spi/parse_spi (blocks -> test cells), byte-identical to the validated prototype on all 37 notebooks (644 cells); run_notebook.ps1 --path x.spi runs a source file as a notebook and never writes over it; lib/spiral/build.ps1 and near/wallet/build.ps1 run .spi and write <nb>.spi.ipynb (README links -> .spi.html); evidence: per-test A/B original livemd vs round trip 353 tests in 31 notebooks same outcome, .spi run directly math/seq/sm' 79 tests same, the 7 lib-dependent harness samples 53 rows identical to the blessed repro, Kino mix test 140/0; open: the full lib build from .spi (running 09:30), then folder samples -> test blocks; 10:10 user committed the lib migration (dc7b955c 09:51); all 39 lib notebooks run from .spi with exit 0 (Kino run_notebook, 2 at a time), 268 tests in 29 notebooks have the same outcome as the original livemd runs (physics/parsing/rust outputs vanished before comparison, both matched in the A/B); .spi -> .livemd kept for a cell UI: from_spi now restores /// doc runs as markdown cells and adds a #### name (test) heading only when the name would not re-derive; run_notebook --livemd-path writes the Livebook view, --with-tests exports back; 39/39 lib notebooks .spi -> .livemd -> .spi byte-identical, 26/39 livemd byte-identical to the deleted originals, 3,503 of 3,531 old cells verbatim (sm' real cells move ahead of the rest); Kino mix test covers both properties; UI direction: Zed (LSP + runnables, ZED-TEST-RUNNABLES) as the editor, Livebook via --livemd-path for rich outputs; split 2026-10-12 into SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS, TEST-EXPECT-PERSIST, CONFIGURABLE-ORACLE, KINO-RUN-ONE-TEST, NOTEBOOKS-TO-SPI-SIBLINGS, SPIR-INTERLEAVED-LIVEMD, ZED-TEST-RUNNABLES; children also TEST-RUN-REPORT, TEST-IDENTITY-I18N, SOURCE-BOUNDARY-AUTHORITY (from the 2026-10-12 external review of dc7b955c)", Epic, P1, D7, 55u32, Active, "", "samples,kino,compiler,dogfood,packages", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_self_checking_samples () = Planning ("SELF-CHECKING-SAMPLES", Minutes 300u32, Load Light, Assigned User)
inl note_of_self_checking_samples_1 () = Note ("SELF-CHECKING-SAMPLES", 1u32, "2026-10-14T11:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-14 07:39 touch: phase-3 family measured: ebpf_* is 31 samples, 407 source lines, 166 oracle rows (C, Ebpf 31 each; Bend, Delphi, F#, Rust 25; OpenQasm 4), every C exit 0: one ebpf test-block file with inline expectation exit 0 replaces 31 folders")
inl note_of_self_checking_samples_2 () = Note ("SELF-CHECKING-SAMPLES", 2u32, "2026-10-15T10:51Z", "main-thread", "06:51 groom: unchanged design; the enabler above (compiler-owned block boundaries) is in place. Next grain: one ebpf sample rewritten with an inline expectation, run by the harness through --test-blocks.")
inl test_signal_metrics () = Task ("TEST-SIGNAL-METRICS", "Test signal and cost metrics drive sample cleanup (2026-10-12, mined from 926 single-flight + 710 hopac runs since 09-26, 362,684 rows): silent rows (no text or outcome change in 20+ runs) are only 3.5% of median cost, so pruning by silence alone saves little; Kotlin is 41% of the total cost (about 92 min native for 253 rows, ~22 s each, JVM kotlinc startup) with 13 rows that ever flipped: batch all Kotlin samples into one kotlinc run (one package per sample) and run each with java -cp; F# is 21% (compile only, mega_omniledger 180 s); the nested-combination families heap_/closure_/union_/array_/case_/method_/mut_ hold ~1,600 rows and about half the cost with at most 50 ever-flipped rows (case_: 323 rows, 0 flips): compact them into composite programs that cover many combinations per compile, keeping every ever-flipped row standalone; highest-signal families: native_ 391 flipped rows, dynamic_ 169, managed_ 108, ts_ 76, frontier_ 52; text-change co-change twins (1,965) are driven by global renames, so redundancy must be measured on outcome flips; next: Kotlin batching in test.ps1, a fast tier (all ever-flipped rows + one per family) for the edit loop, full matrix for bless; Kotlin done 2026-10-12: tests/native-shims/kotlin-server/KotlinServer.java compiles every Kotlin row of a run in one warm JVM (K2JVMCompiler in process, -no-stdlib against kotlin-stdlib.jar), a pre-pass in test.ps1 before the native lanes, lanes only run java -cp; Kotlin row median 285 ms vs ~22 s (single-flight-20261012-050734, all 246 rows agree, bless + reproduction 0 differences); a full single-flight suite 50 -> 26 min; next: same treatment for Swift (swiftc ~3 s/row), compaction of the low-signal families, the fast tier; 2026-10-12 12:00 recomputed over all single-flight runs (6,079 rows, 4,011 with >=5 runs, summed row time 8,162 s): signal (output changes + 2x status flips) concentrates in native_ (3,546), dynamic_ (1,218), managed_ (1,084), frontier_ (779), ts_ (631); silent cost concentrates in array_ (200 of 270 rows silent, 1,106 s), heap_ (205/276, 978 s), union_ (116/192), method_ (91/126), mut_ (65/90); negative_/contract_ rows are silent by design (stable rejections); coverage-first fast tiers (every backend and family, then signal per second): 300 s = 734 rows / 3.7% of the time / 51% of historical signal, 600 s = 1,098 / 7.4% / 73%, 1,200 s = 1,657 / 14.7% / 88%; lists in the job scratch (metrics/fast-tier-*.tsv, compaction.py); next: a harness fast suite from the 600 s list (harness.psd1, compiler lane) and folding the silent combinatorial families into fewer test blocks (SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS); caveat: signal = changed during development, a proxy, not proof a row catches bugs; 2026-10-14 00:43 fast suite built (uncommitted): pwsh tmp/scripts/test.ps1 -Suite fast; tmp/scripts/fast-tier.ps1 mines this mode's <cache>/runs/*/results.tsv at run time (~21 s, examples/ ids folded into samples/, runs >20% timeouts skipped) and writes <run dir>/fast-tier.tsv, so no generated list goes into the tree (harness.psd1 keeps only the hand-written policy Fast = budget 600 s, mature 5 runs, timeout share 0.2, cost = median of the last 7 runs); passes: cheapest row per backend and family, every row with <5 runs, then signal per second; a non-C row brings its C oracle row inside the budget; rows absent from the latest full run always run; -Bless/-Record refuse it; recomputed over 916 single-flight runs (reference single-flight-20261013-225859, 3,872 rows, summed median cost 5,866 s, signal 9,908): 300 s = 455 rows / 5.1% / 29.5% of signal, 600 s = 725 / 10.2% / 55.9%, 1,200 s = 1,297 / 20.5% / 79.2%, every backend and family covered (the 10-12 figures were lower-cost because examples/ and samples/ halves counted as separate rows and C oracle rows were not budgeted); gpt5parallel over 716 hopac runs: 462 rows / 6.0% of 9,950 s / 60.8%; static checks: both scripts parse, all 725 fast keys map to -Suite all jobs (4,094 jobs; the 222 new Bend rows have no history yet and run as unseen); not run (main thread owns test.ps1 runs); next: one pwsh tmp/scripts/test.ps1 -Suite fast -Native on the main thread, compare wall time to the budget and check for no false REGRESSED, then the Swift warm-compile treatment and SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS; 2026-10-14 01:57 cost by backend now (normalized history, summed median row cost 5,866 s): Gleam 1,261 s for 129 rows (~9.8 s/row, 21%, signal 544), Rust 911 s (signal 3,081), Swift 781 s for 248 rows (13%, signal 157), Lean 590 s, C 531 s, Delphi 413 s, F# 302 s, Zig 297 s, Bend 211 s, Kotlin 132 s after the warm server; so the next warm-compile batching targets are Gleam (one gleam build per row) and Swift; silent rows (no residual change or outcome flip in >=5 runs): 1,708 of 3,868 mature rows (44%) holding 1,459 s (25%), led by array_ 190 rows/270 s, heap_ 202/217 s, mega 252/200 s, union_ 120/119 s, ebpf_ 114/92 s, method_ 90/92 s, mut_ 65/74 s; contract_ 330 rows are silent by design (stable rejections, 47 s); 2026-10-14 02:22 redundancy measured on outcome flips (job scratch metrics/coflip.ps1, 916 single-flight runs, examples/ ids folded): 5,613 rows ever seen, 992 ever flipped an outcome (compile status or native status/exit/oracle), but those 992 rows have only 157 distinct flip histories (the run indices where they flipped): 835 rows are co-flip twins of another row and only 76 rows have a unique history; the largest groups are whole backend lanes flipping in the same runs (146 Wasm rows, 125 Zig, 72 Bend, 51 Rust, 44 Bend, 42 Gleam, 35 Lua, 33 Rust array_), i.e. most outcome flips are backend-wide events (toolchain, prelude, codegen switch) that one row per backend would have caught; next for the tier: keep one cheapest row per flip-history group and spend the freed budget on rows with unique histories; caveat: no-toolchain/ran transitions count as flips, so environment changes inflate the groups; 2026-10-14 02:28 fast tier made redundancy-aware (decided after measuring both): a pass now takes the cheapest row of every distinct outcome-flip history before the signal-per-second pass, and that pass skips rows that only repeat a covered history with no residual change; 600 s: 671 rows, 10.2% of cost, all 130 flip histories of the current rows, 49.3% of the raw signal (before: 725 rows, 85 of 130 histories, 55.9% raw signal, the difference being co-flip twins counted again); 300 s: 316 rows, 129/130, 19.8%; 1,200 s: 1,237 rows, 130/130, 78.1%; fixed in review: test.ps1 added keys through a subexpression that enumerates the HashSet (fails on the first row), now plain if/else, its loop checked standalone against the list (671 fast, 3,201 rest); a signal-pass break that could stop early is now continue; tmp/AGENTS.md documents -Suite fast with these numbers and notes that unseen rows run outside the budget (222 new Bend rows, ~947 jobs until a full run); 2026-10-14 03:14 Gleam lane measured (a copy of the warm <cache>/scratch/single-flight/gleam-native-0 project, 5 samples): gleam build 2.2-3.8 s, then gleam run -m spiral_run 3.1-4.4 s, but erl -noshell -pa build/dev/erlang/*/ebin -eval 'spiral_run:main().' runs the same build in 1.1-1.4 s with the same exit codes (0, 42) and byte-identical stdout (printf_portable, portable_console_lines, static_string); so replacing gleam run with a direct erl call in test.ps1's Gleam lane (around line 482) saves about 2 s per row (~260 s of Gleam's 1,261 s), and compiling every Gleam row of a run as modules of one project with one gleam build (like the Kotlin warm server) would remove most of the remaining ~2.5 s build per row; not changed tonight because the main thread is running and blessing suites with this harness; 2026-10-14 03:42 Gleam batching prototyped outside the harness: 40 sample residuals copied as modules prog_k with one runner module run_k each into a copy of the warm gleam-native-0 project: one gleam build for all 40 took 5.5 s (vs about 2.7 s per row today, ~108 s), then erl -noshell -pa build/dev/erlang/*/ebin -eval 'run_k:main().' per program 1.07 s each (42.8 s); all 40 exit codes and stdout hashes equal single-flight-20261013-225859's Gleam rows; so a Gleam pre-pass in test.ps1 (like the Kotlin warm server: write every Gleam row as a module of one project, one build, lanes only run erl) cuts the lane about 5x (40 rows: ~48 s vs ~240 s); running all programs in one VM with captured group leaders would cut the 1 s erl start too; left for the main thread since it is running and blessing suites with this harness; 2026-10-14 03:59 budget curve with the flip-history pass (single-flight, 3,872 rows, 5,866 s summed): 300 s 316 rows 19.8% of raw signal 129/130 histories; 450 s 539 rows 35.0% 130/130; 600 s 671 rows 49.3%; 900 s 962 rows 68.7%; 1,200 s 1,237 rows 78.1%; 1,800 s 1,632 rows 86.6%; gpt5parallel at 600 s: 346 rows, 6.0% of 9,950 s, 50.7% of raw signal, 197 of 200 histories; 2026-10-14 04:14 fast runs no longer feed the signal: a run directory holding fast-tier.tsv updates last-seen state and costs but adds no changes or flips, so picked rows do not gain signal by being picked (600 s tier unchanged: 671 rows, 130/130 histories, no fast runs exist yet); documented in tmp/AGENTS.md; stopped 04:14: the next step is the main thread's first pwsh tmp/scripts/test.ps1 -Suite fast -Native (compare wall time with the 600 s budget, expect no false REGRESSED), then the Gleam pre-pass and Swift batching in test.ps1 and SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS", Feature, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "harness,metrics,samples,performance", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_test_signal_metrics () = Planning ("TEST-SIGNAL-METRICS", Minutes 300u32, Load Medium, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_test_signal_metrics_1 () = Note ("TEST-SIGNAL-METRICS", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:50Z", "main-thread", "08:50 cost shares re-measured on single-flight-20261014-073517 (4,094 jobs, 3.77 h of summed compile+native): Gleam 27.0% (129 rows, ~28 s each, a cold gleam build + run per row), Rust 13.4%, Swift 10.5%, Bend 10.2%, C 8.6%, Lean 8.0%, Delphi 5.5%, Zig 5.0%, F# 2.9% (compile only), Wgsl 2.4%. Kotlin left the top 10 since the warm Kotlin server (was 41%). Next levers: Gleam pre-pass (lane B), Swift batching (SWIFT-WARM-COMPILE, main), then the composite-program compaction of the nested families.")
