inl note_of_package_registry_design_5 () = Note ("PACKAGE-REGISTRY-DESIGN", 5u32, "2026-10-17T19:02Z", "main-thread", "15:02 main-thread, user direction 2026-10-17: the Rust/cargo ecosystem (rayon, many small crates under the 1k-line gate, compiled in parallel) is the end-product target to the end, not Zig and its package manager, and no dynamic languages for end products. So the registry design builds on cargo: Spiral packages map to crates, locks pin crate versions and Spiral package digests together, and the 1k-line crate split is a design input the registry must make cheap (many tiny crates, one lock).")
inl social_transport_sim () = Task ("SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM", "X-like simulator as Kino's battle test (user 2026-10-12: deferring it was a bad call; it truly battle-tests Kino): a deterministic, seeded event simulator of many humans/agents publishing Spiral cells as posts (post, reply = downstream cell, quote/link = upstream dependency, edit = new revision, delete, like = demand/interest, unlike = withdrawn demand), with delayed, duplicate, reordered and stale deliveries and cancelled consumers, driving Kino's real compile/run path (warm server, concurrent cells over shared prefixes); invariants checked on every schedule: each visible result equals the sequential re-run of its exact revision, stale revisions never surface, shared prefixes compile once (cache hits counted), withdrawn demand cancels work and frees memory, no cross-request contamination (hopac fix 57 class), equivalent reorderings commute; phase 1: event log format + replay runner + 5 invariants + a 100-cell scenario from lib notebooks; phase 2: scale (10^4 cells), memory ceilings, agent personas; later: transport frames (280-char, twitter-text v3 weighting, NFC) and optional real X/Bluesky/Mastodon mirrors; 2026-10-12 phase 1 built (apps/xsim, untracked, Elixir, path dep on apps/kino): Scenario (cells of any .spi via Kino parse_spi -> posts: definitions as a reply chain, tests replying to the latest definition; seeded likes/unlikes/edits; deliveries shuffled in a window with duplicates), World (idempotent per event index, causal per post+user, replies/edits/likes before their post buffered), Runner (demand-driven: wanted posts -> prefix chain key -> one task per key, cancel on withdrawn demand or edit, results in completion order), Invariants (sequential equivalence vs a fresh baseline, no stale results, one compile per prefix, withdrawn demand hidden, commute across schedules), fault knobs (naive_visibility, cancel: false, rerun_completed, leaking executor) each proven to trip its invariant; mix test 8/8 x5 seeds; mix xsim.run on lib math/date_time/sm' with the fake executor: up to 195 posts, 1,068 deliveries, 104 duplicates, 80 buffered, all invariants ok, commute ok; a leaking executor fails sequential equivalence and commute; next: --executor kino on Kino's new in-memory Notebook.evaluate (agent B), then scale and memory ceilings; 11:55 first runs on real Kino (Notebook.evaluate): two genuine findings, KINO-SOURCE-DIAGNOSTICS (temp paths make outputs nondeterministic) and TEST-PRELUDE-SEMANTICS (tests depend on an earlier static test's open); xsim now models Kino's linear prefixes (tests on the trunk, --branch tests optional) and normalizes temp paths; 12:15 simulator made incremental (prefix key = H(parent key, source), edits re-key only their subtree, the runner only touches posts each delivery changed, refcounted prefix keys, lazy prefix sources): full lib corpus (36 notebooks with tests as interleaved author threads, 1,651 posts, 5,916 deliveries, 529 duplicates, 4 edits + 2 breaking edits per thread) 626.5 s -> 17.5 s simulator time with the fake executor, ~linear (228/535/1,651 posts: 2.3/5.1/17.5 s), every invariant and commute ok; tests 10/10 (new: root edit re-keys the subtree); multi-notebook corpus (--spi a,b,...) and --breaking edits added; 12:25 transport frames: Xsim.Frames splits a cell into numbered frames within 280 weighted characters (twitter-text v3 ranges: Latin weight 1, CJK/Hangul 2; NFC; split at grapheme boundaries; header \"<post> k/n\"), World buffers frames per post and applies the rebuilt post at its earliest frame index once complete (parent missing -> pending), --frames in mix xsim.run; tests 12/12 (Hangul cell -> several frames, each <= 280, shuffled rebuild exact; framed corpus with 30% duplicates and a 20-wide shuffle window keeps every invariant and commutes with the unframed run); 12:20 real-Kino finding KINO-DAEMON-START-RACE; 12:40 --churn N (N edits cycling the root of each thread, woven through the stream: agent-rewrite storms re-key every prefix and cancel in-flight work) exposed an over-strict invariant, not a runner bug: cancelled-then-restarted prefixes counted as double compiles (14 cancels, 5 keys restarted); one_compile_per_prefix is now starts - cancellations <= 1 per key (the rerun_completed fault still trips it); --save/--replay delivery logs (JSON lines) for exact bug reproduction; full lib corpus with frames: 1,651 posts -> 6,643 events, ~8,000 deliveries incl. ~1,330 duplicates, all invariants + commute ok in 20-24 s per schedule; tests 14/14; 12:50 phase-2 scale reached with the fake executor: lib corpus x6 as 216 author threads (--spi-file, since mix.bat caps the command line at 8,191 chars), 9,906 posts -> 40,585 events -> 46,604 deliveries incl. 6,019 duplicates, all framed, with edits, breaking edits and root churn: every invariant + commute ok, 118.9 s simulator time, peak BEAM 110 MB, 300 s wall incl. the sequential baseline; next: real-Kino scenarios at growing size, memory ceilings of the daemon side, agent personas; 13:20 in CI (uncommitted): apps/xsim/build.ps1 (mix test; the framed lib corpus with edits, breaking edits and churn over 2 schedules + commute; unless -fast a cold 2-concurrent real-Kino scenario that guards KINO-DAEMON-START-RACE, then the daemon is stopped) runs from spiral scripts/build.ps1 after the lib notebooks; local -fast run 190 s wall incl. deps and compile; 14:20 semantic oracle added after the KINO-C-FALSE-PASS report: breaking edits are known-wrong revisions, so on the real Kino path any visible ok result for a post whose current revision is a breaking edit is a violation (breaking_edits_fail); a lenient executor that passes everything is caught, an honest one is not (test); the concurrent-vs-sequential invariants alone cannot see a false pass because both sides are wrong the same way; tests 15/15; 18:53: CI run 38312827285 (spiral 17:47 commit) failed in apps/xsim mix test: the churn test asserted map_size(results) > 6, which depends on timing (a fast runner cancels every stale revision, leaving exactly 6); replaced with a deterministic check (the root ends at the last churn edit); 15/15 pass on seeds 0, 1, 7, 42, 1234; uncommitted; 2026-10-14 01:14 agent personas (uncommitted): Xsim.Personas, mix xsim.run --personas lurker=N,fickle=N,editor=N,breaker=N; lurkers like about half the posts, fickle users toggle like/unlike 5 times on one post (withdrawn demand, cancel and restart), editors rewrite one definition 3 times and then revert to the original source (ABA: the prefix key returns to an earlier value), breakers push a breaking test edit and then fix it back (the broken source joins scenario.broken); persona streams interleave round-robin and weave with churn; fixed an off-by-one: --edits 0 produced one edit; tests 25/25 on seeds 0, 1, 7, 42 (new: 4 schedules with 30% duplicates and a 10-wide window, every invariant, breaking_edits_fail with an honest executor, every post ends at its original source, the schedules commute; an unknown persona raises); full lib corpus (36 notebooks) with personas lurker=2,fickle=3,editor=2,breaker=2, frames, edits, breaking edits and churn: 1,662 posts, 8,467 events, about 9,730 deliveries, 5,690-5,745 prefixes, 47-54 cancels, every invariant and commute ok, 17.5-21.6 s per schedule, peak BEAM 70 MB; next: the real-Kino size ladder with daemon memory ceilings (script ready at the job scratch xsim/ladder.ps1: isolated port 13977, peak working set of the daemon process tree, retire over TCP), waiting for the heavy gate; 2026-10-14 02:04 real-Kino size ladder (job scratch xsim/ladder.ps1: its own daemon on port 13977 with SPIRAL_KINO_COMPILER_IDLE_MIN=3, peak working set of the daemon process tree, retire over TCP, users 2, 2 schedules, concurrency 2, through the heavy gate): math.spi 3 cells: 3 prefixes, 52 s cold + 10 s, daemon tree peak 1,464 MB; 6 cells: 5 prefixes, 69-101 s + 22-34 s, 1,735-1,858 MB; 12 cells: 10 prefixes, 201 s + 103 s, 2,353 MB (one pool compiler reached 1,116 MB); 8 cells with --memo and personas fickle=2,editor=1,breaker=1: 8 prefixes, 43.8 s then 0.8 s (memo hits 15 then 43 cumulative, misses 13), 2,331 MB, every invariant and commute ok; daemon tree = erl 58 MB + 3 pool compilers (dotnet 374-1,116 MB) + 3 pwsh compiler_bridge relays at 101-114 MB each (about 320 MB, filed as KINO-BRIDGE-OVERHEAD); genuine finding: the 6-cell rung failed sequential equivalence and commute only because a failing Rust cell prints the OS thread id of its panic (thread '<unnamed>' (52388) panicked), so two runs of one revision differ; xsim's Invariants.comparable now normalizes the id as it did temp paths (test added, 26/26), rerun passes; Kino-side normalization filed as KINO-PANIC-THREAD-ID; apps/xsim/build.ps1's CI run now passes --personas lurker=2,fickle=3,editor=2,breaker=2 (that exact command: pass, 102 s wall); 2026-10-14 02:17 phase-2 scale with personas (fake executor): lib corpus x6 (216 author threads) with frames, edits, breaking edits, churn and personas lurker=2,fickle=3,editor=2,breaker=2 per thread: 9,972 posts -> 50,647 events -> about 58,160 deliveries incl. about 7,510 duplicates, 22,638-22,888 prefixes, 63-64 cancels, every invariant and commute ok, 89-93 s per schedule, peak BEAM 147 MB, 467 s wall; xsim tests 26/26 on seeds 0, 1, 5, 7, 13, 42, 99, 1234, 2026; daemon-side ceiling observed on the real path: each pool compiler recycles past about 1.2 GB (logged at 1,228-1,318 MB, KINO-DAEMON-MEMORY policy), so with 3 compilers plus 3 pwsh relays and erl the daemon tree is bounded near 4.3 GB; 2026-10-14 02:21 full math.spi on real Kino with --memo, personas fickle=2,editor=1,breaker=1, edits 1, breaking 1, concurrency 3: 22 posts, 80 events, about 92 deliveries, 21 prefixes, 2-3 cancels, schedule 1 291 s then schedule 2 5.2 s (memo hits 179 then 390 cumulative, misses 32), daemon tree peak 3,115 MB (compilers 788/803/1,087 MB, 3 recycles at 1,228-1,318 MB), 32.7 min wall including the sequential baselines; sequential equivalence, no stale results, one compile per prefix, withdrawn demand and commute all ok, but breaking_edits_fail flagged f0p4: an xsim oracle bug, not a Kino false pass: the breaking edit bumped the last digit run, turning 7.3890560989306495 into 7.3890560989306496 (the same f64, and inside _assert_approx_eq's tolerance), so the edit was not breaking; Scenario.break_source now bumps the whole literal's integer part (7.389... -> 8.389..., 9 -> 10), shared by --breaking and the breaker persona, with a test (27/27 on seeds 0, 1, 42); 2026-10-14 02:48 fix verified on real Kino: math.spi 5 cells with --breaking 1 (the edit now turns the f0p4 test's 7.3890560989306495 into 8.3890560989306495) on its own daemon: 5 prefixes, 53 s + 18 s, every invariant ok including breaking_edits_fail (the cell now fails honestly), commute ok, daemon tree 1,796 MB; xsim tests 27/27 on 69 seeds (0, 1, 5, 7, 13, 42, 99, 1234, 2026 and 100-159); 2026-10-14 03:00 the --edits 0 fix changes build.ps1's cold real-Kino race guard (math.spi 3 cells, users 2, edits 0, 1 schedule, concurrency 2) from 8 to 7 events; that exact scenario on a cold isolated daemon still passes with peak_inflight=2 (3 prefixes, 32.7 s, every invariant ok, daemon tree 1,226 MB, 47.8 s wall); churn-heavy persona stress over math+date_time+sm' (319 posts, 2,000+ events, 40-50% duplicates, churn 6-12, fickle 6-8, breaker 4-6) passes on seeds 1-16 at concurrency 1, 3 and 8; 2026-10-14 03:26 simulator-side memory ceiling: Runner evict: true / mix xsim.run --evict frees a prefix's result when its last demand goes (withdrawn like, edit, re-key), recomputes on renewed demand, counts evictions in one_compile_per_prefix (starts - cancels - evictions <= 1) and adds the invariant unwanted_results_freed (every held result belongs to a wanted post); summary gains peak_results and evictions; full lib corpus with personas, frames, edits, breaking edits and churn: results held 1,608 instead of about 5,700 (-72%), about 4,000 evictions per schedule, starts 5,657-5,689 vs 5,744-5,792 kept (no extra compiles at this scale), every invariant and commute ok; on math+date_time+sm' with churn 6: 316 held instead of 1,548-1,599 at +1.4-4% starts; tests 28/28 on seeds 0-29 (new: eviction commutes with keeping, never holds more results, evictions happen); the default stays off so earlier numbers compare; 2026-10-14 03:37 second notebook on real Kino: date_time.spi 20 cells with --memo, personas fickle=2,editor=1,breaker=1, edits 1, breaking 1, concurrency 3: 20 posts, 70 events, about 80 deliveries, 20 prefixes, schedule 1 94.5 s then 3.1 s (memo hits 131 then 306 cumulative, misses 44), every invariant incl. breaking_edits_fail and commute ok, daemon tree peak 2,181 MB (one compiler at 1,190 MB, 5 recycles in the daemon log), 11 min wall; my daemon port 13977 is free again; next: multi-notebook real-Kino corpora (math+date_time interleaved threads) and --evict against Kino's memo; 2026-10-14 03:39 eviction at phase-2 scale (lib corpus x6, same options as the 9,972-post run): results held 8,232 instead of about 22,700 (-64%), but 24,250-24,798 starts instead of 22,701-22,952 (+7-8% recomputes from renewed demand after churn and fickle toggles), 148-188 s per schedule instead of 89-93 s, peak BEAM 133 vs 147 MB; every invariant incl. unwanted_results_freed and commute ok; so dropping results on withdrawn demand trades about 8% more compiles for a bounded result store, which is the policy question for Kino's memo (keep, evict, or evict with a grace window); 2026-10-14 03:44 correction: the 4.3 GB daemon-tree bound above is an estimate from the recycle threshold (3 compilers at about 1.3 GB, relays, erl), not a measurement; the highest measured peak is 3,115 MB (full math.spi); 2026-10-14 04:09 first multi-notebook real-Kino run (math.spi + date_time.spi, 10 cells each, as two interleaved author threads via --spi-file; a comma list through mix.bat had silently kept only math): 20 posts, 91 events, about 105 deliveries, 16 prefixes, with --memo, --evict and personas fickle=2,editor=1,breaker=1, edits 1, breaking 1, concurrency 3: schedule 1 224 s then 21 s (memo hits 52 then 121 cumulative, misses 18-19), 0 evictions needed, every invariant incl. unwanted_results_freed and breaking_edits_fail ok, commute ok; daemon tree peak 3,398 MB (compilers 877/981/1,104 MB), the highest measured so far; 16.6 min wall; evict stress on 3 notebooks passes on seeds 20-35 at concurrency 1 and 6; xsim tests 28/28 on seeds 0-29 and 200-259; next: a daemon-memory sampler in xsim itself (today the ladder script reads the tree after the run), larger multi-notebook corpora on real Kino when the gate is quiet, and Kino's memo policy under eviction; 2026-10-14 04:14 final code checked as CI runs it: pwsh apps/xsim/build.ps1 -fast 1 (mix test, then the personas corpus run) exit 0 in 117 s: 1,662 posts, about 9,730 deliveries, every invariant and commute ok; 2026-10-14 04:26 review fixes: --breaking and the breaker persona now only pick test cells whose source Scenario.break_source actually changes (a tail like pair.1 was picked, left unchanged and then flagged as a false breaking_edits_fail violation), and the fickle persona returns no events on an empty thread instead of crashing; test added, 29/29 on seeds 0-19", Epic, P1, D7, 78u32, Active, "", "social,simulator,kino,stress", "current", "current")
