inl cross_backend_benchmark () = Task ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", "Performance modes and a cross-backend benchmark (in-session task since 2026-10-07, never a backlog item): the same programs timed on every backend that runs them (C as baseline; Rust, Zig, Wasm, Kotlin, Swift, Lean, Gleam, Lua, Bend, WGSL), optimization modes per backend, results as a table in a published page; feeds ZIG-HUB-SPEED and DEFAULT-BACKEND-DECISION", Feature, P2, D5, 40u32, Active, "", "backends,performance", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_cross_backend_benchmark () = Planning ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", Minutes 600u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_1 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 1u32, "2026-10-14T12:52Z", "main-thread", "08:52 design: reuse the harness's per-backend build and run logic, with no separate bench runner. A staged patch (job tmp/rotation/patches/run_ms.ps1, all 5 anchors dry-run clean, applied after the current bless) adds results.tsv run_ms, the last native process of a job: the program run for compiled backends, elaboration plus run for interpreters. The benchmark is then a quiet '-Filter' run over the megas, runtime_bench and cube with -Parallel 1, repeated 3 times, and a table of median run_ms per backend vs C, published as a page. A first rough cut (native_ms, mixed build+run, loaded) is on ZIG-HUB-SPEED.")
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_2 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 2u32, "2026-10-14T21:25Z", "main-thread", "17:25 run_ms is in results.tsv (installed today). First table from single-flight-20261014-164608: median run_ms vs C on the same rows: Zig 0.96 (145 rows, sum 11.1 s vs C 10.8 s), Rust 1.16 (409), Swift 1.54 (248), Wasm 2.66 (149), Lua 4.07 (137), Kotlin 9.4 (248, JVM start), Bend 28 (344), Lean 89 (137, lean --run elaboration). The quiet mega/cube/bench pass (-Parallel 1, 3 repeats) is the next step toward the published page.")
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_3 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 3u32, "2026-10-14T21:49Z", "main-thread", "17:49 method fixed: suite run_ms is too noisy under load, so time the built binaries directly (min of 7, Process.WaitForExit; PowerShell Start-Process -Wait adds a ~1 s floor). First clean table: min of 7 direct runs of the harness-built binaries (System.Diagnostics.Process, every exit correct; C gcc as built by the harness, Zig ReleaseFast, Rust release), ms C/Zig/Rust: brzozowski runtime_bench 333/87/346, indexed_dfa 78/30/79, inventory_dfa 14/13/27, runtime_native 13/11/14, lean_cic 11/11/11, omniledger 18/10/14, spiral_proves 16/13/19, zeta 15/13/22, cube 14/10/18. Next: extend to Swift/Lua/Kotlin/Wasm/Lean/Bend binaries or runners, then the published page.")
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_4 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 4u32, "2026-10-15T10:47Z", "main-thread", "06:47 groom: input added 10-15: compiled F# baseline (Release exe, min of 7): runtime_bench C 266 / Zig 63 / F# 319 ms, indexed_dfa 53 / 23 / 170 ms (job tmp/fsbase). Method settled (direct Process timing, not suite run_ms). Next grain: put the 9-program table into a script with the F# column.")
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_5 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 5u32, "2026-10-15T10:52Z", "main-thread", "06:52 groom caveat: the 10-15 F#/C/Zig baseline (tmp/fsbase) built Zig with scoop/apps/zig/current = stable 0.17.0, not the harness's zig-dev. The Zig-vs-C ratio matches the harness-built 10-14 numbers (runtime_bench 0.24x, indexed_dfa 0.43x), so the conclusion holds. A scripted rerun should pin the harness's zig-dev.")
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_6 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 6u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: new apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/bench-native.ps1 (uncommitted) times harness-built natives directly, min of N, exit checked against C; quiet run on 9 programs all match C.")
inl note_of_cross_backend_benchmark_7 () = Note ("CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK", 7u32, "2026-10-17T18:24Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:24 from the bundles (samples alpha705, Sep 2026): the fresh-pair protocol that finally gave honest A/B numbers: a unique graph identity for each side, session teardown before EVERY measured compile (two different graphs in one session degraded 4.5 s -> 12.7 s whichever went second), both orders, and a symmetric cache nonce on both sides (a predecessor SHA that hit the cache made a candidate look +4.7% while fresh-vs-fresh was -14%). Run compiler-family jobs serially (4 concurrent supervisors timed out) and parallelize inside them. Record workload cardinality (active_calls) and the runtime profile (one-shot FSI vs a persistent daemon differed enough to straddle a 10 s gate) in each receipt.")
inl zed_git_storm () = Task ("ZED-GIT-STORM", "Zed git integration floods the machine when the workspace is C:/home/git (2026-10-12: ~48 repos opened 6x each, up to ~90-102 concurrent git commands, 260+ processes, ~3.9 GB; git.disable_git only hides UI, git_store ignores it; Zed runs the scoop shim chain, 3 processes per call): options: open one repo, a Zed launcher with mingw64 git first on PATH, or a patch in the zed checkout that skips repository discovery when disable_git is set; decided by the user 2026-10-12 18:44: leave it; check git/conhost counts before blaming jobs", TaskKind, P3, D2, 100u32, Done, "", "zed,tooling", "current", "current")
