inl ci_compiler_full_suite () = Task ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", "Run the compiler's full suite (-Suite all -Native, both lanes as needed) in CI on every push (user decision 2026-10-12 19:09); today it takes ~35-70 min locally; needs the native toolchains on the runners (gcc, rustc, zig, lean, bun, gleam, swift, kotlin, wasmtime, spiral-wgsl with a software adapter or no-toolchain rows), the oracle EXPECTED.tsv available to CI without committing generated results to the tree, and a per-job time budget under GitHub's 6 h limit", TaskKind, P1, D5, 40u32, Active, "PIPELINES-GREEN", "ci,compiler,harness", "current", "current")
inl planning_of_ci_compiler_full_suite () = Planning ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", Minutes 480u32, Load Heavy, Assigned Agent)
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_1 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 1u32, "2026-10-14T11:39Z", "main-thread", "2026-10-14 07:39 touch: proposal from today's numbers: CI runs -Suite fast (600 s budget, 671 rows, all 130 flip histories) on every push and -Suite all -Native nightly (16 min quiet, 32-51 min loaded locally); the oracle stays cache-only, fetched as a CI artifact from the last bless")
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_2 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 2u32, "2026-10-14T21:50Z", "main-thread", "17:50 design (not yet in .github; it needs a CI dry run, so the YAML must not land untested and turn every push red). (1) Nightly job (schedule + workflow_dispatch): provision toolchains (scripts/init.ps1 at the repo root, install-dotnet.ps1, setup-rust, setup-zig, setup-beam with gleam, bun, python, swift/kotlin/lean where the runner has them, wgsl via the fallback adapter or no-toolchain rows), build.ps1 -Mode single-flight, test.ps1 -Suite all -Native -Bless in the CI cache. Upload <cache>/runs/<last> plus baseline/EXPECTED.tsv as artifact 'compiler-oracle'. The CI oracle is the previous nightly, separate from the local one, so nothing is committed. (2) Push job: download the latest 'compiler-oracle' artifact into the cache (it gives the baseline and the fast-tier history), build, test.ps1 -Suite fast -Native; fail on DISAGREE/REGRESSED. Without that history -Suite fast runs every row (rows missing from the last full run always run), so the artifact is what keeps push CI near 10 min. (3) Time: full ~16-50 min locally, about 2x on runners, under 6 h. Next: write .github/workflows/compiler.yml and do one workflow_dispatch dry run before enabling the push trigger.")
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_3 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 3u32, "2026-10-14T21:51Z", "main-thread", "17:51 .github/workflows/compiler.yml written (uncommitted): schedule 06:17 UTC + workflow_dispatch (input suite), ubuntu-24.04, setup-beam/bun/python, scripts/init.ps1, build.ps1 -Mode single-flight, test.ps1 -Suite all -Native -Parallel 2 -Bless into SPIRAL_BIN_CACHE_DIR beside the workspace, uploads 'compiler-oracle'. No push trigger: until a dispatch run passes it cannot turn push CI red. Untested (no local Actions runner); toolchains init.ps1 does not provide (zig, lean, swift, kotlin, wasmtime, wgsl adapter) will show as no-toolchain rows, which is acceptable for a first run.")
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_4 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 4u32, "2026-10-15T01:15Z", "main-thread", "21:15 NEXT SESSION #5, after the user commits: one workflow_dispatch run of compiler.yml, then the push -Suite fast job seeded from its artifact.")
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_5 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 5u32, "2026-10-15T09:49Z", "main-thread", "05:49 NEXT SESSION #4 (user): commit and push .github/workflows/compiler.yml, then run one workflow_dispatch; a push-triggered fast job comes after that run is green.")
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_6 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 6u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: the 10-16 scheduled compiler run failed twice over: workflow.ps1 -Compiler saw a .NET SDK in its check subprocess so install-dotnet.ps1 never ran, then build.ps1 threw No .NET 11 SDK found; and upload-artifact rejected the .. path. Fixed in the tree: workflow.ps1 resolves once, installs only when empty, exports SPIRAL_DOTNET and logs dotnet: path; the cache dir moved under RUNNER_TEMP via GITHUB_ENV. Needs one workflow_dispatch after the push.")
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_7 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 7u32, "2026-10-17T15:57Z", "main-thread", "11:57 main-thread design update from today's native memo: the per-push job can be the FULL suite, not -Suite fast. The nightly bless (it must run every native row) leaves <cache>/native-memo filled, and an actions/cache save keyed by runner OS + hashFiles of the native shims and test.ps1 can persist it. A push job restores it and runs test.ps1 -Suite all -Native -ReuseNative, so it pays only the compile phase (~4 min locally) plus native runs for rows whose output bytes changed (Zig-only change today: 457 rows, 91 s). Not wired yet: the nightly job has never run green since the 10-16 fixes (needs the user's push and one workflow_dispatch). Adding actions/cache also needs a 'gh actions-lock' regeneration of .github/workflows/actions.lock (machine-generated, CI-ACTIONS-LOCKFILE).")
inl note_of_ci_compiler_full_suite_8 () = Note ("CI-COMPILER-FULL-SUITE", 8u32, "2026-10-17T16:42Z", "main-thread", "12:42 main-thread: with -ReuseCompile in the harness, a push that touches neither the compiler nor any Spiral source replays the whole suite in about 1 min locally, if the CI cache restores <cache>/compile-memo and native-memo (same OS, same toolchains). That makes '-Suite all on every push' affordable: a docs- or CI-only push costs about 1 min, and a compiler change costs the compile phase plus the natives whose output changed.")
inl x_compat_check () = Task ("X-COMPAT-CHECK", "X compatibility check for edition-3 sources (user decision 2026-10-12 19:14, spiral3 prioritizes being X compatible): the compiler/Kino fails or warns on anything X rewrites when posted: '#' before a letter (hashtag), '@' before a name (mention), '$' before letters (cashtag; Spiral's \\$\"...\" is fine), autolink-looking text such as x.io / cell.ai in record access (shortened to 23-char t.co links), plus a per-post weight <= 280 using the twitter-text v3 weights already in apps/xsim/lib/xsim/frames.ex (CJK/Hangul count 2); the edition-3 symbol table itself is clean (no # @ \\$-letter tokens); 00:14 checker in (uncommitted): apps/xsim lib/xsim/x_compat.ex (Xsim.XCompat.check/2 -> hashtag/mention/cashtag/autolink findings with line+column and an over_limit finding per post whose X weight > 280; x_weight counts each autolink as 23; autolinks follow twitter-text: IANA TLD (apps/xsim/priv/tlds.txt, IANA 2026101200, 1286 entries without xn--), not after - _ . / @, 2-letter ccTLD only when the label before it is longer than 2, t.co special), mix xsim.x_compat FILE... (exit 1 on findings); test/x_compat_test.exs 7/7; note: record access like listm.map / x.data / s.run / a.name would be linked by X (map, data, run, name, new, one, zip are TLDs); 00:19 survey (rewrite findings): edition-3 corpus 834 files, 40 with findings: autolink 37 (record access: .map 16, .run 6, .id 5, .seven 3, .md 2, .next 2), cashtag 16 (\\/\\/\\ inside macro text), mention 1; lib/spiral 194 files, 42 with findings: autolink 259 (.map 153, .run 20, .to 20, .box 16, .date 9, .now 7, .store 5), hashtag 50, cashtag 24, mention 18 (@dim in am'.spi, @compileError in backend.spi Zig macros); also fixed Xsim.Frames.split: threads were chunked by plain weight, so every post was filled to 279 and any autolink pushed it past 280 once shortened; split now chunks X-weighted units (XCompat.segments keeps each autolink whole at weight 23), xsim tests 23/23; design question for the user: record access like listm.map is what X links most, so edition-3 X-compat needs either a different accessor spelling or posting through a code-block/image path; 02:44 splitter fixed twice more (apps/xsim/lib/xsim/frames.ex, uncommitted): the post budget assumed a 99/99 header, so threads of 100+ posts (the mega samples reach 2,492) overflowed by the extra header digits; the budget now uses the real post count (re-split when the digit count grows); and cuts move back to the last whitespace within 60 units, so a post never ends inside a word (cutting state.mapping to state.map minted a new autolink); xsim tests 24/24; survey now: over_limit 0 everywhere; edition-3 corpus 28 files with rewrite findings (autolink 37, cashtag 16, mention 1), lib 42 files (autolink 259, hashtag 50, cashtag 24, mention 18); remaining work: decide the edition-3 answer to record-access autolinks (.map/.run/.to/.box) and wire the check into Kino/compiler for edition-3 sources; user decision 2026-10-13 07:01: record access stays a.b in edition 3; the X-compat check only warns (no hard error, no TLD-key lint); posting tools wrap code (image/code block) instead; remaining: wire the warning into Kino/compiler for edition-3 sources; user decision 2026-10-13 07:14: surface X-compat warnings everywhere: Kino cell output (next to each edition-3 cell's result), compiler diagnostics (coded warnings editors underline), the posting tool (xsim/X path, right before posting) and a CI report (summary per repo, never failing the build); 2026-10-13 10:03 Kino lane: checker moved to apps/kino (Spiral.Kino.XCompat + priv/tlds.txt; weights too), xsim's Xsim.XCompat keeps check/over_limit and delegates the rest, Xsim.Frames.weight delegates (no cycle: xsim depends on kino); Kino prints x-compat warning lines when the nearest package.spiproj says edition: 3 or --x-compat is passed (never changes the result); CI report: mix spiral.x_compat <file|dir>... always exit 0; tests: kino x_compat_test 3/3, xsim x_compat_test 9/9; README; 2026-10-13 12:25 Kino surfaces done (warnings for edition: 3 packages, mix spiral.x_compat CI report); 2026-10-13 22:59 the spiral-split gear splitter (scripts/gear-dev.ps1, bench-split.ps1, build-splitter.ps1, tmp/splitter, lanes/splitter) was removed at the user's request: idle since early October (234 runs Sep 26-Oct 1, then 21), built for gpt5parallel's 159k lines; use build.ps1 -Configuration Debug -CoreSource <copy> for candidates; 2026-10-13 22:59 correction: the previous note about removing the spiral-split gear splitter does not apply to this item; here 'splitter' means xsim's X-thread splitter (apps/xsim/lib/xsim/frames.ex), which is unchanged", TaskKind, P1, D4, 85u32, Active, "SPIRAL3-EDITIONS", "edition,x,lint", "current", "current")
