inl note_of_near_windows_native_1 () = Note ("NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE", 1u32, "2026-10-16T02:24Z", "main-thread", "22:24 main: no WSL3 exists as far as known; WSL2's memory is bounded only by .wslconfig (memory=, autoMemoryReclaim). Decision kept: no WSL on the dev machine, NEAR and other Linux-only parts run on CI Linux runners.")
inl note_of_near_windows_native_2 () = Note ("NEAR-WINDOWS-NATIVE", 2u32, "2026-10-16T02:56Z", "main-thread", "22:56 main: correction of my previous note: WSL 3.0.3.0 exists and is installed (kernel 6.18.54, user updated it 10-15). Benchmark (job tmp/wslbench/bench.ps1, Ubuntu): cold boot 12.7 s, warm call 0.9 s, idle VM ~2.0-2.3 GB of host memory, a 2 GB allocation inside raised the VM to 4.3 GB and it returned to 2.3 GB within 20 s (reclaim works, unlike WSL2), ext4 write 643 MB/s vs /mnt/c 74 MB/s, listing 9,006 files over /mnt/c 5.9 s, shutdown releases everything. Verdict: NEAR tests can come back as bounded WSL jobs: clone into ext4, run through Invoke-Heavy -Wsl, wsl --shutdown afterwards, never during a Tibia live block. Risk: .wslconfig memory=12GB on a 16 GB machine lets one cargo build take the host down; recommended memory=6GB (user's file, not edited). NixOS and nixos2 distros are installed too (CI-NIX can be tried locally in WSL).")
inl sefaz_xsd_app () = Task ("SEFAZ-XSD-APP", "New app (user 2026-10-15 21:50): a Spiral generator from all SEFAZ XSDs, Tibia style with test blocks and X-compatible from day one, Delphi backend, ACBr and Zeus as the benchmarks the way Tibia uses open-source bots, generating GADTs/existentials/HKTs/prototypes able to carry ERP engines; open choice: generate at build time in Rust (build.rs + OUT_DIR caching, crate reuse) or in Spiral itself, since Zig comptime would re-parse the XSDs on every build", Epic, P2, D8, 0u32, Planned, "", "app,sefaz,delphi,spiral", "current", "current")
inl note_of_sefaz_xsd_app_1 () = Note ("SEFAZ-XSD-APP", 1u32, "2026-10-17T14:22Z", "main-thread", "10:22 main-thread fork: decision input (user question 2026-10-17: keep advancing Tibia for dogfood, or start the XSD tasks?). Measured from the backlog: the Tibia lane surfaced ~20 tooling items on 2026-10-12..14 (compiler GADT-ONCE fixes, C self-tail/incref loops, WGSL tail-call/union/dispatch bugs, Kino memo scope and Rust/C flag parity, hopac Debug crash, private-cache leak, macro-argument limits, eoie note author), 2 tooling notes on 10-14, and 0 since: 10-15 had 68 tibia-lane notes all on TIBIA items (potion fidelity, hunting ground, retreat, policy), 10-16/17 none. Remaining TIBIA work is game fidelity and policy, i.e. business logic; its compiled modules stay valuable as a regression corpus (already in the pre-pack corpus check). SEFAZ-XSD would exercise surfaces Tibia never touched: generating thousands of types from XSDs (compile-time scale, a real load for hopacsota's parallel evaluation), reflection/prototype codegen (the CONVO-IN-SPIRAL schema machinery, both directions), XML parse/serialize, the Delphi backend (least used), X-compat from day one. Recommendation: make SEFAZ-XSD-APP the next dogfood once reprioritized; Tibia stays maintenance + corpus. Open choice in the title (Rust build.rs vs Spiral-side generation) is the first experiment, in spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app/ per LAB-IN-REPO.")
inl note_of_sefaz_xsd_app_2 () = Note ("SEFAZ-XSD-APP", 2u32, "2026-10-17T14:46Z", "main-thread", "10:46 main-thread fork: XSD census (research only, not a start; spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app/census.py, input = sparse XSD-only clone of the Zeus benchmark ZeusAutomacao/DFe.NET, kept outside the repo as data): 580 .xsd (500 distinct contents, 5.1 MB, 1 parse error; NF-e/NFC-e, CT-e and its modals, MDF-e, events, DF-e distribution, several versions each), 12,076 elements, 2,238 complexTypes (572 named, 151 distinct names; the rest anonymous inline), 5,389 simpleTypes (1,445 named, 179 distinct names; TCOrgaoIBGE x35, TVerEvento x33 across versions), 11,360 enumeration facets, 2,055 regex patterns, 1,875 maxLength / 1,759 minLength, 393 choices, 819 attributes, 590 includes + 83 imports, max nesting depth 23. Mapping for the generator: enumerations -> unions (11k cases), choices -> unions, minOccurs/maxOccurs -> option/array, length/pattern facets -> validated nominal types whose regexes compile through the brzozowski derivatives work (lib dogfood), shared tiposBasico -> one module per (family, version) with content-hash dedupe (80 duplicate files). Build.rs-vs-Spiral question: parsing 5 MB is ~1 s, so the real cost is compiling the generated Spiral (12k elements of types and validators) - first experiment: generate nfe v4.00 + its tiposBasico only, measure single-flight and hopacsota compile time, which doubles as a large hopacsota load test.")
inl note_of_sefaz_xsd_app_3 () = Note ("SEFAZ-XSD-APP", 3u32, "2026-10-17T14:54Z", "main-thread", "10:54 main-thread fork: enumeration mapping measured (research, not a start; scripts spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app/enum_gen.py and enums_gen.py, generated .spi written to the git-ignored lab/.runs/sefaz-generated/, XSD data = the DFe.NET sparse clone): TCodUfIBGE (tiposBasico_v1.03, 27 values) -> union + parse (option) + code_of: 94 lines, compiles and round-trips '35' (exit 27), 3 s with gcc. All 58 distinct named enumerated simpleTypes of the corpus = 2,179 cases -> 4,653 lines in one module: compiles in 5 s total (only one parse reachable from main, so this is front end + type check cost, peval of the rest is lazy), 1.25 MB of C for that one used parser chain. Most of the 11,360 enumeration facets are anonymous inline simpleTypes inside the leiaute files, so the generator must name them by element path. Next for the decision: generate the full NF-e v4.00 type set (named + inline) and compile it with every type reachable, single-flight vs hopacsota.")
inl note_of_sefaz_xsd_app_4 () = Note ("SEFAZ-XSD-APP", 4u32, "2026-10-17T15:11Z", "main-thread", "11:11 main-thread fork: inline enumerations measured (research; spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app/inline_enums.py): 1,405 enumerated simpleTypes (313 named, 1,092 inline: leiauteNFe 325, cteTiposBasico 186, prod 81, ...) collapse to only 266 DISTINCT value sets holding 3,312 values (vs 11,360 enumeration facets): dedupe by value set is a ~3.4x cut. Shape: 473 single-value enums (fixed constants such as versao 1.00 -> a literal/singleton type, not a union), 306 two-value (mostly 0/1 or 1/2 flags), 130 three, 132 four; the largest has 792 values. So the generator emits ~266 shared enumeration unions plus typed singleton constants, keyed by value set and referenced by element path; that is far below the 4,653-line / 2,179-case module that already compiles in 5 s. Still to measure for the decision: complexTypes (2,238, mostly anonymous) and the 2,055 patterns.")
inl note_of_sefaz_xsd_app_5 () = Note ("SEFAZ-XSD-APP", 5u32, "2026-10-17T15:12Z", "main-thread", "11:12 main-thread fork: patterns measured (research; spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app/patterns.py): 2,055 pattern facets are only 196 DISTINCT regexes (median length 20, max 650: the NFC-e QR-code URL); features over the distinct ones: char classes 182, bounded repeats {n,m} 166, alternation 98, groups 64, optional 51, star/plus 16, \\\\d\\\\s\\\\w 12, XSD class subtraction -[ 4, negated class 1, \\\\p properties 0; most reused [0-9]{8} x73, [0-9]{1,4} x67, [0-9]{6} x67, [0-9]{1,15} x64. Reading: a small regex dialect (classes, bounded repeats, alternation, groups) compiled once per distinct pattern into DFAs - exactly what the brzozowski-derivatives work does; only XSD class subtraction needs a dedicated rule. Decision inputs now complete on enumerations (266 value sets) and patterns (196); complexTypes are the remaining unmeasured part.")
inl note_of_sefaz_xsd_app_6 () = Note ("SEFAZ-XSD-APP", 6u32, "2026-10-17T15:12Z", "main-thread", "11:12 main-thread fork: complexTypes measured (research; spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app/complex_types.py, structure = element names/types/occurs and sequence/choice nesting): 2,238 complexTypes are 826 distinct structures (2.7x dedupe); 539 contain a choice (-> unions), 719 carry attributes; most are small records (1-10 descendant elements for ~1,650 of them) with a long tail (84 with 40+ descendants: the NF-e/CT-e document roots). Generator size estimate after dedupe: ~826 records/unions + ~266 enumeration unions + ~196 compiled patterns + typed singleton constants, organized per (family, version) with shared basics. All research scripts (census, enum_gen, enums_gen, inline_enums, patterns, complex_types) live in spiral/lab/sefaz-xsd-app and read a DFe.NET sparse XSD clone (git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --no-checkout https://github.com/ZeusAutomacao/DFe.NET.git; git sparse-checkout set --no-cone '*.xsd'). Still a research item: start only on an explicit go.")
inl note_of_sefaz_xsd_app_7 () = Note ("SEFAZ-XSD-APP", 7u32, "2026-10-17T22:05Z", "main-thread", "18:05 main-thread lab cleanup: the six measurement .py scripts deleted; every number they produced (1,405 enums -> 266 value sets, 2,055 patterns -> 196 regexes, 2,238 complexTypes -> 826 structures) and the DFe.NET sparse clone command are in notes 1-6. The generator itself is to be written in Spiral on an explicit go.")
