inl note_of_samples_stale_outputs_1 () = Note ("SAMPLES-STALE-OUTPUTS", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: found while counting Rust tag impls: the 4 main.rs files date from 10-07 and their samples have no Rust row in harness.psd1, so nothing regenerates them.")
inl date_time_portable () = Task ("DATE-TIME-PORTABLE", "Finish portable date_time guids: the timestamp pair is portable since 2026-10-17; date_time_guid_from_date_time and date_time_from_guid still use backend chains; the drafted civil_of_days and guid_prefix_of_micros (6 backends pass) replace them once ticks vs micros for the 7th fraction digit is decided", TaskKind, P2, D2, 50u32, Active, "", "lib,portable,zig", "current", "current")
inl note_of_date_time_portable_1 () = Note ("DATE-TIME-PORTABLE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T10:02Z", "main-thread", "06:02 main-thread night: timestamp_guid_from_timestamp and timestamp_from_guid are portable in the tree (tests on 6 backends). Drafts for the rest in job tmp/night/civil/civil.spi (civil_of_days, guid_prefix_of_micros; 36 + 30 asserts on fsharp/rust/zig/python/gleam/lua). Decision needed: keep 100 ns ticks (F# today) or micros everywhere (then date_time_guid_from_date_time_3 expects ...9999-0).")
inl harness_run_only_useful () = Task ("HARNESS-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", "Run only what can change (user 2026-10-17: benchmark everything, run only what is useful): (1) every heavy/timed run in the ledger %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\ledger\\runs.tsv + cost-report.ps1 (ledger, harness runs, per backend, signal over full runs, Kino per-target ms); (2) harness native memo: test.ps1 -ReuseNative replays native outcomes keyed by output bytes + toolchain/harness signature (refused for -Bless/-Record); (3) deterministic compiler builds across cache folders (PathMap + content-addressed core staging in build.ps1) so a candidate DLL can be compared to the installed one by hash and Kino's memo survives a rebuild elsewhere; (4) Kino warm Gleam packages.", Story, P1, D4, 90u32, Active, "", "harness,kino,cost", "current", "current")
inl note_of_harness_run_only_useful_1 () = Note ("HARNESS-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 1u32, "2026-10-17T12:45Z", "main-thread", "08:45 main-thread: native memo proven. Full -ReuseNative run on the blessed binaries replayed 5,892 rows (59.9 summed native minutes) with 0 to run: native tier 9.3 s vs 1,298 s, whole run about 3.5 min vs 25, and 0 of 6,906 rows differ from the bless single-flight-20261017-081502. Invalidation: changing SPIRAL_CXX (part of the signature) replays 0 of 15 rows, against 15 of 15 unchanged. Rule: review and iteration runs use -ReuseNative; a bless always runs every native row (refused otherwise), and the bless must reproduce the review.")
inl note_of_harness_run_only_useful_2 () = Note ("HARNESS-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 2u32, "2026-10-17T13:28Z", "main-thread", "09:28 main-thread: compiler builds are byte-reproducible across cache folders. Isolated det-f and the shared cache both give SpiralCompiler.dll 53878A40C43B7A94 and SpiralCompilerCore.dll CAC5AC4544446A7D on SDK rc.2. Four fixes got there: build.ps1 passes -p:PathMap=<cache>=<shared> for the pdb path; the core is staged content-addressed under <shared>/core-src/<mode>/<sha16>/; Directory.Build.props sets TargetFrameworkMonikerAssemblyAttributesPath to <shared>/obj-shared/<project>/ (F#'s deflated signature data embedded the obj path); and env.ps1 resolves the shared SDK for private caches (it silently fell back to C:\\Program Files\\dotnet rc.1). With this, a candidate DLL can be proven equal to the installed one by sha256, and Kino's content-keyed memo survives rebuilds elsewhere. SDK moved to 11.0.100-rc.2.26504.105. The -ReuseNative review single-flight-20261017-092327 equals the bless on all 6,906 rows in about 4 min.")
inl note_of_harness_run_only_useful_3 () = Note ("HARNESS-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 3u32, "2026-10-17T14:29Z", "main-thread", "10:29 main-thread: tooling moved out of job tmp into spiral/lab/harness-run-only-useful/: the heavy gate with the run ledger (heavy.ps1; the job-tmp path is now a forwarder, the queue lives in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\heavy-queue, comments stripped, internal names made distinctive), compare-runs, rebuild-determinism, cross-folder-determinism, bindiff.py, fsharp-resources (inflates F#'s signature data to find embedded paths), kino-tests and kino-suite (now with --slowest). Known entries in tests/harness.psd1 can be scoped by Oracle. Under -Oracle Zig the whole suite has DISAGREE 0, with Zig agreeing on 5,354 rows against 5,383 under C; the only divergent sample is dynamic_array_bounds_negative (checked vs unchecked index). The memo signature now hashes only test.ps1's native code, found through its AST.")
inl note_of_harness_run_only_useful_4 () = Note ("HARNESS-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 4u32, "2026-10-17T16:28Z", "main-thread", "12:28 main-thread: Kino runs now benchmark themselves, whoever calls them. apps/kino/spi/run_notebook.ps1 appends a 'notebook' row (label: notebook file and warm/mix route, wall seconds, outcome) and run_suite.ps1 a 'kino-suite' row to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\ledger\\runs.tsv, both through scripts/heavy.ps1's Write-RunLedger; run_suite now also passes --slowest 25. Found while testing: a notebook path with an apostrophe (lib/spiral/sm'.spi) broke the command scriptblock, and the swallowed error dropped the row, so the text is now an escaped string literal and failures print a warning. Harness runs keep per-row times in runs/*/results.tsv and builds in results/builds.tsv. cost-report.ps1 covers ledger, harness and Kino per-target times.")
inl note_of_harness_run_only_useful_5 () = Note ("HARNESS-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 5u32, "2026-10-17T16:42Z", "main-thread", "12:42 main-thread: compile memo landed (test.ps1 -ReuseCompile), the compile-side twin of -ReuseNative. Key: sha256 of every compiler DLL (sound now that builds are reproducible), every .spi/.spir/.spiproj under the bundle, lib/ and the core package dir (271 samples reach packages through packageDir ../..), SPIRAL_* settings, mode, and the job's backend, input, output and timeout. ok/error compiles are stored with their output bytes. A replayed ok row restores the output when it differs and is tagged compile-reused; timeouts, crashes and emitted rows are never stored; -Bless/-Record refuse the flag. Proof: on the same compiler, run 1 (fill) compiled in 226 s and run 2 replayed all 6,915 compiles in 2.3 s. The whole -Suite all -Native run took 73 s, and both equal single-flight-20261017-121042 (0 cells). A SPIRAL_IF_NESTING_LIMIT change replays 0 of 1. Measured costs today: a full cold run with natives is about 30 min; a compiler-change review with -ReuseNative about 5 min; an unchanged-compiler rerun about 1 min. cost-report counts compile-reused rows apart.")
inl note_of_harness_run_only_useful_6 () = Note ("HARNESS-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 6u32, "2026-10-17T17:44Z", "main-thread", "13:44 main-thread: SDK incident and fix. After the rc.2 SDK move, the rebuilt compiler's runtimeconfig required the 11.0.0-rc.2 runtime, and eoie's regeneration (which runs the compiler through C:\\Program Files\\dotnet, rc.1 only) failed with 'You must install or update .NET'. A tree global.json pin vanished within minutes (cause unknown), so build.ps1 now chooses the newest installed SDK whose bundled runtime (sdk/<v>/Microsoft.NETCoreSdk.BundledVersions.props) the system dotnet also has, writes the global.json into <cache>/build-cwd/<sdk>/ and builds from there, with crossgen2 targeting the same runtime. It moves to rc.2 by itself once the system has it. MSBuild did not regenerate runtimeconfig.json on an SDK change (it isn't a target input), so the stale bin/*.runtimeconfig.json and obj/*.genruntimeconfig.cache had to be deleted once. Rebuilt Debug plus 3 modes; single-flight-20261017-133144 and hopacsota-20261017-133648 equal the grain 2a review on all 6,915 rows.")
inl note_of_harness_run_only_useful_7 () = Note ("HARNESS-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 7u32, "2026-10-17T18:00Z", "main-thread", "14:00 main-thread: fast-tier mining is incremental. -Suite fast used to re-mine every run history before its first compile (995 single-flight runs: 54 s). The fold state is now cached in <cache>/fast-tier-fold/<mode>-<policy>.json (2.6 MB) with a stamp per run (name, results.tsv size and mtime, fast-tier.tsv marker), and only new runs are mined; any changed earlier stamp means a full recompute. Proofs: the selection list (-ListPath) is byte-identical to the uncached baseline both on build (29 s) and on resume (10 s), and after appending a run the resumed fold (10 s) equals a full recompute (31 s). The remaining ~10 s is pwsh startup, statting 1,096 run folders and JSON load: the PWSH-FREE native-verb target. Also today: lib/spiral/crypto.spi lost get_file_hash' (it shelled out to pwsh for a sha256 that the native get_file_hash already computes); crypto.spi now has 4 of 4 tests passing.")
inl note_of_harness_run_only_useful_8 () = Note ("HARNESS-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 8u32, "2026-10-17T18:24Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:24 from the bundles (spiral-samples alpha705 + brzozowski alpha54, Jun-Sep 2026; BUNDLE-WISDOM note 6): a rule worth adding to the memo: if the generated residual hash equals the control's, a wall-time delta is lifecycle noise, not a regression. The exact-graph cache once returned a false fresh hit after a source edit until the key bound normalized path + source SHA (schema 3); check that the native memo signature covers every input the same way (it already covers SPIRAL_CXX). Cache hits are identity evidence only: performance claims need fresh runs with session teardown before each measured compile.")
inl note_of_harness_run_only_useful_9 () = Note ("HARNESS-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL", 9u32, "2026-10-17T22:05Z", "main-thread", "18:05 main-thread lab cleanup: rebuild-determinism promoted to apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/rebuild-determinism.ps1 (now touches the content-addressed staged core so the rebuild is real; log in <cache>/runs); the one-off proofs (compile-memo-proof, cross-folder-determinism, fsharp-resources, bindiff.py, kino-tests) deleted, their results are in this item's notes and AGENTS.md; lab/harness-run-only-useful removed. The bounded runner lab/try.ps1 moved to apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/try.ps1 (runs in <cache>/runs/try).")
inl hopacsota_hopac_depth () = Task ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", "hopacsota uses Hopac's real API, not gpt5parallel's subset (user 2026-10-17). Measured: gpt5parallel's side of spiral_compiler.fs calls about 20 entry points (IVar.fill 165, Job.bind 70, Promise.Now 50, Ch.send 41, start 28; withNack/Latch/MVar/Proc/Scheduler 0) of Hopac 0.5.1's 424 documented members. Adopt where PLAN D1-D16 and gate G2 allow (choice stays left-biased and deterministic: no Alt.random/chooser/<~>): Scheduler.create per build + Scheduler.kill at build end (the fix-57 cross-build leak class becomes impossible); Alt.withNackJob/wrapAbortJob for D16.2 speculation losers; Latch (holding/within) for fork-join of a body's children instead of IVar counting; Job.conCollect / <*> for independent argument evaluation; MVar.modifyFun or Job.foreverServer/iterateServer for owner state instead of locks; Stream.mapPipelinedJob for bounded codegen-per-method pipelining (D16.4); Job.isolate/switchToWorker/onThreadPool for blocking file IO off workers; Job.tryFinallyJob/useIn for scoped cleanup; Scheduler startWithActions top-level handler feeding the typed Diagnostic (D13); Alt.paranoid in Debug builds to catch double commits; Promise.queue/Hopac.memo for demand-driven keys. Each adoption is a measured A/B against single-flight with the hopacsota gates.", Story, P1, D5, 30u32, Paused, "", "compiler,hopacsota,hopac", "current", "current")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_1 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 1u32, "2026-10-17T13:14Z", "main-thread", "09:14 main-thread fork: first measurement (fsi --optimize+, Hopac 0.5.1, 64 children x 2000-iteration work, 2000 rounds, machine loaded by parallel compiler builds; script job-tmp study/hopac-bench/bench.fsx): fork-join via IVar + Interlocked counting (gpt5parallel's pattern) 540 us, Latch 272 us, Job.conCollect 275 us, Promise.start+read 386 us -> Latch/conCollect ~2x. Scheduler.create (NumWorkers = cores, MaxStackSize 1.5 GB) + one fork-join + Scheduler.kill = 2.2 ms per build, so a scheduler per build (kills the fix-57 leak class) is free next to any compile. Alt.withNackJob as speculation loser: 71 us per race, nack delivered 2000/2000 (the loser must be the left alternative or it is never instantiated). Job.Scheduler.isolate vs thunk: inconclusive (warm-up artifact). Next: repeat on an idle machine, then the same A/B inside the hopacsota engine (P2).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_2 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 2u32, "2026-10-17T13:36Z", "main-thread", "09:36 main-thread fork: D3 fill-once key table, cost per lookup+tryFill (job-tmp study/hopac-bench/table.fsx; 8 concurrent jobs x 20,000 lookups over 4,096 keys, loaded machine): ConcurrentDictionary.GetOrAdd of an IVar 447 ns, MVar.modifyFun over a Dictionary 931 ns, an owner server (Job.foreverServer + request/reply IVar) 1,221 ns. The Hopac-native forms add 0.5-0.8 us per lookup, negligible next to a join-point specialization (ms) unless a build does >100k table lookups. Decision input for P2: keep the table inside the engine module (G2 allowlists one structure there) and pick by measured lookups per build; the evaluator only sees fill/read either way (D15).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_3 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 3u32, "2026-10-17T13:54Z", "main-thread", "09:54 main-thread fork: D16.4 codegen-per-method shape (job-tmp study/hopac-bench/pipeline.fsx; 400 methods of uneven cost, 8 cores, loaded): sequential 87.2 ms, Job.conCollect 34.5 ms (2.5x) with results in the original order (conCollect returns by position, so D6's deterministic merge order comes for free); Stream.ofSeq |> Stream.mapPipelinedJob d |> Stream.foldFun did not finish within 240 s (killed) for d=2 in Hopac 0.5.1 - treat mapPipelinedJob as suspect until a minimal repro shows whether it is this usage or the library. For bounded parallelism use conCollect over fixed-size chunks or a Latch-limited pool instead.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_4 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 4u32, "2026-10-17T13:57Z", "main-thread", "09:57 main-thread fork: mapPipelinedJob minimal probe (job-tmp study/hopac-bench/pipelined-min.fsx, each probe raced against a 5 s timeout): mapPipelinedJob 2/1 and mapPipelinedFun 2 over 10 items all return 55 correctly (0-47 ms), so the library function works; the earlier non-finishing run is specific to that benchmark's shape (400 CPU-heavy Job.thunk bodies, list-consing fold) and stays unexplained - repeat it with a timeout race before ruling on mapPipelinedJob for codegen.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_5 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 5u32, "2026-10-17T14:10Z", "main-thread", "10:10 main-thread fork: HANDOFF (fork lane closed, resume in main lane after reprioritization): every job-tmp study/hopac-bench path in earlier notes is now %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\study\\2026-10-17\\hopac-bench (bench.fsx fork-join/scheduler/nack, table.fsx key table, pipeline.fsx + pipelined-min.fsx). Proven: Latch/conCollect 2x over IVar counting; Scheduler per build 2.2 ms; withNackJob reliable (loser left); D3 table ConcurrentDictionary 447 ns vs MVar 931 vs server 1,221; conCollect keeps order. Open: rerun all on an idle machine; pipeline.fsx mapPipelinedJob non-finish (raced 240 s) vs minimal OK; then A/B inside the hopacsota engine at P2. Run with: dotnet fsi --optimize+ <file> (refs Hopac 0.5.1 from ~/.nuget).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_6 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 6u32, "2026-10-17T14:17Z", "main-thread", "10:17 main-thread fork: LOCATION (supersedes the job-tmp and spiral-bin\\study paths in earlier notes): spiral/lab/hopacsota-hopac-depth/ (fork-join.fsx, key-table.fsx, codegen-pipeline.fsx, pipelined-min.fsx; each starts with #r nuget: Hopac, 0.5.1 so any machine restores it; run dotnet fsi --optimize+ <file>). CORRECTION: rerunning pipelined-min.fsx from the repo, mapPipelinedJob 2 over 10 items timed out (None after 5 s) while degree 1 and mapPipelinedFun 2 returned 55; the earlier run of the same file returned 55 - an intermittent hang at degree >= 2 in Hopac 0.5.1, not this benchmark's shape. Do not use mapPipelinedJob for codegen; conCollect is the order-preserving form.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_7 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 7u32, "2026-10-17T14:38Z", "main-thread", "10:38 main-thread fork: mapPipelined hang RATE (spiral/lab/hopacsota-hopac-depth/pipelined-rate.fsx, 30 trials each, 10 items, 2 s race): mapPipelinedJob degree 1 finished 30/30, degree 2 18/30, 4 17/30, 8 22/30; mapPipelinedFun 2 18/30; mapJob control 30/30. So Hopac 0.5.1's pipelined stream maps lose ~25-45% of runs at any degree > 1 (the stream never closes), independent of the mapper. Rule for hopacsota: no Stream.mapPipelined*; bounded parallel map = Job.conCollect over chunks or a Latch-limited pool; if the D14 HUD uses Stream.Src taps, verify them under the same race harness first. A minimal repro for the library is this file.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_8 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 8u32, "2026-10-17T14:58Z", "main-thread", "10:58 main-thread fork: ordered bounded maps measured as the mapPipelined replacement (spiral/lab/hopacsota-hopac-depth/ordered-pool.fsx, 400 uneven methods, 30 raced trials each, machine loaded by a suite): unbounded Job.conCollect 39.6 ms; chunked conCollect d=2/4/8 100/107/74 ms; token pool (Ch of d tokens, Promise per item, read in order) 113/134/135 ms; ALL correct, finished 30/30 and in sequential order. Reading: bounding costs 2-3x here (chunk barriers wait for their slowest member; the token pool pays a channel round trip per item), so for D16.4 use unbounded conCollect and bound only when memory per item demands it (then chunking, sized so chunks are long relative to the slowest item). The Hopac primitives that hang are only the Stream.mapPipelined family.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_9 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 9u32, "2026-10-17T15:11Z", "main-thread", "11:11 main-thread fork: HANDOFF 2 (lane paused until main-lane reprioritization): lab/hopacsota-hopac-depth now holds fork-join, key-table, codegen-pipeline, pipelined-min, pipelined-rate, ordered-pool (.fsx, #r nuget Hopac 0.5.1). Settled: Latch/conCollect over IVar counting (2x); Scheduler per build (2.2 ms); withNackJob for speculation; Stream.mapPipelined* hang 25-45% at degree > 1 - banned; unbounded conCollect is the ordered parallel map (bounded forms cost 2-3x). Open: idle-machine reruns; A/B inside the hopacsota engine at P2.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_hopac_depth_10 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-HOPAC-DEPTH", 10u32, "2026-10-17T22:05Z", "main-thread", "18:05 main-thread lab cleanup: the six Hopac probes (.fsx, nuget Hopac 0.5.1, portable) moved to apps/compiler/tmp/lanes/hopacsota/hopac-probes/ next to PLAN.md, which cites their numbers and asks for an idle-machine rerun before Gate 3.")
inl hopacsota_lf_scheduling () = Task ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", "Lingua Franca ideas for hopacsota, where they do not conflict with Hopac (user 2026-10-17, like the Bend study). LF (lf-lang/lingua-franca core/.../generator/ReactionInstanceGraph.java, graph/PrecedenceGraph.java; lf-lang/reactor-c core/threaded/scheduler_*.c) gets deterministic multicore execution from a static precedence graph: reactions get levels (longest upstream path), getBreadth = max reactions per level bounds useful workers, causality cycles are found by SCC and reported with the cycle, deadlines propagate upstream to give an EDF priority, chains of single-dominated reactions run inline without sync, and the adaptive scheduler measures per-level cost each tag to choose the worker count for the next. For hopacsota: (1) level/breadth of the D3 key graph as the frontier report and the worker-count cap (P2/P3); (2) critical-path-first worklist order (longest remaining chain), still merged in single-flight order by D6 so output is unchanged; (3) inline-run single-dominated chains below the P3 granularity cutoff; (4) a cycle report for a key graph deadlock in rustc style (like EJP0040), from the D4 wait-for graph; (5) measured per-level cost feeding the cutoff; (6) LF tracepoints with Chrome-trace export as the D13/P4 trace format.", Story, P1, D5, 35u32, Paused, "", "compiler,hopacsota,prior-art", "current", "current")
inl note_of_hopacsota_lf_scheduling_1 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", 1u32, "2026-10-17T13:15Z", "main-thread", "09:15 main-thread fork: first LF-style measurement on real graphs (job-tmp study/lf-levels/levels.py: method call graph of committed C residuals, runtime helpers excluded, Tarjan SCC then longest-path levels on the condensation; the output method graph is a proxy for the evaluation key graph, since each method is one join-point specialization). mega_brzozowski_derivatives: 15-39 methods, 7-9 levels, breadth 4-17, average parallelism (SCCs/levels) 1.7-5.0, 9-17 leaf methods, 11-22 self-recursive, 0 multi-node cycles; rust_static_closure_chain 81 independent closures in 1 level (breadth 81); mega_omniledger_erp_kernel 0 methods: all 15,757 lines are inlined into main, so method-level forking finds nothing there and only D16.2 speculation / inline-body splitting can shorten it. So LF's breadth bound says ~2-5 workers per level are useful on the brzozowski family, more on closure-heavy code. Next: the same metrics on hopacsota's own key graph (dfs/bfs worklist trace) instead of the output proxy.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_lf_scheduling_2 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", 2u32, "2026-10-17T13:36Z", "main-thread", "09:36 main-thread fork: corpus distribution (job-tmp study/lf-levels/corpus.py over every samples/**/main.c; 214 residuals with >= 2 methods): median 2 methods, 2 levels, breadth 2, average parallelism 2.0; buckets <=1: 64, 1-2: 99, 2-4: 41, >4: 10; deepest are the mega_brzozowski family (7-9 levels); widest rust_static_closure_chain (81 independent closures, 1 level) and the dynamic_array_* samples (7-13 wide); 5 residuals have multi-method cycles (largest 3 methods: mutual recursion). Reading: method-level forking alone gives ~2x on a typical sample and up to 4-5x on the mega family, so the hopacsota win on large programs has to come from D16.1 typed bodies at finer grain (closures, type join points) and D16.4 parse/typecheck/codegen pipelining - consistent with PLAN D16; a breadth-based worker cap (LF getBreadth) would keep most samples at 2 workers and avoid oversubscription. Caveat: residual methods are post-inlining, so the evaluation-time key graph is wider than this proxy.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_lf_scheduling_3 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", 3u32, "2026-10-17T13:55Z", "main-thread", "09:55 main-thread fork: weighted critical path (job-tmp study/lf-levels/critical.py; work = body lines of each residual method, SCC-condensed call DAG, bound = total work / heaviest path = the most any scheduler can gain at method grain): mega_brzozowski runtime_bench 4,006 lines over 39 methods, critical 825 -> bound 4.86; inventory_closed_dfa_runtime 4.5; indexed_dfa_state_cardinality 4.28; runtime_native 3.58; the native_cube_* frames ~2.8; across 103 residuals with >= 3 methods the median bound is 1.74 (max 22.7, the closure chain). So critical-path-first ordering matters exactly on the mega family: with 8 workers and a ~4.5x bound, a scheduler that starts the 825-line chain first finishes near the bound, while one that starts leaves first can lose most of it. This is the number to beat on hopacsota's real key graph (next: the same metric from a dfs worklist trace with measured per-key time instead of residual lines).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_lf_scheduling_4 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", 4u32, "2026-10-17T14:10Z", "main-thread", "10:10 main-thread fork: HANDOFF (fork lane closed): job-tmp study/lf-levels is now %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\study\\2026-10-17\\lf-levels (levels.py per-file, corpus.py distribution, critical.py weighted bound; python -I <script> apps/compiler/tmp/samples). Proven on residual C call graphs: median 2 levels/breadth 2; mega_brzozowski 7-9 levels, breadth 4-17, weighted bound 3.6-4.9x; omniledger fully inlined (0 methods). Open: the same metrics on hopacsota's real key graph from a dfs worklist trace with measured per-key time (needs a trace hook in the hopacsota layer), then critical-path-first ordering A/B.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_lf_scheduling_5 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", 5u32, "2026-10-17T14:17Z", "main-thread", "10:17 main-thread fork: LOCATION (supersedes earlier paths): spiral/lab/hopacsota-lf-scheduling/ (levels.py, corpus.py, critical.py; python -I lab/hopacsota-lf-scheduling/corpus.py apps/compiler/tmp/samples).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_lf_scheduling_6 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", 6u32, "2026-10-17T14:36Z", "main-thread", "10:36 main-thread fork: policy simulation (spiral/lab/hopacsota-lf-scheduling/schedule.py: list scheduling on the SCC-condensed residual call DAG, a method runs once its callees are done, cost = body lines; python -I schedule.py apps/compiler/tmp/samples <P>). Critical-path-first (HLFET upward rank) reaches the lower bound max(critical, work/P) within 0-4% on every graph at P=2/4/8. Leaves-first is the bad case: at P=4 runtime_bench 1,338 vs 1,006 (33% slower), inventory_closed_dfa_runtime 1,128 vs 872 (29%), indexed_dfa 1,047 vs 851 (23%); random order sits between (up to 24% slower). At P=8 workers are abundant and order mostly stops mattering (leaves-first still 13-20% slower on 3 graphs). Decision input for P2/P3: order the hopacsota worklist by upward rank (known after one build, or estimated by key depth), keep the D6 merge in single-flight order so output is unchanged; the gain is largest when workers < breadth, i.e. under the suite's parallel load.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_lf_scheduling_7 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", 7u32, "2026-10-17T15:01Z", "main-thread", "11:01 main-thread fork: the first build has no weights, so schedule.py now also tries a structural proxy: hop-rank = number of methods on the longest path to the root (graph shape only), ties broken by the node's own size. At P=2 it is within 0-5% of weighted critical-first everywhere; at P=4 within 0-6% on 4 of 6 graphs (runtime_bench 1,012 vs 1,006, cube equal) and 6-9% behind on the other 3 dfa graphs (906 vs 851, 918 vs 872, 786 vs 723) - still well ahead of leaves-first (+23-33%) and random. Decision input: first build orders by hop-rank (available as soon as the key graph is discovered), later builds by the weighted upward rank measured in the previous build (the D16.3 hint store already keeps per-key data).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_lf_scheduling_8 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", 8u32, "2026-10-17T15:01Z", "main-thread", "11:01 main-thread fork: correction to the hop-rank P=4 numbers: equal or within 1% of weighted critical-first on 3 of 6 graphs (bench_interned, runtime_bench 1,012 vs 1,006, cube) and 5-9% behind on the other 3 (inventory 918 vs 872 = 5.3%, indexed_dfa 906 vs 851 = 6.5%, runtime_native 786 vs 723 = 8.7%).")
inl note_of_hopacsota_lf_scheduling_9 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", 9u32, "2026-10-17T15:11Z", "main-thread", "11:11 main-thread fork: HANDOFF 2 (lane paused): lab/hopacsota-lf-scheduling has levels.py, corpus.py, critical.py, schedule.py. Settled: critical-path-first reaches the bound within 0-4%; leaves-first up to 33% slower at P=4; hop-rank (structure only) is the first-build proxy. Open: the same on hopacsota's real key graph (needs a worklist trace hook), then the ordering A/B.")
inl note_of_hopacsota_lf_scheduling_10 () = Note ("HOPACSOTA-LF-SCHEDULING", 10u32, "2026-10-17T22:05Z", "main-thread", "18:05 main-thread lab cleanup: the four LF simulation .py files deleted; their results (worklist order: longest remaining chain first within 4 percent) are absorbed in lanes/hopacsota/PLAN.md 2026-10-17. A rerun, if wanted, should be written in Spiral.")
inl lib_reactors () = Task ("LIB-REACTORS", "A reactor library in Spiral after Lingua Franca's model (user 2026-10-17): reactors with typed input/output ports, state, timers and logical/physical actions, reactions with declared triggers/sources/effects, logical time tags (time, microstep) and after-delays, banks/multiports, modal reactors, deterministic execution by precedence levels, deadlines; modeled GADT-first (port direction and payload type in the index, existential reactor packing for heterogeneous graphs, HKT over the scheduler) so the graph is checked at compile time and partial evaluation flattens it to straight-line code per backend (Zig/Rust/C/Lean). Steal LF's semantics and test corpus shapes (test/C/src: concurrent, generics, multiport, modal_models; examples-lingua-franca patterns Chain/Loop/FullyConnected/ForkJoin), not its runtimes. Stretch: LF's @property MTL specs (verifier/ CToUclidVisitor + statespace explorer) as properties proved on the Lean backend. Generic library work; consumers decide their own use.", Feature, P2, D6, 50u32, Paused, "", "lib,spiral,prior-art,lean", "current", "current")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_1 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 1u32, "2026-10-17T13:18Z", "main-thread", "09:18 main-thread fork: first prototype compiles and runs (scratch, job-tmp study/reactors/main.spi, 60 lines; not in lib/ yet): union signal t = Present | Absent (LF's absent port), type reactor s i o = {init : s; react : s -> i -> s * o}, timer/scale/after (one-tag logical delay)/sink reactors, chain composes at inl level so the precedence order is static, run is one let rec over logical time. Partial evaluation erases the records and closures: the whole timer->scale->after->sink graph becomes one C loop0(count, held signal, sum, seen) - the fused straight-line form the item asks for. 20 ticks, timer every 4: C exit 305, Lua 305, Zig 49 (= 305 mod 256: Zig exits u8; keep test results < 256). Spiral facts learned: record field access needs a named record type (no row polymorphism on inl params); inl patterns take one tuple level (inl a, b = ...), not nested parens; conversion is !!!!Conv(i32, x); untyped integer literals are i32. Next: ports as GADT indices (direction + payload in the type), banks/multiports, microsteps, an LF pattern test (Chain/Loop/ForkJoin) as Spiral test blocks.")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_2 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 2u32, "2026-10-17T13:35Z", "main-thread", "09:35 main-thread fork: LF ForkJoin pattern works (job-tmp study/reactors/forkjoin.spi): both a b (same input to two reactors, paired outputs) builds a compile-time bank of 4 scale reactors, join4 merges with LF's absent semantics (Present+Absent = the present value), all chained after a timer and into a sink. 12 ticks, timer every 3 (values 1..4), bank x1..x4, join sum -> sink 100 + 4 seen = exit 104 as computed by hand. Still one loop0 with the bank unrolled inline (v*2, v*3, v*4 statements): the static graph is fused as intended. Next: a banks combinator over n (inl rec over a compile-time count instead of nested both), microstep tags for zero-delay cycles, and multiports typed by a GADT index.")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_3 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 3u32, "2026-10-17T13:49Z", "main-thread", "09:49 main-thread fork: banks of a compile-time size n (LF new[n]): a real-dialect helper (job-tmp study/reactors/bank_real.spir: open real_core; inl rec bank n mk = if n = 1i32 then mk 1i32 else both (mk n) (bank (n - 1i32) mk), plus join_signals over the nested output pairs) builds the nested both tree, but the typed .spi side cannot receive it: annotating the result as reactor _ (signal i64) _ hits ETY-ValueRestriction (holes are not allowed for a non-value), and the state type differs per n. Design consequence for the lib: (a) composition (chain/both/bank/run) lives in the real dialect and only the program's edge types (inputs, outputs, final result) are typed .spi; or (b) banks of identical members keep their states in an array (one loop over members, parallel-friendly); or (c) a type-level natural index, blocked by GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG. Plan: (a) for heterogeneous graphs, (b) for LF multiports/banks.")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_4 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 4u32, "2026-10-17T13:56Z", "main-thread", "09:56 main-thread fork: option (b) works - homogeneous bank with array state (job-tmp study/reactors/arraybank.spi): member states in one array_base i64 created once (!!!!ArrayCreate), each tag a let rec over members reads/updates its slot (!!!!ArrayIndex / !!!!ArrayIndexSet), member count is a RUNTIME value (inl ~members = 4i32) and the types stay fixed. 4 members x 5 ticks (member i adds input*(i+1)): C exit 94 = 350 mod 256, matching an independent Python computation. This is the LF bank/multiport form, and its per-member loop is the natural place for parallel execution later (members of one level are independent). Heterogeneous graphs keep the inl-composed form (fused loop0), banks of identical members use this array form.")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_5 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 5u32, "2026-10-17T14:10Z", "main-thread", "10:10 main-thread fork: HANDOFF (fork lane closed): job-tmp study/reactors is now %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\study\\2026-10-17\\reactors (main.spi chain -> 305, forkjoin.spi -> 104, arraybank.spi -> 94, bank_real.spir sketch; compile any with ..\\convo-spiral\\try.ps1 -Name X -Source file -Root <dir>, it kills after 60 s and uses the shared single-flight DLL). Proven: inl-composed reactors fuse into one loop; ForkJoin with absent semantics; array-state banks with a runtime member count. Decided: heterogeneous graphs = inl composition, identical-member banks = array state (n-indexed typed banks hit the value restriction). Open: microsteps/zero-delay cycles, GADT-typed ports, LF pattern tests as Spiral test blocks, then move into lib/spiral via a scratch copy (never half-edited lib files).")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_6 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 6u32, "2026-10-17T14:17Z", "main-thread", "10:17 main-thread fork: LOCATION (supersedes earlier paths): spiral/lab/lib-reactors/ - chain.spi (305), forkjoin.spi (104), array-bank.spi (94), bank-value-restriction.spi + bank_real.spir (the ValueRestriction evidence); all reverified 2026-10-17 from the repo with pwsh lab/try.ps1 -Source <file> [-Modules <x_real.spir>] [-Backend C|Zig|Lua|Rust] (60 s kill, shared single-flight DLL or SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL, outputs in git-ignored lab/.runs).")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_7 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 7u32, "2026-10-17T14:43Z", "main-thread", "10:43 main-thread fork: (1) port typing for free: lab/lib-reactors/negative-port-type-mismatch.spi wires a signal f64 reactor after a signal i64 one and fails at compile time (ETY-TermError: react : f64 -> signal f64 ... vs signal i64), i.e. LF's connection type check is just chain's type; keep it as a negative test block when this moves to lib. (2) microsteps (lab/lib-reactors/microsteps.spi): tag = {time; micro}, a reaction that emits Present re-schedules itself at the same time and micro+1 (LF zero-delay logical action), Absent advances logical time, a cap bounds zero-delay cycles; countdown bursts 3,2,1 at t=0 and t=5 -> sum 12 over 6 microsteps, C exit 126 = Python check. Next: microsteps across a chained graph (each reactor sees the same tag), deadlines, then the lib move with test blocks.")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_8 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 8u32, "2026-10-17T14:54Z", "main-thread", "10:54 main-thread fork: microsteps through a chained graph (lab/lib-reactors/microsteps-chain.spi): reactor s i o now takes the tag as an argument (react : s -> tag -> i -> s * signal o) and chain passes the SAME tag to every member, so a downstream reactor (stamp) sees each microstep's exact {time; micro}; countdown -> stamp (time*1000 + micro*10 + value) -> total, 10 ticks, bursts at t=0 and 5: total 15,072 over 6 microsteps, exit 230 = Python model. This is LF's superdense time with a fused chain (still one loop). Next: deadlines (a reaction whose physical lag exceeds its deadline runs a handler) and the lib move with test blocks.")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_9 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 9u32, "2026-10-17T15:08Z", "main-thread", "11:08 main-thread fork: deadlines (lab/lib-reactors/deadlines.spi): clock = {logical; physical}, with_deadline d body on_violation wraps any reactor and runs the handler when physical - logical > d (LF deadline semantics); physical time simulated by a deterministic lag sequence (lag 9 every 4th tick, else t mod 3) so the result is checkable: 12 ticks, deadline 5 -> 3 violations handled, 9 on-time samples summing 450, exit 3450 = Python model. Prototype set now: chain (fused loop), forkjoin, array banks, port typing (negative test), microsteps alone and through a chain, deadlines. Next: move into lib/spiral as reactors.spi with these as test blocks via a scratch lib copy, then a real physical clock on the native backends.")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_10 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 10u32, "2026-10-17T15:11Z", "main-thread", "11:11 main-thread fork: HANDOFF 2 (lane paused): lab/lib-reactors has chain, forkjoin, array-bank, microsteps, microsteps-chain, deadlines (+ negative-port-type-mismatch, bank-value-restriction evidence); every lab experiment re-verified 11:09 from the repo (25 of 26 pass; the 26th, gadt repro, now compiles because the bug is fixed). Open: lib move (reactors.spi with test blocks via a scratch lib copy), real physical clock on native backends.")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_11 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 11u32, "2026-10-17T22:05Z", "main-thread", "18:05 main-thread lib move done (unstaged): lib/spiral/reactors.spi (module registered after async) unifies the prototypes into one model: signal, tag {time; micro}, reactor s i o = {init; react : s -> tag -> i -> s * o}, chain, both, with_deadline (input = physical lag), run_ticks, run_micro (fold over present outputs, microstep cap). Six test blocks on C and Rust, 6/6 green through cells: chain_delays_one_tick (45, 5; the unified timer emits its ordinal), both_fans_out_and_joins (100, 4), microsteps_run_until_absent (12, 6), microsteps_share_the_tag_through_a_chain (15072, 6), deadlines_run_the_violation_handler (450, 3), array_bank_keeps_member_state (350, via core a arrays, no raw ops). Prototype oracle rerun first (exits 305/104/94/126/230/3450 matched). The port-typing negative became harness sample samples/reactors_negative_port_type (|core-, ExpectedErrors Contains the Expected: ... signal i64 record; 4 backends error as intended, new rows until the next bless). bank-value-restriction is a language limitation, not a bug: a bank built in real mode cannot be bound in top-down code (ETY-ValueRestriction on the unannotated reactor record); banks stay explicit (both) or runtime (array). lab/lib-reactors deleted. Open: modal reactors, real physical clock on native backends, GADT port direction in the index.")
inl note_of_lib_reactors_12 () = Note ("LIB-REACTORS", 12u32, "2026-10-17T22:16Z", "main-thread", "18:16 main-thread: samples/reactors_negative_port_type now uses lib reactors through packageDir (timer -> a gauge reactor taking signal f64: Expected ... signal i64), no private copy of the definitions.")
inl convo_in_spiral () = Task ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", "Rewrite what is useful from Convo-Lang (convo-lang/convo-lang, GPL-3.0, TypeScript; nothing vendored) in Spiral itself with GADTs/existentials/HKTs/prototypes/reflection (user 2026-10-17). Useful parts (packages/convo-lang/src/lib, examples/convo 290 files): the transcript is both program and record (> system/user/assistant/define blocks, completions appended back with @tokenUsage/@format tags); functions with doc-commented typed params become tool schemas; struct types drive @json structured output validated against the type; @condition'd messages; graph-routed agents (convo-node-graph); a completion cache keyed by the sorted hash of the messages (ConvoHashCacheBase) = deterministic replay without network; a global concurrency lock; ConvoModelTester runs a convo across models asserting expected roles, format, function called and return value with duration. Spiral design: role-indexed GADT messages, tool registry as an existential list with reflection-derived JSON schema (prototypes per type), HKT over the completion provider, type-directed decoders for structured output, transcripts as livemd/.spi cells whose recorded completions are inline expectations, hash-keyed record/replay as the default test mode, assertions as Spiral test blocks. First consumers: eoie one-call TDD cases (spec/one-call.md), xsim, the X client.", Epic, P2, D6, 65u32, Paused, "", "spiral,lib,eoie,prior-art", "current", "current")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_1 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-17T13:20Z", "main-thread", "09:20 main-thread fork: (1) scope mined from Convo's 290 .convo files (job-tmp study/convo-mine/mine.py): files using > user 270, > assistant 215, @import 176 (composition of convos), @tokenUsage 152 / @model 147 / @time 140 (each completion recorded inline), > define 94, > system 75, typed functions 47 with recorded > call 30 / > result 29, struct 34, @json 24, @enableVision 15; rare: @edge graph routing 8, @on 8, @condition 7, components/RAG/tailwind 1-3. MVP = transcript + import + recorded completion metadata + typed tools with call/result records + struct-typed @json output; skip vision/components/RAG. (2) First Spiral sketch compiles and runs (job-tmp study/convo-spiral/main.spi): role-indexed GADT union message role (System/User/Assistant :: string * recorded/Call/Result), transcript = union rec of (exists role. message role * transcript) built with say, walked by inl rec with inl exists unpacking; a call/result balance check and a weight fold run entirely in partial evaluation - the C residual is only the arithmetic over the one runtime value (exit 110 = expected). Spiral facts: an existential must be the whole case field (Say :: (exists role. message role * transcript)), packed as Say (exists (m, rest)); runtime-loaded transcripts need a plain (non-GADT) union, the GADT index is for statically built flows. Next: make an unanswered Call a type error (index transcript by a pending-call phantom), reflection-derived tool schema from a function's record type, and a hash-keyed record/replay completion store.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_2 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-10-17T13:33Z", "main-thread", "09:33 main-thread fork: type-level tool-call protocol designed and half-proven (job-tmp study/convo-spiral/protocol.spi): union rec transcript pending indexed by the count of tail Results not yet claimed by a Call (Done : transcript zero; Result : n -> succ n; Call : succ n -> n; others keep n). An unanswered Call and an orphan Result are both rejected at compile time with clear errors (Got transcript (succ zero) / Expected transcript zero). The valid transcript hits compiler bug GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG (any value of such a union hangs compilation), so the protocol waits on that fix; the existential-per-message sketch (main.spi, exit 110) remains the working form meanwhile.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_3 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-10-17T13:43Z", "main-thread", "09:43 main-thread fork: reflection-derived tool schema is WIP (job-tmp study/convo-spiral/schema_real.spir + schema2-main.spi; bounded runner try.ps1). Working pieces: a .spir module listed as 'schema_real*' in package.spiproj; .spi -> real via a forall of the calling .spi function (schema_of forall t. (name) (_ : t) = real schema_real.tool_schema name `t); real functions take term args first and return a forall (inl tool_schema name = forall params. => ...), type-only ones as 'inl json_type forall t. : string' with typecase; a type application binds LOOSER than + (name + json_type `(i32) parses as (name + json_type) `(i32) -> 'Cannot apply a forall with a term'), so parenthesize every type application inside an expression. Blocked: any real_core.record_type_fold over the parameter record (even with a constant folder body, in a .spir module or in a .spi real block like lib reflection.spi) fails with 'Cannot apply a forall with a term'; the compiler's evaluation (single-flight EOp RecordTypeFold: apply f state, apply DSymbol key, then type_apply the field type) matches lib's folder shape, so the suspect is how the record TYPE argument reaches record_type_fold's returned forall from a top-down forall variable (lib only calls it on a type bound by real's own forall inside union_to_record's callback). Next: bind the record type with a real-side forall (a real helper taking `t and calling the fold inside its own forall), or fold with real_core.record_type_try_find over known keys as a fallback.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_4 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 4u32, "2026-10-17T13:46Z", "main-thread", "09:46 main-thread fork: schema bisect state (job-tmp study/convo-spiral/p-*/schema_real.spir + try.ps1, each variant compiled under a 60 s kill): OK = tool_schema name = forall params. => name (exit 10); json_type `(i32) (7); json_type `params (6 = 'object', i.e. params falls to typecase's _ branch). FAIL 'Cannot apply a forall with a term' = every record op on params: real_core.record_type_fold (multi-line, one-line, bound folder, .spi real block, literal record type), real_core.record_type_length `params (plain, parenthesized, aliased) and even the raw !!!!RecordTypeLength(`params). Lead: params arriving through schema_of forall t. (_ : t) from inl args : get_weather is not a bare record type at that point (alias/nominal or an unresolved forall), and the working lib path (arg_union -> reflection.get_union_fields_untag, passed in last night's corpus) only folds records produced by union_to_record's callback. Next session: print the type with !!!!PrintStatic inside tool_schema, then try schema_of with an explicit record type annotation instead of the type alias, or pass the record type with `({location : string; ...}) from .spi.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_5 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 5u32, "2026-10-17T13:48Z", "main-thread", "09:48 main-thread fork: SOLVED - reflection-derived tool schema works (job-tmp study/convo-spiral/schema_real.spir + schema2-main.spi): type get_weather = {location : string; days : i32; metric : bool} compiles to ONE C string literal, the JSON schema {name: getWeather, parameters: {type: object, properties: {location: string, days: integer, metric: boolean}}}, via real_core.record_type_fold + typecase + !!!!SymbolToString, zero runtime cost. Root cause of the earlier 'Cannot apply a forall with a term': a .spir module must start with 'open real_core' (core's own iam_real.spir does), else + and = resolve to forall-typed definitions; real + is numeric !!!!Add, so string joins need !!!!StaticStringConcat (defined here as inl (++) a b). Also: list the module as schema_real* in package.spiproj; parenthesize type applications inside expressions. Next: nested record/array/union fields (recursive typecase), optional fields, descriptions from a parallel record, then decode a model's JSON reply back into the same record type.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_6 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 6u32, "2026-10-17T13:55Z", "main-thread", "09:55 main-thread fork: nested schemas work (job-tmp study/convo-spiral/nested_real.spir + nested-main.spi): one recursive inl rec schema_of forall t. with typecase over primitives, array_base ~el (items schema) and {} (object: record_type_fold over the fields, recursing per value type). type get_forecast = {where : place; days : i32; hours : array_base i32} with place = {city : string; lat : f64} compiles to one C string literal: an object whose where is a nested object (city string, lat number), days integer, hours array of integer. Remaining for the tool layer: unions as oneOf/enum (union_to_record), optional fields, descriptions, field order (record_type_fold walks the map order, here location/days/metric came out in declaration order but that should be checked against Map key order), and the reverse direction: a type-directed JSON decoder into the same record.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_7 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 7u32, "2026-10-17T13:55Z", "main-thread", "09:55 main-thread fork: field order checked: record_type_fold walks a map keyed by (field index, name), so schemas list fields in declaration order (where, days, hours here; alphabetical would be days, hours, where). No sort needed.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_8 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 8u32, "2026-10-17T13:58Z", "main-thread", "09:58 main-thread fork: unions as enums work (job-tmp study/convo-spiral/p-enum/schema_real.spir + enum-main.spi): the _ branch checks union_type_is and folds union_to_record's case record keys; union unit_system = Metric | Imperial inside type set_units = {system : unit_system; precision : i32} compiles to one literal whose system property is a string enum [Metric, Imperial] (declaration order) and precision an integer. Covered now: primitives, nested records, arrays, payload-less unions. Next: unions with payloads as oneOf, option as optional field, the reverse JSON decoder.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_9 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 9u32, "2026-10-17T14:10Z", "main-thread", "10:10 main-thread fork: HANDOFF (fork lane closed): job-tmp study/convo-mine and study/convo-spiral are now %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\study\\2026-10-17\\convo-mine and %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\study\\2026-10-17\\convo-spiral (main.spi existential transcript -> 110; protocol.spi type-level call/result protocol, rejects bad transcripts, valid one blocked by GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG; schema_real.spir + schema2-main.spi flat schema; nested_real.spir + nested-main.spi nested/array; p-enum unions as enum; try.ps1 bounded runner). Proven: compile-time JSON schema by reflection for primitives, nested records, arrays, payload-less unions (one C string literal, declaration order). Open: payload unions as oneOf, option as optional, descriptions, type-directed JSON decoder, hash-keyed record/replay store, then lib placement and first consumer (eoie one-call cases).")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_10 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 10u32, "2026-10-17T14:17Z", "main-thread", "10:17 main-thread fork: LOCATION (supersedes earlier paths): spiral/lab/convo-in-spiral/ - transcript.spi (110), protocol.spi (type-level protocol, valid case blocked by GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG), schema.spi + schema_real.spir (150), nested.spi + nested_real.spir (236), enum.spi + enum_real.spir (152), mine.py (python -I mine.py <a convo-lang clone>); all reverified from the repo with pwsh lab/try.ps1 -Source <file> [-Modules <x_real.spir>] [-Backend C|Zig|Lua|Rust] (60 s kill, shared single-flight DLL or SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL, outputs in git-ignored lab/.runs).")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_11 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 11u32, "2026-10-17T14:42Z", "main-thread", "10:42 main-thread fork: schema coverage complete for tool parameters (spiral/lab/convo-in-spiral/full_real.spir + full.spi, run: pwsh lab/try.ps1 -Source lab/convo-in-spiral/full.spi -Modules lab/convo-in-spiral/full_real.spir): option fields are left out of required, payload unions become oneOf (payload case = object keyed by the case name, nullary case = const), all-nullary unions a string oneOf of consts, tuples arrays with prefixItems, plus primitives, nested records and arrays; one compile-time literal (452 bytes for a 4-field forecast record), exit 5 = 453 mod 256 with the tuple added. Spiral facts: join is a keyword (a helper named join gave 'Expected a function... Got:' far from the cause), and type-only functions in an inl rec ... and inl group need a unit argument (forall t. () : string). Next: descriptions from a parallel record, then the reverse direction (type-directed JSON decoder), which is the larger piece.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_12 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 12u32, "2026-10-17T14:42Z", "main-thread", "10:42 main-thread fork: correction: the full forecast schema is 451 characters before tuples (exit 195) and 517 with tuple prefixItems (exit 5 = 517 mod 256), not 453.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_13 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 13u32, "2026-10-17T14:51Z", "main-thread", "10:51 main-thread fork: reverse direction started - type-directed JSON decoder works (spiral/lab/convo-in-spiral/decode.spi + json_scan.spi + decode_real.spir; in PowerShell call the runner in-process: & lab/try.ps1 -Source lab/convo-in-spiral/decode.spi -Modules @('lab/convo-in-spiral/json_scan.spi','lab/convo-in-spiral/decode_real.spir')). decode_real.decode s t uses record_type_map over the target record, each field's parser picked by typecase on its type (i32, string, bool) from typed .spi scanners (value_at finds the key, parse_i32/parse_string/parse_bool); a runtime reply string {location: Cincinnati, days: 3, metric: true} decodes into get_weather: exit 113 = 3 + 100 + 10 as computed. Learned: in .spi, + on strings is a constraint error (use !!!!StaticStringConcat, which also works on runtime strings), char has no ordering and no conversion to i32 (digit lookup by equality). Limits now: flat records, no escapes inside strings, no nested objects/arrays/unions/option - next is recursion through the same typecase arms the schema uses, then the record/replay store keyed by message hash.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_14 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 14u32, "2026-10-17T14:52Z", "main-thread", "10:52 main-thread fork: decoder handles nested records (decode-nested.spi; runner now takes -Modules a,b through pwsh -File too): field and decode are one inl rec ... and inl group, a record-typed field slices its object by brace matching (object_end skips string contents and escapes) and decodes it recursively; {days: 3, where: {city: Cincinnati, days: 40}, metric: true} -> exit 153 = 3 + 100 + 40 + 10, flat decode.spi still 113. Known flaw: value_at finds the first occurrence of a key at any depth, so a nested key that comes BEFORE the same top-level key would be read as the top-level one - next fix: scan only depth-1 keys of the current object (reuse object_end's depth walk), then arrays, option (missing key = None) and unions (oneOf by case key), mirroring full_real.spir's schema arms.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_15 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 15u32, "2026-10-17T14:55Z", "main-thread", "10:55 main-thread fork: decoder key lookup fixed: json_scan.value_at now walks the object tracking depth and string state (escapes skipped) and matches a key only at depth 1 outside strings. Regression input decode-key-depth.spi puts the nested where.days (40) BEFORE the top-level days and adds a string value whose text is an escaped days:9 key; top-level days still reads 3 (exit 153), decode.spi 113 and decode-nested.spi 153 unchanged. Next: arrays (items between brackets at depth), option (missing key = None), unions (case key), and string escapes in parse_string.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_16 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 16u32, "2026-10-17T15:00Z", "main-thread", "11:00 main-thread fork: option fields decode (decode-option.spi): a missing key is None, a present one Some of the decoded value; {city: Recife, days: 4} into {city : string; days : option i32; units : option string} -> exit 60 = 4 + 50 (units None) + 6; decode/decode-nested/decode-key-depth unchanged (113/153/153). Real-dialect fact: the core option constructors take their type first there (None `inner, Some `inner x), else 'Cannot dyn a forall into a runtime var' / 'Cannot apply a forall with a term'. Decoder now covers primitives, nested records, option, depth-correct keys; left: arrays, unions, string escapes.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_17 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 17u32, "2026-10-17T15:07Z", "main-thread", "11:07 main-thread fork: arrays decode (decode-arrays.spi): json_scan.item_positions walks the array at depth 1 (strings and escapes skipped, so commas/brackets inside strings do not split), counts items, then allocates and records each item start; parse_i32_array / parse_string_array fill typed arrays; decode_real dispatches array_base by element type. {customer: ana, quantities: [3, 40, 200], tags: [a,b / ]x]} -> 233243 = 3*1000 + 243 + 3*10000 + 2*100000 exactly. Spiral facts reapplied: bind values before passing them to !!!! ops, and use block form for if-then sequences (a one-line 'if c then a; f x' sequences as (if c then a); f x). Decoder now: primitives, nested records, option, arrays of i32/string, depth-correct keys. Left: arrays of records/other element types (generic fill), unions, string escapes in values.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_18 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 18u32, "2026-10-17T15:10Z", "main-thread", "11:10 main-thread fork: type-level tool-call protocol UNBLOCKED by the GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG fix: lab/convo-in-spiral/protocol.spi (transcript pending indexed by unclaimed tail Results) now compiles and runs (exit 110, same weights as the existential form), and negative-protocol-unanswered-call.spi / negative-protocol-orphan-result.spi are compile errors (Got transcript (succ zero) / Expected transcript zero). So an unanswered tool call is a TYPE error now.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_19 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 19u32, "2026-10-17T15:11Z", "main-thread", "11:11 main-thread fork: HANDOFF 2 (lane paused): lab/convo-in-spiral has transcript, protocol (+2 negatives), schema/nested/enum/full (_real.spir) for schemas, decode* + json_scan.spi + decode_real.spir for decoding, mine.py; every lab experiment re-verified 11:09 from the repo (25 of 26 pass; the 26th, gadt repro, now compiles because the bug is fixed). Covered: compile-time JSON schema (primitives, records, arrays, tuples, option, unions as oneOf/enum); runtime decoder (primitives, nested records, option, i32/string arrays, depth-correct keys); type-level call/result protocol. Open: arrays of records, union decoding, string escapes, record/replay store, lib placement, first consumer (eoie one-call cases).")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_20 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 20u32, "2026-10-17T15:14Z", "main-thread", "11:14 main-thread fork: arrays of records decode (decode-record-array.spi): json_scan.fill_array forall el. (count) (item : i32 -> el) is a generic typed .spi filler, and decode_real passes a closure that decodes each item's object slice recursively (real side applies the type first: fill_array `el ...); {customer: ana, lines: [{sku: p1, qty: 2}, {sku: p22, qty: 30}]} -> 32232 exactly. Decoder suite 6 of 6 (113, 153, 153, 60, 233243, 32232). Note for tests: values read with !!!!ArrayIndex need a type annotation before field access. Left: union decoding, string escapes, record/replay store.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_21 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 21u32, "2026-10-17T15:16Z", "main-thread", "11:16 main-thread fork: unions decode (decode-union.spi), mirroring the schema's oneOf: a nullary case is its name as a string, a payload case a one-key object {Case: payload}; decode_real folds union_to_record's cases at compile time into a runtime if-chain on the case name and builds the value with nominal_create (key, payload) - the same constructor lib reflection uses; {units: Imperial, where: {City: Recife}, other: {Grid: {row: 4, col: 7}}} -> 4762 = 2 + 60 + 4700. The decoder is now one positional decode_at (field lookup only in decode), and the suite is 7 of 7 (113, 153, 153, 60, 233243, 32232, 4762). Coverage now matches the schema side except tuples and string escapes. Paused here.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_22 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 22u32, "2026-10-17T15:17Z", "main-thread", "11:17 main-thread fork: tuples decode (decode-tuple.spi) as 2-item arrays, matching the schema's prefixItems: {span: [12, days], where: {Coordinates: [3, 9]}} into {span : i32 * string; where : location} -> 43912 = 12 + 40000 + 3900, including a tuple union payload. Decoder suite 8 of 8 (lab/convo-in-spiral/decode*.spi with json_scan.spi + decode_real.spir). Decoder and schema now cover the same types; open: string escapes in values, tuples longer than 2, record/replay store, lib placement. Paused.")
inl note_of_convo_in_spiral_23 () = Note ("CONVO-IN-SPIRAL", 23u32, "2026-10-17T22:11Z", "main-thread", "18:11 main-thread lib move done (unstaged): the typed JSON codec is lib/spiral json_scan.spi (runtime scanner) + json_real.spir (full schema: records with required, options, tuples via prefixItems, unions as const/oneOf, arrays; plus the typecase decoder) + json.spi (public decode and tool_schema, value witness for the type), registered after reactors; 9 test blocks on C and Rust, 9/9 through cells (the 8-case decoder suite 113/60/153/153/233243/32232/4762/43912 plus the exact tool schema JSON). The call/result protocol is lib/spiral/conversation.spi: a GADT transcript indexed by pending calls (no_pending, pending_call n), so an unanswered call or an orphan result is a type error; test an_answered_call_closes_the_transcript (weight 11118 = prototype exit 110 mod 256). Its two negatives became harness samples conversation_negative_orphan_result and conversation_negative_unanswered_call (ExpectedErrors Expected: transcript zero, 4 backends, new rows until the next bless). Superseded and deleted: the flat/enum/nested schema iterations, the runtime transcript checker, mine.py (its corpus counts are in note 1). lab/convo-in-spiral removed. Open: string escapes in decoded values, tuples longer than 2, record/replay store, the .convo surface syntax.")
inl gadt_growing_index_hang () = Task ("GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG", "Compiler hang (both builds tested: candidate single-flight DLL of pack night-1017): a value of a recursive GADT whose case FIELD mentions the type at a grown index never finishes compiling (CPU-bound, ~230 MB, killed after 45-250 s). 8-line repro: nominal zero = (); nominal succ n = (); union rec transcript pending = | Done :: transcript zero | Call :: forall n. i32 * transcript (succ n) -> transcript n; inl main () : i32 = (inl t : transcript zero = Done) then 7i32. Same union defined but unused: compiles. Index kept the same (chain n -> chain n) or a non-recursive index change (box (succ n)): compile and run. Wrong programs over the full Call/Result transcript are rejected fast and correctly (Got transcript (succ zero) / Expected transcript zero), so type checking is fine; the suspect is the representation/reachable-cases closure of a GADT-indexed union (pack-1x: 'a GADT-indexed union holds only its reachable cases'), which expands transcript zero -> transcript (succ zero) -> ... without bound. Fix: compute reachable cases over the index modulo the case's quantified variables (a case whose field index grows is reachable for every instance, no expansion), or bound the closure and fall back to all cases; add the repro as the regression sample. Found while prototyping CONVO-IN-SPIRAL's type-level tool-call protocol (unanswered Call = type error).", TaskKind, P1, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "compiler,gadt,bug", "current", "current")
inl note_of_gadt_growing_index_hang_1 () = Note ("GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG", 1u32, "2026-10-17T13:34Z", "main-thread", "09:34 main-thread fork: correction to the title: only the single-flight candidate DLL (job-tmp night/cand-a, same source as the installed pack) was tested; gpt5parallel and hopacsota are untested. Repro files in job-tmp study/convo-spiral/v-grow-field.spi and v-min.spi; bounded runner try.ps1 (kills after N s).")
inl note_of_gadt_growing_index_hang_2 () = Note ("GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG", 2u32, "2026-10-17T14:10Z", "main-thread", "10:10 main-thread fork: HANDOFF: repro files moved to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\study\\2026-10-17\\convo-spiral (v-grow-field.spi = 8-line repro, v-min.spi, v-unused.spi compiles, v-rec-same-index.spi and v-nonrec-index.spi compile); run with %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\study\\2026-10-17\\convo-spiral\\try.ps1 -Name x -Source <file> (60 s kill). Not started; fix belongs to the compiler lane.")
inl note_of_gadt_growing_index_hang_3 () = Note ("GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG", 3u32, "2026-10-17T14:17Z", "main-thread", "10:17 main-thread fork: LOCATION: spiral/lab/gadt-growing-index-hang/ - repro.spi (hangs: TIMEOUT 30 s), call-result.spi (hangs), defined-unused-compiles.spi / same-index-compiles.spi / nonrecursive-index-compiles.spi (exit 7). Reverified on the REBUILT installed single-flight DLL (after the rc.2 rebuild), so the bug is in the current tree. When fixed: repro.spi becomes the regression sample under apps/compiler/tmp/samples and this lab dir is deleted.")
inl note_of_gadt_growing_index_hang_4 () = Note ("GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG", 4u32, "2026-10-17T14:39Z", "main-thread", "10:39 main-thread fork: ROOT CAUSE located (read-only, single-flight PartEval ~159191-159290 of spiral_compiler.fs): ty (TUnion ...) computes each case payload type and drops it when ty_is_uninhabited holds (the pruning added for lane F's compiler_probe_gadt_uninhabited_payload_runtime 'Pattern miss'). ty_is_uninhabited has a depth cap (loop 32) but follows YNominal/YApply through nominal_type_apply, which evaluates the applied union's TUnion again, which calls ty_is_uninhabited with a FRESH cap of 32. With a growing index (transcript (succ n) -> transcript (succ (succ n)) -> ...) every nested instance is new, so neither the cap nor any memo stops it: unbounded recursion, CPU-bound with slowly growing memory, triggered only when a value forces the union's representation (matches every bisect result). Fix sketch: make the inhabitation check coinductive per nominal head - carry a set of nominal global ids currently being expanded (in s or a [<ThreadStatic>]-free parameter) and answer 'inhabited' (false) on re-entry of the same head, or pass the remaining depth into nominal_type_apply's union evaluation instead of restarting at 32. Pruning is only an optimization, so assuming inhabited on re-entry is safe. Check the gpt5parallel pair for the same pattern; regression sample = lab/gadt-growing-index-hang/repro.spi plus the 3 *-compiles.spi controls and lane F's probe.")
inl note_of_gadt_growing_index_hang_5 () = Note ("GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG", 5u32, "2026-10-17T14:58Z", "main-thread", "10:58 main-thread: root cause found and fixed in the tree (both cores). PartEval's TUnion evaluation drops cases whose payload is uninhabited with ty_is_uninhabited, which caps its own walk at depth 32. But it reaches a payload's union body through nominal_type_apply, and evaluating that TUnion calls ty_is_uninhabited again with a fresh depth of 32. Regular recursion (chain n -> chain n) stops when the second visit hits the type join point's pending entry, which raises and counts as inhabited. A growing index (transcript (succ n)) makes a new type join point key at every level, so the forcing never ends. Fix: one nesting budget of 32 shared across those re-entries (single-flight a ref cell, gpt5parallel a ThreadLocal); when it's exhausted the answer is 'inhabited', the conservative direction the existing fallbacks already take. Patch: lab/gadt-growing-index-hang/uninhabited-nesting.ps1 (-DryRun). Regression sample samples/frontier_gadt_growing_index (the repro): before, timeout at 20 s on F#/C/Rust/Delphi (hopacsota-20261017-104913); after, compiled in 34-835 ms, exit 7. The study's full Call/Result transcript (lab call-result.spi) compiles and runs too. Pack review with the Zig exit fix is running.")
inl note_of_gadt_growing_index_hang_6 () = Note ("GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG", 6u32, "2026-10-17T15:10Z", "main-thread", "11:10 main-thread fork: VERIFIED FIXED by the main lane's change in the tree (ty_is_uninhabited now counts nesting across re-entries with uninhabited_nesting, capped at 32 overall, instead of restarting its depth per nominal_type_apply; single-flight/gpt5parallel/hopacsota rebuilt 10:41-11:05, installed single-flight DLL 10:57): lab/gadt-growing-index-hang/repro.spi now compiles and runs (exit 7) instead of hanging, the 3 *-compiles.spi controls still exit 7, and the full Convo protocol transcript (previously hanging) compiles. Left per the lab lifecycle: add repro.spi as the regression sample under apps/compiler/tmp/samples with the review/bless, then delete lab/gadt-growing-index-hang. Note for hopacsota review: uninhabited_nesting is shared mutable state (gate G2 bans ThreadLocal and shared counters outside the engine) - check how it is declared before the hopacsota layer evaluates types in parallel.")
inl note_of_gadt_growing_index_hang_7 () = Note ("GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG", 7u32, "2026-10-17T15:47Z", "main-thread", "11:47 main-thread: DONE. Blessed from single-flight-20261017-111908 (6,915 rows, DISAGREE 0), which reproduces the review 105810 on every cell; hopacsota-20261017-111317 equals it too. The only changes from the 08:40 bless are Zig's 457 residuals (ZIG-HUB-SPEED's wide exit, same pack) and the new rows of the two regression samples. The repro is promoted to samples/frontier_gadt_growing_index and lab/gadt-growing-index-hang is deleted (lab lifecycle).")
inl lab_in_repo () = Task ("LAB-IN-REPO", "Experiments live in the repo, not in job tmp (user 2026-10-17: tmp overuse is drift; it also blocks continuing on another machine or in a cloud agent). Convention started in spiral/lab: one directory per backlog item named by its id lowercased (lab/convo-in-spiral, lab/lib-reactors, lab/hopacsota-hopac-depth, lab/hopacsota-lf-scheduling, lab/gadt-growing-index-hang), git-tracked sources only, one shared bounded runner lab/try.ps1 (60 s kill, shared DLL or SPIRAL_COMPILER_DLL), run outputs in git-ignored lab/.runs; portable references only (nuget refs, repo-relative paths, no user-profile paths). Lifecycle: when the item is Done, its lab dir is promoted (lib/, apps/, samples/) or deleted in the same change; an item Paused/Planned keeps it. Measured drift it replaces: this job's tmp holds ~850 scripts in ~120 topic folders (packs, lanes, probes, benches) plus spiral-bin\\packs, all lost with the job and invisible to other machines; the heavy gate itself (tmp/shared/heavy.ps1 with the run ledger) lives there. Next: (1) EOIE-LAB-AUDIT; (2) move the reusable main-lane tools (heavy gate + ledger, compare-runs, kino-suite runner, pack scripts) into repo homes (apps/compiler/tmp/scripts or lab/<item>); (3) per-repo lab/ in eoie, i574n.github, polyglot as they need it.", Story, P1, D4, 45u32, Paused, "", "process,lab,drift", "current", "current")
inl note_of_lab_in_repo_1 () = Note ("LAB-IN-REPO", 1u32, "2026-10-17T14:47Z", "main-thread", "10:47 main-thread fork: first main-lane tools moved into the repo: (1) apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/heavy.ps1 = the heavy gate + run ledger, comment-free and split into named functions (Wait-HeavySlot, Get-HeavyTicketPrefix, Wait-WslReady, Stop-IdleWsl); the FIFO queue moved from the job tmp to %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\heavy-queue (machine state, outlives jobs); exclusion still comes from the machine-global mutexes, so the job-tmp copy and this one exclude each other (smoke: repo copy took slot 2 while the parent held slot 1; both ledger rows written); until every lane sources the repo copy, FIFO order between the two queue dirs is not shared. (2) apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/compare-runs.ps1 = cell-by-cell diff of two harness runs (run name or dir), DISAGREE count; verified on bless 081502 vs reuse run 084050 (0 cells). Next: lanes switch to . apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/heavy.ps1, then the Kino suite runner and the pack scripts (spiral-bin\\packs) get repo homes.")
inl note_of_lab_in_repo_2 () = Note ("LAB-IN-REPO", 2u32, "2026-10-17T15:01Z", "main-thread", "11:01 main-thread fork: (3) apps/kino/spi/run_suite.ps1 = the Kino suite runner from the job-tmp lane-b script merged with the targeted-tests runner (-Label, -Tests a,b, -Dll, -Port, -TimeoutMin): repo-relative paths, MIX_BUILD_PATH and logs under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin (kino-builds/<label>, kino-suite/<label>.log), retires its daemon by TCP afterwards, and defers while a live Tibia client/server process runs (the old lane-specific arena flag file is gone). Smoke through the repo heavy gate: cell_test 12 tests, 0 failures. Moved so far: heavy.ps1, compare-runs.ps1, run_suite.ps1; still in job tmp: the pack scripts (spiral-bin\\packs), after-bless / determinism scripts, kino-tests.ps1 (superseded by run_suite -Tests).")
inl note_of_lab_in_repo_3 () = Note ("LAB-IN-REPO", 3u32, "2026-10-17T15:11Z", "main-thread", "11:11 main-thread fork: HANDOFF (lane paused): lab convention live in spiral/lab (7 item dirs, runner lab/try.ps1); main-lane tools moved: apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/heavy.ps1 + compare-runs.ps1, apps/kino/spi/run_suite.ps1. Next: lanes source the repo heavy.ps1 (shared FIFO queue), move the pack scripts, per-repo lab/ elsewhere.")
inl note_of_lab_in_repo_4 () = Note ("LAB-IN-REPO", 4u32, "2026-10-17T15:30Z", "main-thread", "11:30 main-thread: reconciled with the study lane. Canonical tools are apps/compiler/tmp/scripts/heavy.ps1 (gate + run ledger), scripts/compare-runs.ps1 and apps/kino/spi/run_suite.ps1, and the job-tmp heavy.ps1 is a one-line forwarder to scripts/heavy.ps1. I removed my duplicates from lab/harness-run-only-useful; it keeps only the experiment probes (rebuild/cross-folder determinism, bindiff.py, fsharp-resources, kino-tests). Patches live in lab/zig-hub-speed and lab/gadt-growing-index-hang.")
inl eoie_lab_audit () = Task ("EOIE-LAB-AUDIT", "eoie verb for lab lifecycle (eoie-first; one call): eoie lab audit <repo lab dir> <backlog root> lists every lab/<id> directory with its backlog item status and last note date, and flags: item missing (orphan lab), item Done (promote or delete now), no note for >14 days (stale), files ignored by .gitignore but present (run outputs outside .runs), absolute user-profile paths in sources (non-portable). Exit non-zero on orphan/Done so CI and the end-of-run check can gate on it. Written as granular Spiral in eoie, tests as Spiral test blocks. Consumer: LAB-IN-REPO.", Story, P1, D4, 10u32, Paused, "", "eoie,lab,process", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_lab_audit_1 () = Note ("EOIE-LAB-AUDIT", 1u32, "2026-10-17T14:48Z", "main-thread", "10:48 main-thread fork: TDD case written first (eoie/spec/one-call.md case 34, 'Audit lab directories against the backlog'): fixture with 3 lab dirs (one Paused item, one orphan, one Done item) and a source holding a C:\\Users nuget path; setup creates the 2 items with agile add/set as eoie do statements; command eoie do . 'lab audit lab against backlog'; exact expected table (lab, item, status, progress, verdict: keep / orphan: no backlog item OLD-PROBE / done: promote or delete), one non-portable line with file:line, receipt 'failed labs 3 keep 1 orphan 1 done 1 stale 0 non-portable 1'. Item id = lab dir name uppercased. Open in the spec: agile statements inside eoie do (the setup needs them) and the stale rule (last note older than 14 days at <clock>). Implementation is granular Spiral in eoie, not started (compiler-heavy builds wait for a free gate).")
inl note_of_eoie_lab_audit_2 () = Note ("EOIE-LAB-AUDIT", 2u32, "2026-10-17T15:11Z", "main-thread", "11:11 main-thread fork: HANDOFF (lane paused): spec case 34 in eoie/spec/one-call.md is the TDD target; implementation not started.")
inl note_of_eoie_lab_audit_3 () = Note ("EOIE-LAB-AUDIT", 3u32, "2026-10-17T22:21Z", "main-thread", "18:21 main-thread: spiral/lab was emptied 2026-10-17 (every folder promoted to lib/samples/scripts/lanes or deleted, with a note on its item; lab/ is now created on demand). eoie/spec/one-call.md lines ~919-943 still cite lab/convo-in-spiral paths as audit fixtures; refresh those expected outputs when the audit verb is built.")
inl spiral_realworld () = Task ("SPIRAL-REALWORLD", "RealWorld (Conduit) in Spiral, after leangineer.com/blog/realworld-in-lean (2026-10): Lean 4 app passes the official API suite (154/154) and website suite (74 run), 1,033 backend lines (TS 1,267, Go 1,371, C# 2,396) + 616 site, typed route table (handler types from routes, copied by hand from OpenAPI), proofs only for slug validity (~40 proof lines) and HTML escaping, Postgres + scrypt through C FFI, 1,170-1,650 req/s (bare Std.Http 1,800 vs Go >100k; 8,640 with an unreleased runtime fix). Assessment: Spiral can beat it NOW on speed (Rust/Zig/C residuals instead of the Lean runtime), on the spec gap (derive the route table and request/response types from the OpenAPI JSON at compile time - the reflection schema work in CONVO-IN-SPIRAL is the same machinery, run in reverse), and on escaping/SQL safety by construction (nominal SafeHtml/parameterized SQL types, typestate GADTs); it can NOT match the machine-checked theorems until the bottom-up CIC kernel is real (LEAN-KERNEL-NOTEBOOKS, 5%; its mega sample checks at compile time only), so the near-term path to proofs is the Lean backend: emit the domain (slug, escape) as Lean and prove it there, while the server runs native. Prerequisites: an HTTP server lib (lib/spiral networking has only a client), a Postgres client, JSON decode (CONVO-IN-SPIRAL), an HTML builder. Measure against the same suites and the article's numbers.", Epic, P3, D7, 10u32, Paused, "", "spiral,app,lean,prior-art", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_realworld_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-REALWORLD", 1u32, "2026-10-17T14:49Z", "main-thread", "10:49 main-thread fork: first step on the article's Proposition 1 gap (route table copied by hand): spiral/lab/spiral-realworld/route_real.spir checks AT COMPILE TIME that a route string names exactly the handler's parameter record (record_type_fold over the fields, each must appear as :name followed by / or the end, and the number of ':' segments must equal the field count; error_type otherwise). route-ok.spi ('/articles/:slug/comments/:id' with {slug : string; id : i32}) compiles, the route string is a constant; negative-route-missing-param, -extra-param and -wrong-name are compile errors naming the route. Learned: literal string ops fold during partial evaluation (!!!!StringLength/StringIndex/StringSlice; the slice end index is inclusive and bounds are checked statically). Next: generate the routes and parameter records from the RealWorld OpenAPI JSON instead of writing them (closes the gap fully), typed handlers per method, then the HTTP server lib question.")
inl note_of_spiral_realworld_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-REALWORLD", 2u32, "2026-10-17T14:56Z", "main-thread", "10:56 main-thread fork: spec gap closed for routes: lab/spiral-realworld/routes_gen.py reads the official RealWorld OpenAPI file (gothinkster/realworld specs/api/openapi.yml, 922 lines, kept outside the repo as data) and emits, per parameterized operation, a parameter record typed from the spec (e.g. DELETE /articles/{slug}/comments/{id} -> {slug : string; id : i32}) and a route_real.route check; generated module in the git-ignored lab/.runs/realworld-generated/routes.spi. Result: 19 operations over 12 paths, all 11 parameterized operations compile through the compile-time route checker (exit 240) - where the Lean app copied its route table by hand, here spec drift is a compile error. Next: request/response bodies from components/schemas through the CONVO schema/decoder machinery (decode_real + full_real), then the handler signatures.")
inl note_of_spiral_realworld_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-REALWORLD", 3u32, "2026-10-17T15:11Z", "main-thread", "11:11 main-thread fork: HANDOFF (lane paused): lab/spiral-realworld has route_real.spir (compile-time route/params check), route-ok + 3 negatives, routes_gen.py (OpenAPI -> routes; 11 parameterized operations checked). Open: request/response bodies from components/schemas via the CONVO schema/decoder, handler signatures, the HTTP server lib question.")
inl note_of_spiral_realworld_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-REALWORLD", 4u32, "2026-10-17T22:16Z", "main-thread", "18:16 main-thread lib move done (unstaged): the compile-time route checker is lib/spiral routes_real.spir + routes.spi (route path witness: the path must name exactly the handler record's fields as :name segments, else a compile error naming the route); test route_names_exactly_the_handler_fields on C and Rust, 1/1. Its three negatives became harness samples routes_negative_missing_param / _extra_param / _wrong_name that use lib through packageDir (no copies), ExpectedErrors match the route-specific message on 4 backends. routes_gen.py (OpenAPI yml -> parameter records) deleted: redo it in Spiral on the new lib json (decode the spec's JSON form into typed records, emit routes and records), which also covers the request/response bodies step. lab/spiral-realworld removed.")
inl ci_actions_lockfile () = Task ("CI-ACTIONS-LOCKFILE", "Adopt GitHub Actions workflow lockfiles (public preview 2026-10-14; .github/workflows/actions.lock via the gh actions-lock CLI extension; pins actions and reusable workflows by commit, transitively; check mode for CI; Dependabot regenerates entries in its update PRs; Require-lockfile policy at repo/org; available on Free/Team). Measured 2026-10-17: 0 of 84 uses: lines across our 8 workflow repos (spiral 17, alphabet 14, polyglot 12, dice 11, i574n.github 11, x 8, dir-tree-html 7, eoie 4) are SHA-pinned; third-party actions by tag include awalsh128/cache-apt-pkgs-action, erlef/setup-beam, mlugg/setup-zig, moonrepo/setup-rust, oven-sh/setup-bun, snok/install-poetry, wtfjoke/setup-tectonic and peaceiris/actions-gh-pages (deploys with a write token). Gain: supply-chain pinning without editing YAML plus reviewable bumps; no effect on the local reference run (i574n.github/scripts/workflow.ps1 runs steps natively, never resolves uses:). Plan: lock one repo first (i574n.github), add the check step, then the rest; dice and alphabet in the same change (mirror rule).", TaskKind, P2, D2, 75u32, Paused, "", "ci,security,workflow", "current", "current")
inl note_of_ci_actions_lockfile_1 () = Note ("CI-ACTIONS-LOCKFILE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T14:45Z", "main-thread", "10:45 main-thread fork: first repo done locally, UNCOMMITTED for review (i574n.github): installed gh extension github/gh-actions-lock (gh 2.83.2, logged in). gh actions-lock --no-fix (read-only) reported 11 unpinned actions in gh-pages.yml; gh actions-lock (fix mode) then (a) rewrote each floating major in the YAML to the exact current release (checkout v6 -> v6.1.0, setup-rust v1 -> v1.3.0, setup-beam v1 -> v1.24.1, setup-dotnet v5 -> v5.4.0, ... 11 lines) and (b) wrote .github/workflows/actions.lock (67 lines, version v0.0.3: per workflow the exact refs, per dependency ref + sha1 commit + owner_id + repo_id); --no-fix afterwards: All 1 workflow valid. Note the YAML rewrite: fix mode stops floating majors (bumps then come from Dependabot or a rerun), so review that before committing. Next: add 'gh actions-lock --no-fix' as a CI step (needs the extension in the runner), then the other 7 repos (dice + alphabet together); gh itself is from 2025-12, update per latest-of-everything.")
inl note_of_ci_actions_lockfile_2 () = Note ("CI-ACTIONS-LOCKFILE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T14:57Z", "main-thread", "10:57 main-thread fork: dice and alphabet locked together (mirror rule), UNCOMMITTED: fix mode rewrote 11 (dice) / 13 (alphabet) uses: lines to exact releases and wrote actions.lock (dice 67 lines, 10 locked dependencies; alphabet 98 lines, 15); --no-fix: All 1 workflow valid in both. The dependency counts differ because the two workflows already diverged before this change (dice 11 uses:, alphabet 14) - a pre-existing mirror gap to reconcile separately. Locked so far: i574n.github, dice, alphabet (3 of 8); left: spiral, polyglot, x, dir-tree-html, eoie.")
inl note_of_ci_actions_lockfile_3 () = Note ("CI-ACTIONS-LOCKFILE", 3u32, "2026-10-17T15:05Z", "main-thread", "11:05 main-thread fork: all 8 repos locked locally, UNCOMMITTED: spiral (10 locked deps, 17 uses: lines rewritten), polyglot (11, 12), x (6, 8), dir-tree-html (6, 7), eoie (4, 4), plus the earlier i574n.github (10, 11), dice (10, 11), alphabet (15, 13); gh actions-lock --no-fix = All 1 workflow valid in every repo. Each repo has .github/workflows/actions.lock (new) and its workflow YAML with exact release tags. Left: (1) review + commit per repo (user), (2) a CI step running gh actions-lock --no-fix so drift fails the run, (3) optionally the Require-lockfile repo setting, (4) update gh (2.83.2 is from 2025-12).")
inl note_of_ci_actions_lockfile_4 () = Note ("CI-ACTIONS-LOCKFILE", 4u32, "2026-10-17T15:11Z", "main-thread", "11:11 main-thread fork: HANDOFF (lane paused): all 8 repos locked locally and UNCOMMITTED (actions.lock + exact tags in each workflow); review and commit per repo, then the --no-fix CI step and gh update.")
inl pwsh_free () = Task ("PWSH-FREE", "Get rid of pwsh with a better product (user 2026-10-17): tracked PowerShell measured 2026-10-17 is ~10.9k non-blank lines in our repos (spiral 7,385: harness.psd1 3,405 of harness DATA, test.ps1 946, scripts/core.ps1 356, fast-tier 190, env 148, build 137, probe/bench/lint/gate/cost ~600, Kino runners ~250, lib/app build scripts ~400; eoie compiler-contracts 1,794; polyglot 678, dice 431, x 266, alphabet 212, i574n.github 75). A pwsh -NoProfile cold start costs ~500 ms per call vs ~20 ms for a native Spiral binary (eoie.exe), so the harness, the gate and the Kino runners pay that on every invocation. Rewrite them as granular Spiral (eoie-style one-call verbs where they belong), harness data as Spiral test blocks (SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS), keeping today's measured behaviour (native/compile memo, run ledger, reproducible builds) as tests. Children: SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR, KINO-HOT-PATHS, EOIE-SCRUM-XP", Epic, P1, D8, 0u32, Planned, "SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR,KINO-HOT-PATHS,EOIE-SCRUM-XP", "pwsh-free,dogfood,epic", "current", "current")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_1 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T17:47Z", "pwsh-inventory", "[1/11 inventory, 2026-10-17 13:45] git ls-files + non-blank counts: 160 tracked .ps1/.psd1 (0 .psm1) in 15 of our repos. spiral 46 files/7,385 lines; eoie 19/1,794; polyglot 23/678; dice 13/431; x 8/266; alphabet 8/212; i574n.github 5/75; dir-tree-html 4/52; legacy: tapas2 6/187, fc1943s.github 5/135, vault 4/110, tictactoe 10/59, singularity 4/37, futuresales 4/9, calc-parser 1/2. Not counted above: 29 pwsh cells in 3 .livemd run by core.ps1 Invoke-PwshNotebook (polyglot init 17, build 8, i574n.github workflow 4), untracked spiral/lab/*.ps1, and agent job-tmp scripts (most ledger rows today wrap one). Classes (non-blank lines): data tables 3,409 (harness.psd1 3,405); harness/test orchestration ~1,700 (test.ps1 950, fast-tier 190, zed test-at-row+test 154, cost-report 109, test-rust-exports 89, probe 83, bench-native 52, spiral-processes 48, compare-runs 22); eoie contracts/attestation 1,794; build/bootstrap/toolchain ~2,900 (core.ps1 356, env.ps1 149, lib.ps1 140, compiler build.ps1 137, apps/*/build ~290, per-repo init/build/outdated/publish); core-source tooling ~280 (pair-drift, projection-hash, hopacsota-lint, formatter/*); process supervision 157 (heavy.ps1, spiral-processes) + the mutex/worker code in test.ps1; Kino runners 157 + core.ps1 notebook helpers ~70; one-off benches ~380; CI: every workflow yml in 11 repos only runs pwsh scripts/workflow.ps1.")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_2 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T17:47Z", "pwsh-inventory", "[2/11 test.ps1 950] Loads harness.psd1, expands suites into (sample, backend) jobs, holds a Global named mutex per cache dir, builds a compile signature (sha256 of every compiler DLL + every .spi/.spir/.spiproj under bundle, lib and core package + SPIRAL_* env), replays compile-memo JSON, spreads jobs over ForEach-Object -Parallel workers that drive dotnet SpiralCompiler.dll --batch jobs.tsv results.tsv with deadline kill and the exit-4 warm-recycle retry protocol. Native tier: 17 backend runners (~360 lines of near-identical Invoke-Native + sha16-of-stdout branches), Kotlin warm JVM server, Swift 16-program chunk batches, Gleam one-project batch with failure attribution, native-memo keyed by an AST hash of its own functions, SetErrorMode 0x8003. Then oracle/baseline verdicts, results.tsv, summary, -Bless merge into EXPECTED.tsv, -Record. Primitives: named mutex; spawn with timeout + tree kill + capture + PATH prefix; throttled parallel lanes; sha256 of files and normalized text; TSV I/O; JSON memo store (40,079 compile-memo + 11,389 native-memo files today); toolchain table. Covered: proxy run/command-capture, eoie_process_streaming Job Object kill, batch-plan parallel chains, proxy hash. Missing: mutex, TSV, memo store, toolchain table. Estimate ~2,000-2,500 Spiral lines in ~6 modules (jobs, compile driver, backend runner table as a union, memo, verdicts, report), each under the 1,000-line gate; the self-AST native signature becomes an explicit runner version.")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_3 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 3u32, "2026-10-17T17:47Z", "pwsh-inventory", "[3/11 fast-tier, compare, cost, probe] fast-tier.ps1 (190) mines every <cache>/runs/<mode>-*/results.tsv: today 1,841 run dirs (1,096 single-flight), latest results.tsv 6,916 rows/9.4 MB, so one -Suite fast selection parses up to ~7M TSV rows in PowerShell before the first compile, then runs 4 budget passes (cheapest per backend/family, young rows, flip histories, signal per second). That, not the 480 ms start, is the largest per-call cost in the harness and the natural first native verb: TSV in, TSV out, deterministic, testable byte for byte against the pwsh output. compare-runs (22, run diff), cost-report (109, ledger + medians), bench-native (52), spiral-processes (48, list/stop compiler daemons and workers by command line) are agent tools: each one verb over the same results-row + process-list primitives (~80-200 Spiral lines each). probe.ps1 (83) needs spawn with timeout, dotnet-stack/trace attach and per-sample log dirs: port after the compile driver. Estimate: fast-tier ~350 Spiral lines (row history, median, passes as a union of pass kinds) on a shared results-row module also used by compare, verdicts and bless.")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_4 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 4u32, "2026-10-17T17:47Z", "pwsh-inventory", "[4/11 harness.psd1 -> test blocks] ~3,150 of its 3,405 lines are backend membership lists, an inverted index backend -> samples: Zig ~455, Wasm ~442, Lua ~440, Bend ~402, Swift ~351, Kotlin ~305, Backends map ~247 (backend set -> samples), Lean ~169, Gleam ~162, AlsoTypeScript ~113, Ebpf ~53, Wgsl ~51, OpenQasm ~32, Calyx ~29. The rest are per-sample attributes: ExpectedErrors 36 (diagnostic substring), Timeouts 22, CFlags 21 (glob -> gcc flags / shim include), Known 14 (backend, optional oracle scope, reason), plus Smoke 15, Mega 5, Fast policy 4 numbers. In the SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS shape each becomes source: the block target line names its backends (replaces every membership list and AlsoTypeScript), expected exit/stdout inline (replaces cache-only EXPECTED.tsv for converted families), expected diagnostic, timeout, c flags/shim and known divergence with oracle scope as block attributes. Only Smoke/Mega/Fast stay, as a ~40-line Spiral policy record. Convert family by family (ebpf_* first: 31 samples, 166 rows), the harness reads both sources until a list is empty, delete each list at zero. Do not write a psd1 reader for the native harness (Import-PowerShellDataFile is the only parser today): migrate the data, or dump it once to TSV as an interim.")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_5 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 5u32, "2026-10-17T17:48Z", "pwsh-inventory", "[5/11 heavy gate + Kino runners] heavy.ps1 (109): Invoke-Heavy = two Global named mutex slots (spiral-heavy-job, -2), FIFO ticket files <ticks>-<pid>.t in %LOCALAPPDATA%/spiral-bin/heavy-queue with dead-pid pruning, slot 2 only above 3,072 MB free, WSL tickets with a 45-min starvation prefix, BelowNormal priority, wsl --shutdown when idle, one runs.tsv ledger row; Invoke-Timed = ledger only. Ledger today 07:02-13:15: 77 rows (51 heavy, 19 timed, 6 notebook, 1 kino-suite), wrapped runs 5-1,832 s, so the 0.5 s start is noise there: the win is one native gate binary every caller (pwsh, mix, eoie, Zed) can use. Keep mutex names, ticket format and ledger columns so pwsh and native callers coexist during the switch. run_notebook.ps1 (115): rebases path args, tries the warm daemon on TCP 13905 (lines: notebook request, then line/error/end), else resolves dotnet + compiler (may build it), mix deps, mix spiral.notebook, ledger row. The warm route is the hot path: pwsh start + heavy.ps1 dot-source to send one request. lib/spiral/build.ps1 runs 38 notebooks this way; zed test-at-row starts pwsh twice per click (itself + run_notebook) plus a dotnet block-rows call. run_suite.ps1 (42): MIX_BUILD_PATH per label, env, mix test with timeout + taskkill /T, daemon retire, ledger. No eoie verb covers these yet. Estimate: gate ~300 Spiral lines, notebook client ~200, suite runner ~150.")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_6 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 6u32, "2026-10-17T17:48Z", "pwsh-inventory", "[6/11 build, bootstrap, toolchain] env.ps1 (149) is the hub everyone dot-sources (test, build, Kino runners, lib.ps1, eoie spiral.ps1/workspace.ps1/contracts, x sim): cache dir, mode names, core projection (#if pair stripper), Resolve-SpiralDotnet (spawns dotnet --list-sdks per call), a 19-tool native toolchain table with env overrides and scoop/current fallbacks, compiler DLL path, package dir, normalized text sha256. Compiler build.ps1 (137): pair-drift + hopacsota-lint gates, core staged by sha16, SDK chosen by what the system dotnet can run (temp global.json), PathMap, crossgen2 ReadyToRun with skip stamps, unlock-by-rename, builds.tsv receipt. lib.ps1 (140): BuildSpiral (compile with timeout, output-freshness check), PublishFsharp (fsproj generation). core.ps1 (356): Invoke-Block (retries, env scoping, WSL), symlink helpers, Invoke-Notebook, Invoke-PwshNotebook; shared by polyglot/dice/alphabet/tapas2/vault/dir-tree-html through polyglot/scripts/core.ps1 -> spiral/scripts/core.ps1. Per-repo init (clone siblings, rustup, symlinks), build (notebook export + BuildSpiral + cargo), outdated, publish are 5-40 line glue. Covered: proxy toolchain/portable-toolchain, fs-symlink, fs-copy-tree, binary-install, run; lib runtime execute_with_options. Estimate: env + toolchain table ~400 Spiral lines (one record per tool), compiler build driver ~300, BuildSpiral/app builds ~300; per-repo glue becomes a repo manifest read by one workflow verb.")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_7 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 7u32, "2026-10-17T17:48Z", "pwsh-inventory", "[7/11 eoie compiler-contracts 1,794] eoie already has the native driver: build.ps1 (39) only cargo-builds eoie-dev, runs it, then Publish-EoieBinary (stage, schema + topology smoke with timeout, hash re-checks, atomic move), which proxy binary-install already models. workspace.ps1 (222, shared): bundle resolve, compiler snapshot with hash re-verification, sidecar guards, atomic generated-file write, Get-EoieOwners (cargo metadata -> [package.metadata.spiral] owners). test-regeneration (225) copies the workspace and compiles each owner in its own dotnet process, sequentially, not --batch: porting it to one --batch process also removes a .NET start per owner. renew-cold-rebuild (165) wraps the existing cold-rebuild-matrix verb; renew-strict-evidence (107), test-native-coverage (185), test-native-probes (94), test-source-package (105), test-strict-preflight (75), test-attestation (129, rustc fixtures), test-binary-publication (96, publisher mutation tests), test-dev-workflow (195, fixture workspaces with spaces), test-selected-build (17, cargo test); scripts/workflow.ps1 is the CI order. Primitives: cargo metadata JSON, spawn with timeout, sha256 snapshot, atomic replace, temp fixtures; nearly all exist as proxy verbs or in eoie_dev. Contracts become Spiral test blocks (eoie_dev_spiral_tests style). Estimate ~1,500 Spiral lines in ~5 packages; dev.ps1, eoie.ps1, spiral.ps1 (93) become eoie-dev subcommands.")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_8 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 8u32, "2026-10-17T17:48Z", "pwsh-inventory", "[8/11 15 largest files] harness.psd1 3,405 -> test-block attributes, then delete (note 4). test.ps1 950 -> native harness verb ~2,200 Spiral (note 2). core.ps1 356 -> Get-LastSortedItem and Update-Json have no callers (delete); Invoke-Block becomes the workflow step runner; symlinks -> proxy fs-symlink; Invoke-PwshNotebook goes with the 29 livemd pwsh cells; ~250. test-regeneration 225 + workspace.ps1 222 -> eoie-dev regenerate with batch compile ~500. lib/spiral/bench/parsing/bench.ps1 214 -> one-off bench: record its numbers, then a Spiral bench block. test-dev-workflow 195, test-native-coverage 185, renew-cold-rebuild 165 -> eoie test blocks over existing verbs ~600. fast-tier 190 -> first port ~350. env.ps1 149 -> workspace toolchain module ~400. lib.ps1 140 -> BuildSpiral as a spiral CLI verb ~150; PublishFsharp leaves with the no-Fable goal. compiler build.ps1 137 -> build verb ~300 that keeps the dotnet/crossgen calls identical so builds stay byte-reproducible (PathMap, obj-shared). apps/spiral/build.ps1 136 -> CLI self-check as test blocks (help, export round trip vs the previous CLI, rust/cuda/lua/gleam cases) ~250. apps/zed/tasks/test-at-row.ps1 133 -> spiral-zed/CLI subcommand (block rows, reach, impacted tests) talking to the warm daemon directly, saving 2 pwsh starts per click ~250. Total ~5,500 Spiral lines for ~6,800 pwsh lines, 3,405 of them data that moves into sources.")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_9 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 9u32, "2026-10-17T17:48Z", "pwsh-inventory", "[9/11 hidden pwsh + primitive gaps] Outside .ps1: lib/spiral/crypto.spi get_file_hash' (Rust side) shells out to pwsh Get-FileHash although sha2 is already in its deps; lib/spiral/runtime.spi test blocks use pwsh -c as the child process (ReadAllText, a 5-line writer), so lib tests need pwsh; apps/spiral/spiral.spi process_rust/gleam/cuda build pwsh -c strings only for error traces (not run), but the wasm contract path really runs pwsh scripts/invoke-block.ps1 -Linux; Kino targets.ex has a pwsh cell kernel; .zed/tasks.json starts test-at-row through pwsh. WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB status: present: spawn + timeout + Job Object tree kill (eoie_process_streaming/windows.rs), run/command-capture/batch-plan with parallel chains, hash/hash-tree, fs-*, symlink, toolchain, binary-install; workspace_primitives: outcome, numbers, bytes, clock, console, files, rooted. Missing (0 hits in eoie/src and lib/spiral): named OS mutex (Global\\ on Windows, flock on Linux), header-indexed TSV codec, JSON memo store (file per key, atomic), normalized text sha256 (Get-TextSha256 defines residual identity in EXPECTED.tsv: must match bit for bit), toolchain table resolving scoop .shim path= lines and current junctions, free-memory query, process priority, git read (ls-remote url, ls-files), SetErrorMode for native children, line-protocol TCP client (Kino daemon).")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_10 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 10u32, "2026-10-17T17:48Z", "pwsh-inventory", "[11/11 risks] Bootstrap: a Spiral tool needs dotnet 11 SDK + SpiralCompiler build + The-Spiral-Language core clone + rustup/cargo. Generated Rust is committed (eoie src, apps/spiral/spiral.rs), so cargo alone builds the tools: keep the chain cargo build -> native tool -> (it installs dotnet, builds the compiler), never the reverse. On Windows install-dotnet.ps1 runs Microsoft dotnet-install.ps1, so download the SDK zip directly or keep that one pwsh call. Windows specifics: Global\\ named mutexes are machine-wide on Windows but file-backed and per-user on Linux; Job Objects are Windows-only (Linux needs process groups/killpg); SetErrorMode 0x8003 stops abort() waiting on WerFault (else 30 s false timeouts); scoop shims and current junctions; Swift batch links with /STACK; paths with spaces and non-ASCII (test-binary-publication fixture). CI: windows-2025 and ubuntu-24.04 runners ship pwsh and only run workflow.ps1, so CI is the last place it matters. Keep byte-identical: Get-TextSha256 residual hashes (EXPECTED.tsv), memo keys (a key change only replays nothing; record it once), TSV quoting (-UseQuotes Never), ledger columns other agents read. Coexistence: mix, Zed, agents and job-tmp scripts call these .ps1 paths today, so each path stays a one-line forwarder until its callers move. Measurement gap: no per-call timing of pwsh start vs script body was taken here (CPU-light lane); measure before ranking steps 2-3 against step 1.")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_11 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 11u32, "2026-10-17T17:48Z", "pwsh-inventory", "[10/11 migration order] 0 primitives (WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB): normalized text sha256, TSV codec, named mutex, memo store, toolchain table, free memory, each with Spiral test blocks pinning todays outputs. 1 fast-tier verb (pure TSV, ~7M rows parsed per fast run) with compare-runs on the same results-row module; test.ps1 calls the binary. 2 Kino warm notebook client + zed test-at-row as native commands (2 pwsh starts per Zed click, 38 per lib build; 4-kind line protocol). 3 heavy gate as a native wrapper with the same mutex names, ticket files and ledger columns; heavy.ps1 becomes a forwarder, then goes. 4 harness: native compile driver (batch workers, memo, warm-recycle) behind test.ps1 first, then the native tier as one backend-runner table, verdicts/bless last; each step must reproduce a run row for row (verdicts, residual hashes) before its pwsh part is deleted. Meanwhile SAMPLES-TO-TEST-BLOCKS empties harness.psd1 family by family. 5 eoie: test-regeneration -> eoie-dev regenerate with --batch, contracts -> test blocks, Publish-EoieBinary -> binary-install. 6 env + compiler build, then per-repo init/build/workflow as data for one workflow verb; CI yml lines change last. Delete now or soon: core.ps1 Get-LastSortedItem/Update-Json (no callers), the pwsh call in crypto get_file_hash' (use sha2), PublishFsharp (no-Fable goal), tictactoe/singularity/futuresales/calc-parser scripts (~107 lines) if those repos are retired.")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_12 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 12u32, "2026-10-17T17:50Z", "main-thread", "13:50 main-thread: absorbed the pwsh-inventory lane. Done now: memo stores no longer grow without bound. test.ps1 deletes compile-memo entries unused for 2 days (176 MB / 53k files after one day: each compiler build adds ~13k) and native-memo entries unused for 14 days (1 MB), and replays refresh their timestamps; the edits sit outside the AST-hashed native region, so the native memo stays valid. Queued facts for the migration: fast-tier scans ~1,096 run folders (~7M TSV rows) per -Suite fast run, the biggest per-call cost and the natural first native verb; the Kino warm-notebook path starts pwsh just to send one TCP request (38 times per lib/spiral build); lib/spiral/crypto.spi get_file_hash' shells out to pwsh even though sha2 is a dependency; and harness.psd1 is ~3,150 lines of per-backend sample lists that become test-block attributes (don't write a psd1 reader).")
inl note_of_pwsh_free_13 () = Note ("PWSH-FREE", 13u32, "2026-10-17T19:11Z", "styles-survey", "15:13 styles-survey: CLI argument style for the pwsh replacements is now CLI-ARGS-STYLE (notes 1-3): clap is used only by apps/spiral, apps/wasm and alphabet documents via lib/spiral/runtime.spi (~310 lines, builder API, stringly ids); eoie and x hand-roll. Recommendation (b): one lib parser derived from unions (subcommands) and records (operands) by a compile-time reflection fold, generating usage/help/schema/errors; ~112 of the 124 tracked .ps1 in our 7 main repos have a param() block, so each becomes a typed record instead of new parsing code.")
inl spiral_cli_granular () = Task ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", "Dogfood: rewrite the spiral/apps/spiral CLI under the 1,000-line generated-crate gate. Today it is one 1,918-line spiral.spi that generates one 24,280-line spiral.rs (24x the gate). Target: granular, domain-modeled Spiral modules, each generating a crate of at most 1k lines, with the CLI composed from them; it is the first pwsh replacement host (PWSH-FREE)", Story, P1, D7, 0u32, Planned, "", "pwsh-free,dogfood,cli", "current", "current")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_1 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 1u32, "2026-10-17T17:46Z", "cli-design", "13:46 cli-design: 1/9 map: apps/spiral/spiral.spi (1,918 lines) = 6 clap subcommands; each prints a JSON envelope {command_result: json(extension, code, code_path, output)} on stdout when SPIRAL_JSON=1. rust (--rs-path, --deps*, --wasm=, --contract=, --cleanup): writes target/spiral/<ws>/packages/Rust/<sha>/Cargo.toml + spiral.rs, cargo fmt with missing-manifest repair, cargo run, or a nightly wasm build then pwsh scripts/invoke-block.ps1 -Linux spiral_wasm. cuda (--py-path, --env Pip|Poetry, --deps): pip/poetry install, python. cpp: g++ -std=c++20, run. lua: in-process mlua, print/io.write capture. gleam (--target Erlang|JavaScript): gleam.toml, gleam run, or esbuild + trunk + index.html. export, alias dib-export (PATH EXT pairs): parses .cells/.dib magic blocks with parsing combinators, writes .spi/.spir/.fs/.md, async par_map. Dead code: persist_code_project/publish_* (fsproj, dotnet publish). Types: package_target, rust_runtime Wasm|Contract, export_output Fs|Md|Spi|Spir, magic, block, gleam_target, cuda_env, lua nominals. Spiral deps: lib/spiral core + spiral (file_system, runtime, parsing, sm', am', listm, mapm, crypto, date_time, env, reflection, async, trace, console). Cargo deps: mimalloc chrono regex sha2 serde_json clap futures encoding_rs(_io) async-walkdir mlua (vendored lua51). Callers: Kino builders.ex argv (rust/cuda/cpp/gleam-js/lua), notebook.ex dib-export PATH KIND, build.ps1 run check, test_json_protocol.py.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_2 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 2u32, "2026-10-17T17:46Z", "cli-design", "13:46 cli-design: 2/9 where the 24,280 lines come from (spiral.rs sampled by fn spans): ~9,000 = runtime.execute_with_options, which re-parses the command STRING with lib parsing combinators: split_args ~5,000 (split_args_86 + method96 + method103, ~1,650 each: the p_segment alternatives are inlined 3x for the many1/sep_by continuations), split_command ~780 + closures, on_stdout/on_stderr ~430, execute body ~450, stdio/encoding helpers. ~3,850 = export parser (magic_command/content/blocks via p_string/many_chars_till/sep_end_by, + format_block/indent_block), +1,155 export loop (loop_144: trace + union_to_string inlined). ~3,380 = run_15: every process_* is inl, so all five runners are inlined into one dispatch fn. ~1,170 = 65 monomorphised unions/structs (each option/result payload is a new USn enum, ~18 lines). ~3,100 = trace state, clap get_command (290), file_system (read_link, find_parent, get_full_path ~115, create_dir ~200, normalize_path), sha2 hash. trace: 41 inline sites, 248 spiral_trace_hold calls, ~50-150 lines each including reflection.union_to_string (a nested Option chain, ~16 lines per union case per call site). Ratio 12.7x rs/spi, vs eoie ~4x (task_listing 189 spi -> 742 rs).")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_3 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 3u32, "2026-10-17T17:46Z", "cli-design", "13:46 cli-design: 3/9 eoie conventions that keep crates under 1k: one Cargo crate per dir with [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output, built by the same dotnet compiler (--batch in eoie spiral.ps1). package.spiproj packages = SOURCE-only Spiral packages inlined into each crate (workspace_primitives, rust_std_string/fs/env: one-line $'...' prims with ~dyn args; no lib/spiral sm'/file_system/runtime/parsing/trace). Cross-crate: !!!!Export(name, (fun ... : sig)) in main -> pub fn name (string params become &str, returns Rc<str>; i32/u64/bool prims; tuple params flatten; no usize/records/unions/lists). The consumer calls it through an inl $'crate::fn(&*!x)' : t wrapper plus a Cargo path dep. Verbs export () -> i32 entries that read std::env::args themselves (eoie_agile_list_run); the router matches argv[1]. let = emitted once (join point), inl = inlined; recursion is let rec, not combinators. Gate: eoie_source_topology topology.rs limit=1000 for the largest .rs file AND per crate dir (.rs + .spi counted, see CRATE-GATE-HARDEN); x/scripts/gate.ps1 counts generated .rs per package.metadata.spiral crate (report-only, -Enforce). Size follows what packages reach: planning_fields.spi is 26 lines and generates 762 rs. Do not copy: eoie_cli routing_terminal / eoie_process rust_global blobs (that era is rust_global, not the target).")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_4 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 4u32, "2026-10-17T17:46Z", "cli-design", "13:46 cli-design: 4/9 proposed split, shared source packages (no crate, inlined). Home = a lib/spiral workspace layer: the WORKSPACE-PRIMITIVES-LIB slice that eoie later repoints its packageDir to, since spiral must not depend on eoie. cli_args: typed reader over std::env::args (flag_value --rs-path, flag_values --deps repeatable, flag_equals --wasm= with an empty default, flag_present --cleanup; union arg_problem Missing|Invalid; ~60-100 rs per crate). command_report: type report = extension code code_path output; union outcome Reported : report | Failed : diagnostic; JSON envelope with sorted keys code, code_path, extension, output and hand escaping; stdout/stderr + exit code; ~100. process_run: type process_request = program, args list, env pairs, cwd; one let run_process over std::process::Command with an argv vector, merged line capture, elapsed, optional timeout budget. It replaces execute_with_options and with it split_command/split_args entirely (~9k -> ~150 per crate). dep_spec: name | name=ver | name[features] -> toml line, shared by rust/gleam/cuda, ~40. text/fs/env prims in the eoie rust_std_* style. Rule for all of them: no lib/spiral trace, reflection.union_to_string, sm' record formatting, async or parsing; name unions with explicit let functions.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_5 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 5u32, "2026-10-17T17:46Z", "cli-design", "13:46 cli-design: 5/9 proposed crates (apps/spiral/<crate>/, workspace members; estimated generated rs each): spiral_cli bin router: argv[1] match, usage text, dib-export alias, calls the exported entries (~200). cuda_runner: Pip|Poetry manifest, install, python, report (~300). cpp_runner: g++/SPIRAL_CXX build + run (~200). lua_runner: the only mlua dep, print/io.write capture (~300). gleam_runner Erlang: gleam.toml, owned src vs gleam_package, gleam build + run, output line filter (~400). gleam_js_bundle JavaScript: build, #app_ hash patch, bunx esbuild, index.html, trunk (~350). rust_manifest, pure text: workspace Cargo.toml (resolver 2|3, profiles), package Cargo.toml bin|cdylib, near main_state, near-sdk/indexmap defaults (~300, exports &str -> Rc<str>). rust_runner: sha2 package dir, write, cargo fmt + missing-manifest repair (str find, no regex), cargo run, output after the profile line, cleanup, exit 1 on error (~700). rust_wasm_runner: nightly wasm build + spiral_wasm called directly, no pwsh invoke-block (~250). notebook_export: line scanner over #! magic lines (no combinators) + Spi/Spir/Md rendering, sequential (~600). notebook_export_fs: F# module/namespace indent_block, or drop it after a caller grep (bold-native: no F#) (~450). Total ~4k vs 24k. Cargo deps left: sha2, mlua, mimalloc (chrono, regex, clap, futures, serde_json, encoding_rs, async-walkdir dropped).")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_6 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 6u32, "2026-10-17T17:46Z", "cli-design", "13:46 cli-design: 6/9 interfaces: each verb crate exports one () -> i32 entry (spiral_cuda_run, spiral_rust_run, spiral_export_run ...) that reads its own argv tail via cli_args, prints the envelope via command_report and returns the exit code. Only prims cross the ABI; domain types (report, cuda_env, gleam_target, export_output, rust_runtime) stay crate-local. Pure helpers another crate needs (rust_manifest) export &str -> Rc<str> renderers. Router side: inl cuda_run () : i32 = $'cuda_runner::spiral_cuda_run()' : i32, plus a Cargo path dep. Build: each crate Cargo.toml carries [package.metadata.spiral] entry/output; one compiler --batch run over all crates (the X-REPO-TEMPLATE build loop), then cargo build -p spiral. Gate: the x gate.ps1 shape for spiral (CRATE-GATE-HARDEN decides .rs-only vs .rs+.spi). Migration keeps ONE entry (no _native wrapper), strangler inside the monolith: spiral.spi keeps clap for help/parsing and routes an extracted verb to its crate, so spiral.rs shrinks with each slice and the build.ps1 run check keeps working. When the last runner leaves, clap/trace/execute_with_options go with it and spiral.spi becomes the ~200-line router.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_7 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 7u32, "2026-10-17T17:46Z", "cli-design", "13:46 cli-design: 7/9 first slice: the cuda verb = cuda_runner crate + the cli_args, command_report and process_run packages, routed from the monolith. Why cuda: it is the smallest verb that has every concern (repeated flags, an enum flag, a deps manifest, a child process, the JSON envelope, failure exit), and it already has a non-pwsh acceptance test: test_json_protocol.py (one JSON document at every TRACE_LEVEL; a child exception gives a non-zero exit without command_result; a CUDA driver failure is not reported as success), plus the build.ps1 cuda check (spiral-check-cuda 42). It needs only python, no compiler or GPU. By contrast cpp needs g++, lua needs a vendored mlua build, rust needs cargo inside the test. Success = cuda_runner.rs under 1k, spiral.rs shrinks, the protocol and run checks pass, Kino cuda cells unchanged. Then: 2 cpp + lua; 3 notebook_export (largest independent win, ~5k lines; build.ps1 already diffs the export of every notebook against the previous CLI byte for byte); 4 gleam erlang + js; 5 rust_manifest + rust_runner; 6 rust_wasm_runner (WSL/Linux contract path, tested last per the no-WSL rule); 7 the router replaces clap and the dead fsproj code goes.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_8 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 8u32, "2026-10-17T17:46Z", "cli-design", "13:46 cli-design: 8/9 risks: (a) ABI: !!!!Export maps only Spiral prims (no usize, records, unions or lists; strings &str in, Rc<str> out), so multi-value results need () -> i32 entries or text receipts. (b) Every crate re-emits its own copies of shared package types/helpers (~100-200 lines overhead each). (c) Explosion sources to block: inl functions with loops or several call sites must be let (join point); parser combinators duplicate continuation bodies (p_segment 3x = ~5k); literal specialisation, so use ~dyn args; reflection.union_to_string and #?{} record formatting expand per call site; trace inlines ~100 lines per site. (d) Behaviour to pin with tests: envelope key order/escaping (today serde_json arbitrary_precision BTreeMap); rust output = merged stream after the cargo profile line (+2); exit codes (rust 1, cuda/cpp failwith panic 101); --cleanup is SetFalse (flag present = keep files); the --help Usage: text; export PATH EXT pairs; the sha of a debug-formatted record names the package dir (changing it only costs cache misses). (e) pwsh/WSL is still inside the wasm/contract path. (f) Compile cost: 11 crates = 11 compiler jobs, so use --batch; the vendored mlua build stays isolated. (g) Parallel export moves from futures par_map to sequential (or std::thread::scope); measure it.")
inl note_of_spiral_cli_granular_9 () = Note ("SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR", 9u32, "2026-10-17T17:46Z", "cli-design", "13:46 cli-design: 9/9 tests: Spiral test blocks only (///> rust, test name =), no tests/*.rs. Per verb crate: a fixture in a temp dir, run the real built CLI as a child, check shows/hides on stdout/stderr (the eoie todo_cli.spi pattern). cuda ports the 4 test_json_protocol.py cases (then the .py is retired); cpp/lua/gleam/rust port the build.ps1 run-check cases (spiral-check-* 42); export ports the help/check.cells case (exact /// # check + inl main) plus a byte-identity test against a committed expected .spi for a few notebooks. Pure packages (cli_args, dep_spec, command_report JSON escaping, rust_manifest text) get unit test blocks with exact expected output. Open: which runner executes Spiral test blocks in the spiral repo (eoie has eoie_dev_spiral_tests), and whether test blocks inside a crate .spi count against the gate (eoie counts .spi; if so, keep tests in a sibling test package). Not verified here: no build or compile was run (CPU-light lane); every line estimate comes from spiral.rs fn spans and eoie ratios.")
inl kino_hot_paths () = Task ("KINO-HOT-PATHS", "Implement Kino's performance-sensitive parts in Spiral (PWSH-FREE, KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR): measure first. Today's numbers: per-cell time is dominated by external tools (gleam build ~2-9 s, dotnet fsi ~5 s floor, rustc); Kino's own overhead shows in warm runs (38 tests, 38 memo hits, 7-9 s = ~200 ms per test: memo key hashing (sha256 of compiler binaries, 20-40 ms per context), the compiler-daemon protocol, notebook parse/mounts). Profile a warm run, move the measured hot paths (memo keys, document/notebook parsing beyond the existing Spiral domain, runner/targets dispatch) into Spiral, and keep Elixir only as glue until KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR removes it", Story, P1, D6, 0u32, Planned, "", "pwsh-free,kino,perf", "current", "current")
inl eoie_scrum_xp () = Task ("EOIE-SCRUM-XP", "eoie agile beyond Kanban-style flow (user 2026-10-17: could we also be scrum or xp?). It has Epic|Feature|Story|TaskKind, priority/difficulty, deps, estimate (minutes), load, assignee agent|pair|user, leases/handoff and plan; missing: time-boxed sprints/iterations (start/end, committed items, a sprint goal), velocity from completed estimates, an explicit epic->child relation (today deps/tags), sprint review/retro records, and XP practices as data (pairing already exists as assignee=pair; TDD is the EOIE-ONE-CALL-SPEC direction; small releases = receipts). Each as a one-call verb with Spiral test blocks", Story, P2, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,agile", "current", "current")
inl eoie_memory_links () = Task ("EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS", "One-call memory/backlog link check (user 2026-10-17: harden the memory+eoie symbiosis): eoie agile links <backlog root> --memory <dir> reports every backlog ID cited in memory files (frontmatter + body), dangling IDs, project/feedback memories citing Done items whose rule may have changed, and backlog items whose notes cite a missing memory slug; --json for agents. Done by hand on 2026-10-17 with a pwsh one-off: 55 memories, 0 dangling, ~12 Done references (mostly historical lessons). Spiral test blocks with fixture memory dirs.", Story, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION", "eoie,agile,memory", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_links_1 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:33Z", "main-thread", "14:33 main-thread: TODO-AS-SOURCE first slice. This item's code-local TODO block is at eoie/src/eoie_agile_mutation/agile_mutation.spi:140 (above agile_entry, with the dispatch arm to add); eoie agile todos harvests it with id EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS.")
inl eoie_memory () = Task ("EOIE-MEMORY", "eoie embodies the agents' memory (user 2026-10-17: the name comes from embodiment; absorb ~/.claude/projects/*/memory and the Codex, Grok, Zed, OpenCode and Antigravity equivalents in a typed way, keep only a bootstrap in each client pointing to the eoie tutorial, sync through eoie in git instead of zipping the profile). Typed model in Spiral, natively what Rust fakes with PhantomData: a memory item is a GADT indexed by kind (user | feedback | project | reference), and feedback and project require Why and HowToApply at the type level; the heterogeneous store is an existential pack; client adapters are an HKT-style interface (one per client format); per-kind prototypes give defaults; reflection derives the renderers (markdown frontmatter, JSON, client bootstraps) and the schema, like the Convo tool-schema reflection the study lane built. Found on this laptop 2026-10-17: Claude .claude/projects/C--home-git/memory (56 typed md files plus the MEMORY.md index); Zed AppData/Roaming/Zed/AGENTS.md (rules); Antigravity .gemini/antigravity (brain/, annotations/); OpenCode .local/share/opencode/opencode.db (read a copy; never the credential/account tables); Codex only config.toml (no memory store yet); Grok .grok (no memory store found). Children: EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL, EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS, EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS, EOIE-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP, EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS", Epic, P1, D8, 0u32, Planned, "EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL,EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS,EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS,EOIE-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP,EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS", "eoie,memory,epic,gadt", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_1 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:19Z", "main-thread", "14:19 main-thread: absorbed the memory-formats lane (9 notes on EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS). New fact for the epic: Grok has a memory store, memory-v2 (topics/*.md: 11 curated topics; observations/_inbox: 19 notes with provenance frontmatter; generated MEMORY.md, never write it), and it also loads ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md through its Claude compatibility setting. Zed, OpenCode, Antigravity and Codex have rules/instructions but no memory store. Claude: 44 feedback, 8 project, 4 reference; every feedback/project item has Why and How; a byte-identical render needs quote style and body segments stored. Privacy: exclude ~/.config/opencode/service.json (plaintext password) and every auth/credential file by name; transcripts (threads, brain, sessions) are never imported. Related DeltaDB idea (Zed, 2026): history that links changes to the agent conversations behind them.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_2 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:23Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:23 from the bundles (tritcritnet alpha716 docs\\vision\\readme_eoie.md Feb 2026; core alpha9237 readmes Jun; samples alpha705; BUNDLE-WISDOM note 10): the old vision put memory in receipts, not in the model ('EOIE is the body that makes the brain leave fingerprints'). Concrete pieces missing from the memory epic: a resume compiler that builds the next-turn agenda from the last ledger (passed -> skip/cheap verify, failed dep -> block downstream, expected failure -> tolerated, stale report -> regenerate, partial patch -> repair plan); an incident-resume summary in one call (active lease, newest savepoint, files changed since, newest receipts, toolchain identity); a claim boundary per report (passed/blocked/synthetic/proxy/tolerated); reports projected from one typed receipt so prose cannot overwrite a metric.")
inl eoie_memory_model () = Task ("EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL", "Typed memory model + lossless import of Claude's 56 memories: GADT memory<kind>, existential store, reflection-derived markdown renderer; stored as Spiral data in the eoie repo (like state/agile.spi). Proof: import, then render back, must be byte-identical to every source file (frontmatter, body, [[links]]); links resolved and dangling ones reported; Spiral test blocks only", Story, P1, D6, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,memory,gadt", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_model_1 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-MODEL", 1u32, "2026-10-17T17:44Z", "main-thread", "13:44 main-thread design sketch (not started; needs the user's go): kinds are nominals: nominal user = (), nominal feedback = (), nominal project = (), nominal reference = (). The item is a GADT: union memory kind = | User :: {slug; description; body} -> memory user | Feedback :: {slug; description; rule; why; how} -> memory feedback | Project :: {slug; description; fact; why; how} -> memory project | Reference :: {slug; description; pointer} -> memory reference, so a feedback without why/how doesn't typecheck. The store is existential: union stored = Stored :: forall k. memory k -> stored, a list or map by slug. Links: [[slug]] parsed to a typed link list resolved against the store; dangling links and backlog IDs reported by check. Prototypes: per-kind defaults by typecase on the index. Reflection: renderers iterate a record's keys at compile time (markdown frontmatter name/description/metadata.type, JSON), the same technique as the Convo tool schemas (lab/convo-in-spiral). Adapters (HKT): a client is a type-level function f with import : path -> f stored and render : stored -> f text. Round-trip proof: the 56 Claude files plus MEMORY.md imported and rendered back byte-identical. Prerequisite checked today: recursive GADTs with grown indices compile since GADT-GROWING-INDEX-HANG's fix (blessed 111908).")
inl eoie_memory_verbs () = Task ("EOIE-MEMORY-VERBS", "One-call verbs: eoie memory recall <query|ids> [--client <c>] (relevant memories for this task, ranked, with related links), eoie memory save <kind> <slug> <description> <body> (type-checked: feedback needs Why/How), eoie memory edit/forget, eoie memory check (subsumes EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS: backlog IDs, dangling links, Done items). Each verb ~20 ms native vs reading 56 files; Spiral test blocks", Story, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,memory", "current", "current")
inl eoie_memory_adapters () = Task ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", "Client adapters (HKT-style interface, one instance per client): Claude (projects/*/memory md + MEMORY.md), Zed (AGENTS.md rules; threads db read-only), Codex (AGENTS.md), OpenCode (AGENTS.md/config; db read on a copy, never credential/account tables), Antigravity (.gemini/antigravity brain/annotations: format to study first), Grok (to find). Import (absorb once) and export (render each client's bootstrap or projection). No secrets ever enter the store (x-api-credentials stays a path reference)", Story, P2, D6, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,memory", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_1 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "14:10 Claude Code items (measured): C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\projects\\C--home-git\\memory = 56 item files + MEMORY.md; no other project dir has memory. Bytes: LF, no BOM, one final LF, no trailing spaces, 11 files non-ASCII (UTF-8), 783..11,214 B (sum 113,785; largest spiral-macro-gotchas). Frontmatter, fixed key order: name, description, metadata: {node_type: memory, type, originSessionId: uuid, modified: ISO-8601 ms Z} in 55 files (7 lines); inner-jev-autonomy has metadata: {type} only (4 lines). Then one blank line, then body. name equals filename stem in all 56. description max 206 chars: 48 YAML double-quoted (4 with backslash-escaped quotes), 8 plain (agent-sync-daily plain with raw Windows backslashes): keep a quote-style tag, never re-derive. Kinds: feedback 44, project 8, reference 4, user 0. All 52 feedback/project bodies hold **Why:** and **How to apply:** (51 line-initial, 1 inline); the 4 references hold neither. So the GADT why/how fields are sound, but bodies have prose around them: store body as ordered segments (text | why | how) so render is byte-exact. Links: 111 [[slug]] in 53 files, 39 distinct targets, 2 dangling: delete-dangling-freely (from dangling-check-by-stem) and corpus-check-before-packs (from kino-copy-lib-resolution; that is the MEMORY.md title of lib-smoke-before-syntax.md, a renamed slug: check should suggest it).")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_2 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "Claude index and loading: MEMORY.md = 56 lines + final LF, 9,151 B, no heading/frontmatter; every line is: - [Title](file.md) — hook (U+2014 em dash, single spaces), max 245 chars; all 56 items indexed, none missing. Title is free text, not the slug (Agent count -> max-three-agents.md, Corpus check before packs -> lib-smoke-before-syntax.md): a separate field, and index order is a list. Loading: the harness injects MEMORY.md into every session (seen verbatim in this agent's own context; documented cap first 200 lines), item files are read on demand; the agent writes all of it with file tools. Instructions: no ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md, no ~/.claude/rules, no CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md at C:\\home\\git or any repo root; only in-repo docs (spiral/apps/compiler/AGENTS.md 931 B, its tmp/AGENTS.md 68,825 B) and upstream zed (.rules 12,651 B; CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, GEMINI.md are 6-byte symlink-placeholder files reading .rules). In-repo AGENTS.md already sync through git: out of scope for import. ~/.claude/settings.json has no memory keys. Bootstrap: ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md (global, always on), 2-3 lines: memory lives in eoie, read the eoie memory tutorial first, record memories with eoie, not in this folder; and MEMORY.md shrunk to one pointer line so auto-memory stops growing a parallel store. Caution: Grok (compat.claude) and OpenCode (docs: fallback when no global AGENTS.md) also read ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md, so keep that bootstrap client-neutral.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_3 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 3u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "Zed: global rules = %APPDATA%\\Zed\\AGENTS.md (paths::agents_file = config_dir/AGENTS.md, zed/crates/paths/src/paths.rs:319), 908 B LF markdown, no frontmatter, one H2 section: Commit message (the customized built-in commit prompt, moved there by prompt_store/rules_to_skills_migration.rs, which appends Default Rules under ## title H2s and turns non-default Rules into ~/.agents/skills/slug/SKILL.md). Loaded into the native agent system prompt of every conversation (always on); no size cap found in crates/agent. Project rules: only the FIRST match of RULES_FILE_NAMES at a worktree root (.rules, .cursorrules, .windsurfrules, .clinerules, .github/copilot-instructions.md, AGENT.md, AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, GEMINI.md; prompts.rs:22). settings.json (2,575 B JSONC, // header) has no rules or profiles fields; its agent block is model/permissions/dock only: adapter never touches it. Legacy Rules LMDB AppData/Local/Zed/prompts/prompts-library-db.0.mdb is kept by the migration (not memory). ~/.agents absent. threads.db (copy, mode=ro, query_only): one table threads(id TEXT PK, summary, updated_at, data_type, data BLOB, parent_id, folder_paths, folder_paths_order, created_at), 8 rows, all zstd = transcripts. Zed has no memory store. Bootstrap: upsert an H2 section ## Memory with one pointer line into the global AGENTS.md; the adapter is a section-preserving edit keyed by H2 title so the Commit message section stays byte-identical.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_4 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 4u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "OpenCode: ~/.config/opencode holds only cli.json (v2 schema, UI prefs) and service.json. PRIVACY: service.json is a plaintext service password: never read or import it (deny by name). No opencode.json(c), no global AGENTS.md, no .opencode dir at C:\\home\\git. opencode.db (db+wal+shm copied; sqlite_master and non-sensitive schemas only): account, account_state, control_account, credential (never read), event, event_sequence, instruction_blob(hash PK, value), instruction_entry(session_id, key, value, removed, time_created, time_updated; PK session_id+key), instruction_state(session_id PK, epoch_start, through_seq, initial_values JSON, current_values JSON), kv, migration, permission, project, project_directory, session_inbox, session_message, session_pending, session_v2, workspace, worktree. instruction_* is a per-session content-addressed snapshot of system-prompt parts: the one state row maps core/codemode, core/skill-guidance, core/date, core/environment to 64-hex hashes into instruction_blob (5 blobs, 17..11,778 B); instruction_entry is empty. No memory feature: nothing to import. Loading (OpenCode docs, not verified on this v2 build): ~/.config/opencode/AGENTS.md plus project AGENTS.md found walking up from cwd, falling back to ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md when no global file exists, plus opencode.json instructions globs; all always on. Bootstrap: create ~/.config/opencode/AGENTS.md with the eoie pointer (this also stops the CLAUDE.md fallback double-load).")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_5 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 5u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "Antigravity (.gemini\\antigravity; antigravity-ide is a same-shape twin, plus antigravity-backup): no memory/knowledge store and no user rules on this machine. brain/conversation-uuid/ is per-conversation working state, not memory: .system_generated/{logs/transcript*.jsonl, messages/uuid.json, steps/n/*.txt tool outputs, tasks/*.log}, .user_uploaded/, scratch/ = conversation content, never import. annotations/uuid.pbtxt is protobuf text (title, last_user_view_time {seconds nanos}); antigravity_state.pbtxt holds onboarding/migration enums and installation_uuid; conversations/uuid.db and conversation_summaries.db are SQLite; agyhub_summaries_proto.pb binary. Rules per the bundled builtin/skills/agy-customizations docs: GEMINI.md and AGENTS.md (no frontmatter, always on) walked up from cwd or edited file to repo root; .agents/rules/*.md with frontmatter trigger: always_on or model_decision; workspace roots .agents/ .agent/ _agents/ _agent/; global ~/.gemini/config/ (here only config.json userSettings, projects/uuid.json, empty plugins and sidecars; no rule file; ~/.gemini/GEMINI.md absent). Limits: 24,000 B per rule file after @[label](path) include expansion, truncated on a line boundary; 20,000-token always-on rules budget, overflow demoted to path pointers. Bootstrap: one global rule under ~/.gemini/config/ (confirm the exact filename in the Customizations UI before the first write) with the eoie pointer. PRIVACY: ~/.gemini/oauth_creds.json and google_accounts.json: exclude.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_6 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 6u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "Codex (.codex): config.toml (283 B: approval/sandbox/model, projects c:\\home\\git trust, windows sandbox) has no instruction keys; no ~/.codex/AGENTS.md or AGENTS.override.md; rules/default.rules is exec-policy Starlark prefix_rule(pattern=[...], decision=...) = command permissions, not memory. A memory feature exists but is empty: memories_1.sqlite (copy) has stage1_outputs(thread_id PK, source_updated_at, raw_memory, rollout_summary, rollout_slug, generated_at, usage_count, last_usage, selected_for_phase2, selected_for_phase2_source_updated_at) 0 rows, jobs 0, consolidation_progress 1, _sqlx_migrations 2; no ~/.codex/memories dir (the phase-2 output folder). skills/ holds only .system vendor skills. Loading (Codex docs): ~/.codex/AGENTS.md (AGENTS.override.md wins) plus AGENTS.md from git root down to cwd, always on, combined cap project_doc_max_bytes (default 32 KiB). Import: nothing today; when memories appear, stage1 raw_memory rows are per-thread drafts and the memories folder is the curated output (read that, never the db). Bootstrap: ~/.codex/AGENTS.md with the eoie pointer. PRIVACY: auth.json, .sandbox-secrets/, history.jsonl, sessions/: exclude.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_7 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 7u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "Grok (.grok) HAS a memory store, memory-v2 (no memory key in config.toml: on via env or managed setting). Per scope (global/, workspaces/git-437d9324/): topics/*.md curated (11 workspace topics 722..16,945 B, 0 global; LF, no frontmatter: # Title, one-line summary, ## sections, - bullets); observations/_inbox/*.md pending (19; YAML frontmatter schema_version: 2, type, topic_hint, keywords, aliases, session_id, from_turn, through_turn, extraction_model, prompt_version, created_at epoch, job_id, observation_ordinal); archive/dream_hex/ consumed observations named session__tFROM-TO__nK.md (30-day retention); MEMORY.md generated, never write it; index.sqlite and memory_state.sqlite: leave alone. Loading (docs/user-guide/13-memory.md): both MEMORY.md indexes injected once per session, 8 KiB per scope; topics read on demand; capture after each turn into the inbox; /dream or auto-dream folds into topics; the model may edit topics/ and _inbox/ with file tools. Rules (12-project-rules.md, always on): ~/.grok/rules/*.md (absent), ~/.claude/rules and ~/.claude/CLAUDE*.md by compat.claude, [paths] extra_rule_dirs, then Agents.md, Claude.md, CLAUDE.md, CLAUDE.local.md, AGENT.md, AGENTS.md from repo root to cwd (cwd only outside git; needs folder trust). Import: topics as a topic kind (title, summary, sections), observations as an observation kind with provenance. Bootstrap: ~/.grok/rules/eoie-memory.md, and either set memory_v2.enabled = false or keep one global topic whose body is the eoie pointer.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_8 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 8u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "Adapter interface proposal: a client is a record over a type-level carrier f (result with a per-client error union, later an io effect): import : root -> f (list stored); render : list stored -> f (list {path; text}); bootstrap : tutorial_ref -> f (list {path; edit}) with union edit = Replace text | UpsertSection {heading; text} | AppendLine text, so foreign content in Zed/Codex/Antigravity AGENTS.md files survives. stored = Stored :: forall k. memory k -> stored, and beside the kind GADT a source layout record, not part of the kind: {client; scope = Global | Workspace slug; path; quote = Plain | Double; meta = {node_type option; origin_session option; modified option}; body = list segment}. Renderers derive frontmatter from layout so bytes round-trip; normalization is a separate explicit verb. Kinds beyond user/feedback/project/reference: topic (Grok topics), observation (Grok inbox, with session/turn provenance), rule (an H2 section of an AGENTS.md). Indexes are derived: Claude MEMORY.md = ordered list of {title; file; hook}; Grok MEMORY.md is never rendered. Deny-list is a type: import walks only an allow-list of paths per client (Claude memory dir, Zed AGENTS.md, Grok topics and _inbox, Codex memories dir, OpenCode and Antigravity rule files); everything else is unrepresentable rather than filtered.")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_adapters_9 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-ADAPTERS", 9u32, "2026-10-17T18:12Z", "memory-formats", "Round-trip test blocks: (1) 56 Claude items render byte-identical; (2) MEMORY.md byte-identical, em dash and order kept; (3) inner-jev-autonomy keeps its 4-line frontmatter (no node_type/session/modified); (4) double-quoted descriptions with escaped quotes and the plain one with raw backslashes keep their quoting; (5) the 11 non-ASCII files keep UTF-8; (6) the one inline How to apply body survives; (7) a feedback without why fails to typecheck (negative), references without Why/How typecheck; (8) links: 111 parsed, 39 targets, exactly 2 dangling reported, corpus-check-before-packs gets lib-smoke-before-syntax suggested via its index title; (9) Zed AGENTS.md: UpsertSection Memory twice is idempotent and Commit message stays byte-identical; (10) the 11 Grok topics and 19 observations re-render byte-identical; (11) import, render, import is a fixed point; (12) an allow-list test: a tree seeded with service.json, auth.json, .credentials.json, oauth_creds.json yields zero items. Privacy flags: OpenCode service.json plaintext password; .claude/.credentials.json; .codex/auth.json and .sandbox-secrets; .grok/auth.json; .gemini/oauth_creds.json and google_accounts.json; OpenCode credential/account tables; Antigravity brain/ and every threads/sessions store are transcripts; Grok observations carry session ids and the agent-sync topic a scheduler id; Claude items carry originSessionId (fine in a private repo, strip before anything public); x-api-credentials stays a path pointer.")
inl eoie_memory_bootstrap () = Task ("EOIE-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP", "Swap to bootstrap (needs the user's go): each client keeps one bootstrap entry ('memory lives in eoie: run eoie memory recall; tutorial at eoie/spec/...') and eoie in git becomes the synced source for the laptop and the work machine; a pull plus 'eoie memory export --client <c>' sets up a new machine. Rollback: export renders the full Claude memory dir again", Story, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,memory", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_memory_bootstrap_1 () = Note ("EOIE-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:24Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:24 prior art in bundle-cleanup-backup-20260926\\bundle_eoie_v20260723-alpha70462.zip state\\bootstrap.spi (Sep 2026): eoie's own bootstrap as typed Spiral. bootstrap_transition Boot -> FallbackSelected -> CandidateBuilt -> CandidateVerified -> Promoted; an execution_route chain Candidate -> RecentFallback -> ArchiveFallback -> HostEscapeRejected (host escape is a typed refusal, not an option); promotion only with typecheck AND e2e AND cold differential; rules such as CompilerPinnedByIdentity, FallbackBeforeHostEscape, GeneratedRustIsMirror, CrateUnderBudget. A ready shape for the bootstrap entry and for 'eoie memory export' rollback.")
inl eoie_union_reflection () = Task ("EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION", "Drift from the GPT era (user 2026-10-17: eoie maps unions to numbers; with reflection and the GADT/prototype feature set we could do it in fewer lines): measured 2026-10-17 across 493 eoie .spi files, 16 modules use the pattern: 67 pipe_index/pipe_member/pipe_lookup calls, 79 '| Nu64 =>' arms and 22 literal 'a|b|c|...' name tables. Example: agile subcommands live in 5 hand-synced tables (the union in agile_subcommand_domain, its name string, the u64 index match, agile_full_usage_error's usage text, agile_operand_shapes in eoie_agile_mutation, plus the pipe list in agile_entry); adding a verb means editing all of them. Target: one union per concept; case names by reflection (lib reflection, with case enumeration added to the lib/compiler if it can't enumerate nullary cases at compile time yet); parsing by a fold over the cases; usage and operand shapes in one exhaustive spec : case -> {name; shape; summary} prototype match the compiler checks for completeness. The numeric layer goes away. Proof: eoie tests unchanged, generated crates stay under the 1k gate, LOC before/after per module. Blocks new verbs such as agile links (EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS)", Story, P1, D5, 60u32, Active, "", "eoie,drift,reflection,gadt", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_union_reflection_1 () = Note ("EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:24Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:24 related audits found in the bundles (BUNDLE-WISDOM note 9, compiler alpha3006 agile_eoie.md, Sep 2026): a DU or option flattened into bools/strings and recombined in a later match; one boolean gating two authority domains; a hint field vetoing semantic closure; comparisons by rendered type name where an interned identity exists. Same family as union-to-reflection drift; one lint could cover all of them.")
inl note_of_eoie_union_reflection_2 () = Note ("EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:33Z", "main-thread", "14:33 main-thread: TODO-AS-SOURCE first slice. This item's code-local TODO block is at eoie/src/agile_subcommand_domain/subcommand.spi (above union agile_subcommand, with the target spec/derivation sketched in Spiral); eoie agile todos harvests it with id EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION.")
inl note_of_eoie_union_reflection_3 () = Note ("EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION", 3u32, "2026-10-17T19:05Z", "main-thread", "15:05 main-thread slice 1 done (unstaged, eoie src): new library package union_reflection/cases.spi (16 lines): union_cases_fold over a union's cases with raw UnionToRecord + RecordTypeFold + NominalCreate inside real (eoie has no core), in declaration order, and union_cases_joined for static text. agile_subcommand_domain now holds ONE exhaustive agile_subcommand_spec match (name + shape per case); names, the usage shape lookup, the full usage error in agile_mutation and the agile line of command_spec help are derived from it. Removed: the u64 index match, the names pipe string, agile_subcommands, agile_operand_shapes and the hand copy inside command_spec usage (4 hand-synced copies of the verb list). Gotchas: no backtick type application in top-down code, annotate lambda params used only inside !!!! ops, and a lookup fold must be let (as inl at 7 sites agile_mutation.rs hit 1567 lines; as let 712 vs 614 before). Publish + build -Test -SkipRelease: 197 Rust + 9 Spiral tests, 0 failed (slice 1a). Next: slice 2 replaces the 3 arity pipe_index dispatch tables in agile_entry with a typed parse (AgileKnown | AgileUnknownName) + exhaustive route match.")
inl note_of_eoie_union_reflection_4 () = Note ("EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION", 4u32, "2026-10-17T19:11Z", "styles-survey", "15:14 styles-survey [1/2 what eoie re-implemented vs lib/spiral] 0 of 324 eoie package.spiproj list core or lib (x and alphabet use packageDir ../spiral/lib with |core- and spiral-). Re-implementations: (1) reflection.spi (374 lines: get_union_fields(_untag), union_try_pick, union_to_string, nameof, get_record_fields) -> pipe tables: rust_std_string pipe_member/pipe_index/pipe_lookup, 62 call sites in 14 files, 61 numeric u64 match arms in 5 files, 26 literal name tables of 4+ members; 145 lines of that numeric layer inside those 15 files (2,527 lines). (2) sm-prime string module (1,868 lines) -> rust_std_string/model.spi 34 lines (concat5, equals, starts_with, ends_with, contains, replace, trim, trim_end_matches), referenced from 119 files, concat5 called 323 times where lib has ++ and interpolation. (3) core operators -> workspace_primitives/numbers.spi (i32_eq, i32_le, i32_next, i32_prev, i32_text, u64_text): 350 call sites. (4) optionm-prime/resultm (310 + 514 lines) -> generic clones such as validation_result a = Valid | Invalid, quoted_result, separator_result among 31 own outcome/result unions (most are real domain unions and should stay). (5) listm-prime/am-prime -> none: 0 Cons in eoie, collections live in pipe strings and rust_global (390 occurrences; 362 raw macro lines). Rough drop with lib: ~145 numeric-layer lines + ~90 primitive lines + the 5-table sync per concept; the bigger gain is readability (~670 helper calls become operators), not size.")
inl note_of_eoie_union_reflection_5 () = Note ("EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION", 5u32, "2026-10-17T19:11Z", "styles-survey", "15:15 styles-survey [2/2 blockers and path] (a) No-core packages: without core there is no |>, not, Some/None or bool =, so every eoie package rebuilds them; lib/spiral itself needs |core-. (b) 1k crate gate: lib code is written for inlining, e.g. spiral.rs is 24,280 lines because lib parsing split_args inlines to ~5,000 and union_to_string/trace inline into loops; lib union_try_pick builds a runtime list (fields ++ lowercased fields) and an array per call, while eoie union_cases_fold unrolls at compile time (an inl lookup at 7 sites took agile_mutation.rs 614 -> 1567 lines, a let fixed it). Importing lib wholesale would break the gate unless lib entry points become let. (c) Export boundary: 431 !!!!Export sites pass only i32/string/tuples, so unions are flattened to codes at every crate edge; x does the same (command_fields kind i32 + 13 scalars). Reflection inside a crate cannot remove the codes that cross crates. Path: 1. move union_cases_fold into lib reflection as the compile-time primitive and rebuild union_try_pick/union_to_string on it (fewer generated lines for lib users too); 2. a core-only eoie probe package (|core- + reflection) measuring crate lines before/after on agile_subcommand_domain; 3. compiler: Export of a nominal union as a pub Rust enum (or tag + payload pair) so crate edges stay typed. Without 3, steps 1-2 still remove the 5 hand-synced tables inside each crate.")
inl note_of_eoie_union_reflection_6 () = Note ("EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION", 6u32, "2026-10-17T19:22Z", "main-thread", "15:22 main-thread slices 2+3 green (publish of 26 owners + build -Test -SkipRelease: 197 Rust + 11 Spiral tests, 0 failed, 583 s). Slice 2: agile_entry dispatch is a typed parse agile_subcommand_of (reflection fold to AgileKnown subcommand | AgileUnknownName) + one exhaustive agile_route match with per-verb arity; the 3 arity pipe_index tables are gone, and a new verb is a compile error until its spec row and route arm exist. Slice 3: union_case_suffix (Unbox + SymbolToString + StringSlice, inside real) derives case text from the case name: agile_case_text replaces the kind/priority/difficulty text matches, parse_agile_case replaces the 3 pipe_index parsers, validate_status and the task census statuses come from agile_status. Generated Rust over the 26 owners 14,556 -> 13,496 lines (-7 percent); pipe call sites 64 -> 57. Regression caught: agile_mutation.rs 1,166 lines because the inl usage error was copied into 19 cold error arms; now a let (slice 4 run checks the 1k gate with a release build).")
inl dib_modernize () = Task ("DIB-MODERNIZE", "Retire every .dib remnant straight to .spi test blocks, skipping the .livemd stage (user 2026-10-17: the livemd phase that lasted until test blocks was never enough). Measured 2026-10-17: 0 .dib files remain in our repos, 14 files mention them. Kino's F# route (fsi_chain.ex and fsi_session.ex resolve #!import x.dib from the dotnet-repl era; ~15 tests build .dib notebook fixtures); polyglot/apps/perf/Perf.livemd imports lib/fsharp/Notebooks.dib and Testing.dib, which no longer exist (dead); history mentions in apps/compiler/tmp/NEXT.md and eoie ingest_frontier's port-now classifier. Delete the route and fixtures, or port what's still live", Story, P2, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "kino,polyglot,migration", "current", "current")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_1 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:33Z", "main-thread", "14:33 main-thread: TODO-AS-SOURCE first slice. This item's code-local TODO block is at spiral/apps/kino/lib/spiral/kino/fsi_chain.ex:184 (at the .dib import route); eoie agile todos harvests it with id DIB-MODERNIZE.")
inl note_of_dib_modernize_2 () = Note ("DIB-MODERNIZE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:41Z", "main-thread", "14:41 main-thread scope measured. Kino .dib surface: Document.parse_dib is the default parser for any unknown notebook extension (notebook.ex parse), cli_export falls back to the old dib-export CLI subcommand, the domain exports parse_dib (spi/document.spi and main.spi), directives.spi has a dib error kind, and fsi_chain.ex resolves #!import x.dib (TODO block placed there). Tests: 21 use .dib fixtures (notebook_test 13, display 3, fsi_session 3, notebook_report 1, document 1); 15 are Spiral notebook-runner behaviours and 6 exercise the F# FSI route. Many test the notebook-to-.spi export feature itself (spi is written only when the cells pass). So the modernization is architectural: .spi is the only source (test blocks are the cells), notebooks are output only (ipynb/html), and the .dib/.livemd parsers and the interactive F# kernel retire together (also the bold-native-goals direction). Order: (1) port the 15 Spiral runner tests to .spi fixtures with test blocks; (2) drop parse_dib and dib-export; (3) decide the FSI kernel (polyglot Perf.livemd and the i574n.github workflow livemd still have cells: port them to .spi or scripts); (4) drop parse_livemd. Each step is proven by the Kino suite (~2.5 min).")
inl kino_rename () = Task ("KINO-RENAME", "Rename the Kino project folder and code to something generic (user 2026-10-17 dislikes the kino name), timed with KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR: the supervision part rewritten in Spiral compiling to Gleam 1.19 (the new compiler) removes Elixir from the repo, and that native core should give the name. Parallel or performance-heavy parts (rayon-style) compile to Rust. Precedent: the X client started as apps/spindle and became x/apps/client (xc); no memory recorded that rename, and backlog items X-CLIENT-THREADS and X-CLIENT-AGENT-JSON still say spindle", Story, P2, D4, 100u32, Done, "", "kino,naming", "current", "current")
inl note_of_kino_rename_1 () = Note ("KINO-RENAME", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:47Z", "kino-native-design", "14:45 kino-native-design naming. The native core runs cells (notebook cells and test blocks, which document.spi already models as cells) through a supervised compile/run tree for many backends. Candidates, all free under spiral/apps, spiral/lib, lib/spiral (modules and bench/leptos/near/rust/target) and the C:\\home\\git repo names: cells, foreman, loom, kiln, workbench, crucible, proving. Avoid: lab (spiral/lab exists), bench (lib/spiral/bench), harness (compiler harness), relay (the removed Kino relay), runner (clashes with the Runner module). Recommendation: cells, i.e. apps/cells, Gleam package spiral_cells, Spiral.Kino.X becoming cells modules, CLI cells run path, env SPIRAL_CELLS_*: a plain role name like the spindle to x/apps/client rename, and it names the unit the core schedules, not the BEAM. Runner-up: foreman, if a name for the supervision core itself is wanted. Rename surface found: 13 non-source files (AGENTS.md, compiler test.ps1, apps/kino/spi run_suite/run_notebook, lab/harness-run-only-useful, formatter PLAN, README, apps/spiral/build.ps1, lib/spiral/build.ps1, near wallet build.ps1, tibia WORKFLOW, scripts/init.ps1, core.ps1) plus SPIRAL_KINO_* env vars, kino-PORT.lock, kino-gleam-warm cache dirs and the memo identity (a rename re-keys the memo once). Time it with the delegate-removal end of KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR so files move once.")
inl note_of_kino_rename_2 () = Note ("KINO-RENAME", 2u32, "2026-10-17T19:43Z", "main-thread", "15:43 main-thread, user 2026-10-17 decision: the new name is cells (apps/cells, Gleam package spiral_cells, SPIRAL_CELLS_* env), the kino-native-design pick. Timing stays as designed: the folder and code move once, when the last Elixir delegates are removed (KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR), so files are not moved twice; the rename itself is backlog work to plan now (13 files besides source, env vars, lock and cache names, memo identity = one cold run).")
inl note_of_kino_rename_3 () = Note ("KINO-RENAME", 3u32, "2026-10-17T20:12Z", "main-thread", "16:12 main-thread, user decisions 2026-10-17 (later round): rename to cells NOW, before the Elixir migration, and hard-cut every old name (SPIRAL_KINO_* env vars, cache and lock dirs) with no aliases; all consumers move in the same change, old caches go cold once.")
inl note_of_kino_rename_4 () = Note ("KINO-RENAME", 4u32, "2026-10-17T20:21Z", "main-thread", "16:21 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: do the cells rename now, in today's run until 18:00; if it is not green by 17:50, restore from the backup and report.")
inl note_of_kino_rename_5 () = Note ("KINO-RENAME", 5u32, "2026-10-17T20:47Z", "main-thread", "16:47 main-thread done in the tree (unstaged; hard cut, no aliases): spiral/apps/kino -> apps/cells; Elixir Spiral.Kino.* -> Spiral.Cells.* (lib/spiral/cells, test/spiral/cells, cells.ex, cells_test.exs, cells_live_test.exs), mix app spiral_cells, Gleam package spiral_cells (src/spiral_cells), env SPIRAL_KINO_* -> SPIRAL_CELLS_* and EOIE_SPIRAL_KINO -> EOIE_SPIRAL_CELLS, cache names kino-* -> cells-*, daemon banner and socket spiral-kino -> spiral-cells, lock/log names, internal identifiers. 112 files in 7 repos (spiral, eoie, x, i574n.github, polyglot, dice, alphabet; the consumer repos reach spiral through symlinked deps) + 8 in a second pass. Livebook's own kino library (Kino.*, the kino dependency, mix.lock) keeps its name; the backlog state and eoie state/ history are untouched. Proof: mix compile ok; mix test 245 tests, 1 failure from a missed escaped literal (\\tKino), fixed and rerun ok; eoie publish + release build with its Spiral tests through apps/cells: 197 Rust + 12 Spiral tests, topology ok. Old spiral-bin kino-* cache dirs are now stale (cold run once); DISK-CLEANUP can drop them.")
inl lib_usage_diagnostics () = Task ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", "Tooling to diagnose lib/spiral usage across our repos: which lib functions and test cells are never reached from any app, sample or repo; duplicated and generated code (the CLI lane found that ~9k of spiral.rs's 24k lines come from inlining the same parser 3 times); and expansion hot spots per generated crate, so the 1k-line packaging limit stays useful. Part of the branded tool suite (with the formatter); reuse and grow lib instead of per-app copies", Feature, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "lib,tooling,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_1 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:39Z", "main-thread", "14:39 main-thread first census (text-based, job tmp lib-usage.ps1 and lib-usage.tsv, 96 s over every tracked .spi/.spir in spiral/eoie/polyglot/dice/alphabet/x): lib/spiral has 1,361 top-level inl/let definitions in 70 modules. 41 are never referenced anywhere, 20 of them FParsec-style trailing-underscore variants in fparsec.spi (p_string_, many1_satisfy_, sep_by1_, not_followed_by_ ...: strong deletion candidates), plus seq 5, testing 4 and listm' 3. 671 are used only inside their own file (candidates for private helpers or inlining). Limit of the text method: backend.run_target shows as unused although it is used everywhere, because it comes into scope by a route a grep can't see. So the real tool must ask the compiler's name resolution (e.g. a --references mode next to --source-blocks that emits definition -> use sites per package) and report per repo, per package and per lib cell; later, generated-code duplication per crate for the 1k gate.")
inl note_of_lib_usage_diagnostics_2 () = Note ("LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS", 2u32, "2026-10-17T19:02Z", "main-thread", "15:02 main-thread, user 2026-10-17: inlining sprawl is wanted where it buys speed, so the tool must not just count expanded lines. Classify each expansion as hot or cold and measure it: an A/B per backend (inl vs let for the same definition) through the harness benchmark rows. Measured today: eoie agile_subcommand_shape, a reflection fold over 18 cases, was an inl called from 7 usage-error sites, and agile_mutation.rs went 614 -> 1567 lines. That is cold code (error paths), so the copies gain nothing at run time and cost rustc time and the 1k gate; as a let it is emitted once. Hot loops are the opposite case: specialization removes dispatch and allocation. Report per crate: expansion factor, hot or cold by benchmark evidence, and a suggested inl/let flip. Longer term the compiler could outline cold paths itself (like Rust's cold attribute).")
inl eoie_state_scale () = Task ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", "eoie backlog state bloat is intended (user 2026-10-17): state/agile.spi is now 1.15 MB and grows with every note; keep it as a realistic append-heavy log to simulate an Apache Iggy-style stream and measure how the compiler scales on real log data (parse/typecheck time of state/agile.spi per MB). Track the growth curve; refactor only if a measured threshold breaks a workflow", Story, P3, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,scale,iggy", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_1 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:46Z", "iggy-research", "14:47 iggy-research (1/3) state/agile.spi measured read-only at 14:41 (before the IGGY-EVAL notes): 1,203,199 bytes, 1,588 lines, longest line 18,177 chars (the EOIE-DOGFOOD title). Rows: 337 Task (556,734 bytes, avg 1.65 KB), 1,040 Note (623,301 bytes, avg 600 B), 170 Planning; the rest is ~40 lines of union schema. Notes per UTC day: 10-14 306, 10-15 363, 10-16 118, 10-17 253 so far. Size over git commits: 10-08 43 KB, 10-09 53 KB, 10-12 163-180 KB, 10-13 313-416 KB, 10-14 686 KB, 10-15 791 KB, 10-17 06:53 996 KB, 13:00 1,091 KB, 14:45 1,213 KB: about 150-270 KB/day lately, so ~6-8 MB in a month if linear. Write path (eoie src/agile_note_model/model.spi): a note is not appended at EOF but spliced after the task's last note (notes_insertion), the whole text is re-read, compared to the original and replaced atomically (state_written; StateChangedSinceRead = optimistic concurrency, nothing written, caller retries); note counts and insertion points come from full-text scans. So every write is O(file) read + scan + rewrite. Receipts today: note writes 8-10 ms at 1.2 MB, show 4 ms: cheap now. Already broken threshold: the file is ~66k tokens, over the agent Read tool's 25k-token limit, so agents cannot read it whole (eoie agile show is the only path). typecheck_receipts.spi last attested agile.spi at 994,972 bytes.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_2 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:46Z", "iggy-research", "14:47 iggy-research (2/3) What to measure (no runs done by this lane). M1 compiler: wall time and peak RSS of the installed compiler's parse + typecheck of agile.spi (eoie agile check --compiler and the compiler's own phase timings), cold (session torn down, per BENCH-FRESH-PAIR) and warm, over the 11 git snapshots 43 KB..1.09 MB plus the live file, plus synthetic 2x/4x/8x copies (bindings renamed, same row mix). Fit time = a * MB^b: b > 1.2 flags superlinear behaviour; split the cost by driver (top-level binding count ~1,550, longest line 18 K chars, total string literal bytes) with single-variable synthetic files. M2 eoie verbs: elapsed_ms of note, set, show, list --json per snapshot (all receipts already print it), p50/p95 over a day of real use. M3 growth: bytes/day and notes/day from note timestamps (a one-line census; the ledger could log it daily). M4 contention: count StateChangedSinceRead rejections; today they are not recorded anywhere, so the note/set receipt should log retries.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_3 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 3u32, "2026-10-17T18:46Z", "iggy-research", "14:47 iggy-research (3/3) Thresholds that would warrant an Iggy-style segment layout (any one, measured twice): compiler check of state over 5 s warm or 15 s cold, or b over 1.3 at 4x size; peak RSS over 1.5 GB for the check; an agile write verb p95 over 100 ms; file over ~8 MB (about a month at the current rate) or a line over 64 KB; more than 1 StateChangedSinceRead retry per hour across lanes. Layout then: state/agile/ with an active segment for current task rows (projection) and append-only note segments sealed at ~1 MB or per month (notes appended at EOF, never spliced), each sealed segment immutable with its own typecheck receipt (typecheck_receipts.spi already keys modules by bytes + hash, so sealed segments are typechecked once and skipped), a small offset index per segment (item id -> note seqs) so show reads one index instead of scanning, and readers keeping their own offsets. To keep the scale signal after a split, freeze the monolithic file at each threshold crossing as a benchmark corpus, so compiler scaling stays measurable on real log data, which is the point of this item.")
inl note_of_eoie_state_scale_4 () = Note ("EOIE-STATE-SCALE", 4u32, "2026-10-17T20:21Z", "main-thread", "16:21 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: measure first (11 git snapshots + 2x/4x/8x copies, warm/cold typecheck time and memory) and switch to the segment layout only when a threshold breaks (check over 5 s warm, memory over 1.5 GB, file over 8 MB, worse-than-linear growth); the single file stays a compiler-scaling corpus.")
inl iggy_eval () = Task ("IGGY-EVAL", "Evaluate integrating Apache Iggy (persistent message streaming) in our projects: typed GADT/existential messages with reflection-derived codecs could make multidimensional sagas (compensations indexed by state at the type level); candidates: eoie job registry and receipts (EOIE-JOBS), the harness run ledger, Kino cell events, xsim. Local workaround and evaluation only (no upstream contact)", Story, P3, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "iggy,messaging,research", "current", "current")
inl note_of_iggy_eval_1 () = Note ("IGGY-EVAL", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:45Z", "iggy-research", "14:44 iggy-research (1/7) Apache Iggy state 2026-10-17. Top-level ASF project since 2026-08-19. Latest server 0.9.0 (blog 2026-09-21; Rust SDK crate iggy 0.11.0, CLI 0.14.0); earlier 0.8.0 2026-04-22, 0.7.0 2026-02-24. 0.9.0: VSR (Viewstamped Replication) clustering inside the standard iggy-server binary, opt-in via cluster.enabled, single node default; per-topic durability (replicated default, persisted = quorum on stable storage), enforce_fsync removed; breaking: 0.8 data dirs not migrated, 0.8 clients cannot talk to 0.9 (until 1.0 every upgrade = fresh data dir). Model: stream > topic > partition, segmented log on disk under streams/s/topics/t/partitions/p, retention via per-topic message_expiry and segment_size, server-stored consumer offsets, consumer groups, optional per-message headers, no server-side compression. Transports TCP (binary, port 8090), QUIC 8080, HTTP REST 3000, WebSocket 8092, TLS on all. Server is thread-per-core shared-nothing on compio + io_uring since 0.6. License Apache-2.0. SDKs: Rust, Python, Node, Java, C#, Go, PHP (no Elixir). Benchmarks (v0.7 blog): ~1,000 MB/s writes at 8-32 producers, P95 1.5-1.8 ms, reads 3,361 MB/s. Sources: github.com/apache/iggy, iggy.apache.org/blogs/2026/09/21/release-0.9.0/, iggy.apache.org/blogs/2026/02/27/thread-per-core-io_uring/, iggy.apache.org/docs/server/configuration/, release-catalog.apache.org/iggy/")
inl note_of_iggy_eval_2 () = Note ("IGGY-EVAL", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:45Z", "iggy-research", "14:44 iggy-research (2/7) Cost on our 16 GB laptop (doc-based estimates, nothing measured). Defaults are server-class: memory_pool size 4 GiB budget, transfer_served_cache_bytes_max 2176 MiB per shard, cpu_allocation numa:auto with pin_cores=true (one shard per core, pinned) - would fight the heavy gate's 2 slots and memory admission. A laptop config must cap it: one shard, pin_cores false, pool ~128-256 MiB, segment_size ~16-64 MiB, durability replicated (single node), BelowNormal priority; idle RSS undocumented, guess tens of MB. Windows: the server's io_uring design is Linux-first; no doc states native Windows server support (compio has an IOCP driver, unverified for iggy-server), so realistically WSL or Docker, which collides with the no-WSL-until-workflow-passes rule. Client side is fine: the Rust SDK and the HTTP API work anywhere. Pre-1.0 churn: wire and disk formats broke at 0.9, so anything we store must be re-derivable. An always-on daemon is also a new liveness problem for EOIE-JOBS to watch. Net: a server process is not justified today; the value is in the log model (segments, offsets, consumer groups, headers), which we can adopt without the server.")
inl note_of_iggy_eval_3 () = Note ("IGGY-EVAL", 3u32, "2026-10-17T18:45Z", "iggy-research", "14:44 iggy-research (3/7) Integration map A (gain vs plain append file). (1) Run ledger %LOCALAPPDATA%\\spiral-bin\\ledger\\runs.tsv: 102 rows, 30 KB today, written by heavy.ps1 Write-RunLedger with AppendAllText + 5 retries from many lanes. Iggy would give atomic multi-writer append, a live tail for watchers and a stored offset per reader (cost-report). Gain low: a per-reader offset file next to the TSV gives the same. (2) Harness results.tsv per run (~6,900 rows/run, ~1,100 run folders): the fast-tier fold cache stamping each run by size+mtime is a hand-made consumer offset, and its remaining ~10 s is statting 1,096 folders. A run-index topic (one event per finished run: id, suite, mode, results sha) consumed with a durable offset removes the walk. Gain medium; keep the folders as the content-addressed artifact, log only index events. (3) eoie job receipts (EOIE-JOBS): best fit. A job is a saga (recorded > queued > admitted > running > exited|timed_out|semantic_failure > reaped); completion notification = consumer wake-up, stop-by-id = command topic, restart = replay from the last offset; write the record before spawn = produce before act. Gain high in design, but eoie must stay one-call and serverless, so an embedded segmented log is the fit, not an iggy-server.")
inl note_of_iggy_eval_4 () = Note ("IGGY-EVAL", 4u32, "2026-10-17T18:45Z", "iggy-research", "14:44 iggy-research (4/7) Integration map B. (4) eoie agile notes: notes are events (id, seq, at, author, text) stored as a projection; an append log + projection would remove the whole-file rewrite and StateChangedSinceRead retries between lanes (see EOIE-STATE-SCALE); keep agile.spi as the compiler stress corpus. Gain medium. (5) Kino cell/test events: verdicts, memo hit/miss and per-target ms live in ipynb metadata.spiral_test and ledger notebook rows; a cell-event topic would fan out to cost-report and xsim with replay. Gain low-medium. (6) agent-sync: each client re-parses other clients' transcripts (jsonl, SQLite copies); a sync topic with one consumer group per client replaces the read-last-150-rows rule, HTTP transport works from any client. Gain low, volume tiny. (7) xsim (x/apps/sim) already simulates an at-least-once, out-of-order stream: World dedups by delivery index (seen set), buffers events whose parent is missing (pending/drain), reassembles k-of-n frames; Log writes JSON lines. Iggy as a real transport would test those invariants against real redelivery: best testbed; no Elixir SDK, so HTTP API or a Rustler NIF over the Rust crate. (8) CI artifacts (CI-OFFLOAD): runners are ephemeral Linux (iggy native there) but results return as files anyway; ship segment files as artifacts, replay locally. Gain low.")
inl note_of_iggy_eval_5 () = Note ("IGGY-EVAL", 5u32, "2026-10-17T18:45Z", "iggy-research", "14:44 iggy-research (5/7) Typed saga sketch, part 1 (Spiral-like pseudo-syntax). Axes as phantom indices: nominal unbuilt = () ; nominal built = () ; nominal untested = () ; nominal tested = () ; nominal undeployed = () ; nominal deployed = (). One GADT, each case moves exactly one axis and states its guards on the others: union rec step b t d = | Start :: step unbuilt untested undeployed | Build :: forall t. step unbuilt t undeployed -> dll_sha -> step built untested undeployed | Test :: step built untested undeployed -> results_sha -> step built tested undeployed | Deploy :: step built tested undeployed -> release_id -> step built tested deployed. Illegal moves do not typecheck: Deploy on step built untested d has no case; rebuilding resets the test axis by its result type. Compensations are typed per step as inverse cases: | Rollback :: step built tested deployed -> step built tested undeployed | Untest :: step built tested undeployed -> step built untested undeployed; there is no Unbuild from deployed, so undo must go through Rollback first. Undo order: a journal indexed by a type-level stack, union rec journal s = | Empty :: journal nil | Push :: forall s x. journal s -> x -> journal (cons x s), and undo only accepts journal (cons x s) with x's inverse in scope, so compensations run strictly LIFO.")
inl note_of_iggy_eval_6 () = Note ("IGGY-EVAL", 6u32, "2026-10-17T18:45Z", "iggy-research", "14:44 iggy-research (6/7) Typed saga sketch, part 2: wire and codecs. Existential envelope: union envelope = | Envelope :: forall b t d. saga_id * u64 offset * step b t d -> envelope; producers erase the indices, a consumer recovers them once at the decode boundary: the Iggy header carries axis tags (build=built, test=untested, deploy=undeployed) plus a schema hash, and a typecase over the tag triple picks the payload decoder; an unknown triple is the only runtime rejection, everything downstream is total. Reflection codecs: payloads are records, and reflection over record keys derives the JSON (or fixed binary) codec and the schema hash from key names and field types, so a renamed field changes the hash and old segments are decoded by the old codec (keep codecs per hash, never migrate in place). Multidimensional: N axes = N indices; a step that touches two axes (Deploy needs tested) states it in its argument type; orthogonal axes (lint, docs) advance concurrently because their cases are polymorphic in the other indices (forall t d). Partitioning: saga_id is the partition key so one saga stays ordered; the consumer group per axis lets build, test and deploy workers scale separately while the GADT keeps the cross-axis order. Prototype/HKT use: one generic saga runner over any step family f b t d with a per-family compensation prototype instead of hand-written runners.")
inl note_of_iggy_eval_7 () = Note ("IGGY-EVAL", 7u32, "2026-10-17T18:45Z", "iggy-research", "14:44 iggy-research (7/7) Recommendation. Do not install iggy-server now (Windows server support unverified, WSL rule, pre-1.0 format churn, server-class defaults on a 16 GB laptop). Adopt the model instead, in order: (a) EOIE-JOBS: an embedded Iggy-shaped log inside eoie: stream/topic/partition folders, fixed-size segment files with an offset index, one offset file per consumer, message headers (kind, axis tags, schema hash), written before spawn; the job saga typed as in notes 5-6. (b) Harness run-index topic so the fast-tier fold reads from its offset instead of statting ~1,100 folders. (c) xsim gets a transport seam (file log today, Iggy HTTP later) so its dedup/out-of-order invariants can run against a real broker on a Linux CI runner where iggy is native, as a CI-OFFLOAD job, no laptop daemon. Keep the on-disk layout compatible with Iggy's concepts (not its bytes, which break pre-1.0), so swapping in iggy-server later is a transport change. Revisit a real server when one of these holds: a second machine or Linux box consumes our events live, more than ~3 concurrent writers contend on one log, or Iggy ships 1.0 with documented Windows support. Sources in note 1.")
inl ci_offload () = Task ("CI-OFFLOAD", "Use the GitHub Actions minutes freed by dropping Fable (builds no longer need them, even on the NEAR projects) for heavy processing that spares the laptop: full harness suites with cached compile-memo/native-memo, benchmarks, Kino lib suites, bundle mining. Results come back as artifacts the local tools read (run ledger rows, results.tsv). Learn first from the recent Anthropic Scion case and the DeltaDB release (research notes on this item)", Story, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "ci,benchmark,offload", "current", "current")
inl note_of_ci_offload_1 () = Note ("CI-OFFLOAD", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:19Z", "main-thread", "14:19 main-thread research 2026-10-17. Anthropic Scion (engineering post 2026-10-13; thenewstack.io/anthropic-scion-git-replacement): Anthropic moved its monorepo off Git to an in-house system after Claude pushed merges from ~2,500/day (May) to 30,000+/day (Sept); its CPU and memory needs are about 200x below the Git mirroring it replaced; a team of ~7 built it in ~5 months, and it is not released. Zed DeltaDB (zed.dev/blog/delta-public-beta, Delta public beta 2026-09-16): extends Git's content-based versioning with incremental deltas and stores the agent conversations and instructions behind each change, while a commit stays the checkpoint you push, pull and build. Lessons for us: (1) agent-scale work needs content-addressed, incremental state; our compile/native memos and reproducible builds are the same idea applied to the harness, and CI can run them cheaply on cached memos; (2) link work history to the conversations behind it (DeltaDB): eoie notes with --as lanes already do this per item, and EOIE-MEMORY plus commit links would make it per change; (3) offload heavy runs to CI only where the result is content-keyed and replayable locally.")
inl todo_as_source () = Task ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", "Destructive migration, to try autonomously with backups (user 2026-10-17): spread // TODO: blocks through the code on purpose as the source of truth for work, multi-line and holding Spiral code (the 120-column formatter allows dense blocks), harvested by eoie agile todos instead of a separate backlog file. Iterate on copies with periodic backups in tmp until it's state of the art. Open design points: how a TODO block links to an eoie item ID, how status and progress live in the block, the formatter's handling of them, and the no-comments rule (TODO blocks become an allowed marker kind)", Epic, P1, D7, 35u32, Active, "", "eoie,todo,migration", "current", "current")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_1 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:20Z", "main-thread", "14:20 main-thread dry run (non-destructive; backlog state backed up to job tmp todo-source/agile.spi.20261017-142038.bak; mapping in todo-source/mapping.tsv): of 332 items (162 open), only 32 open items cite a source file that exists, spread over 22 files, and 8 of those point at lanes/hopacsota/PLAN.md, which is prose. The other 130 open items (epics, research, cross-cutting work) have no code home. A full move into code TODO blocks would orphan ~80% of the open backlog, so the measured design is a hybrid. Code-anchored tasks become typed top-level todo blocks, parsed like test blocks (the compiler already skips top-level 'test' blocks in both BlockParsing sections, so a 'todo ID P1 D3 = <multi-line Spiral>' block can hold typecheckable Spiral and costs nothing in a build); everything else stays in eoie state. eoie agile todos then harvests both into one view, and the block's ID keys its notes. Next step, autonomous with backups: the compiler/formatter accepts todo blocks (one regression sample), then the 32 code-anchored items move into their files, rolled back by restoring the backup.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_2 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:31Z", "main-thread", "14:31 main-thread: step 1 landed in eoie and is published. eoie agile todos now harvests TODO blocks. A full-line TODO comment absorbs the following comment lines whose leader starts at the same column, keeping their indentation, so multi-line Spiral survives; a blank line, code, a directive or another TODO ends the block. TODO(ID): yields an id field in --json (only when present), and json_escaped now also escapes CR and LF. Lives in src/agile_todo_model/model.spi (new test todo_blocks_keep_their_spiral_lines_and_ids; 6/6 model and 3/3 todo_cli tests pass). The first build broke the 1k crate gate (todo_lines.rs 685 -> 1,024 lines: json_escaped was inlined 3x in finding_json and comment_starts_line twice). Making json_escaped, todo_id, finding_json, comment_starts_line and todo_free let join points brought it to 846. Release built, topology checks passed, eoie.exe published. End to end on a fixture: the block prints with its Spiral lines, and JSON carries id EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS. Next: join harvested IDs with backlog status in agile todos, then move the 32 code-anchored open items into blocks (backup in job tmp todo-source/).")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_3 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 3u32, "2026-10-17T18:33Z", "main-thread", "14:33 main-thread: first migration slice. Three items with an exact code home now have TODO(ID) blocks: EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION (agile_subcommand_domain/subcommand.spi, target shape in Spiral), EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS (eoie_agile_mutation/agile_mutation.spi) and DIB-MODERNIZE (kino fsi_chain.ex). Backups are in job tmp todo-source/*.bak. The generated .rs is unchanged (comments). Finding: the automatic file mapping is weak (titles cite files in passing, e.g. SOCIAL-TRANSPORT-SIM -> scripts/build.ps1), so placement needs per-item judgment; items in the Tibia lane and design-prose targets were left alone. Usage note: agile todos takes the backlog root first, then the paths.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_4 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 4u32, "2026-10-17T19:19Z", "main-thread", "15:19 main-thread next grain, designed not built: agile todos joins each TODO(ID) block with the backlog row of that ID (the scan already gets the backlog root as its first operand) and prints the status after the id, plus tally stale=N (block whose item is Done) and unknown=M (no such item). Placement: a new small crate agile_todo_status (status lookup by the Task anchor + agile_task_text tuple_field 6, a typed todo_status union Open | Stale | Unknown), because agile_todo_lines is at 846 of the 1000-line gate and agile_todo_scan at 678. JSON adds a status field next to id.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_5 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 5u32, "2026-10-17T19:52Z", "main-thread", "15:52 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17 15:5x: tag format is // TODO: ID text (tag right after the colon, IDE TODO patterns respected; the ID is the leading upper-case token with a dash; TIBIA, HOPACSOTA and BLOG are the only dashless IDs, never used as tags). Done today: agile todos joins each tagged block with its backlog row and reports stale (item Done) and unknown (no item) blocks, via a new census export eoie_agile_task_status.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_6 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 6u32, "2026-10-17T20:13Z", "main-thread", "16:12 main-thread, user 2026-10-17: tags also accept a single upper-case word (TODO: TIBIA ...), so dashless IDs work. Stale tags leave the code by a deterministic rule (Jira-style state change + comment): agile todos --apply removes a block whose item is Done and archives its text as a note on that item; an unknown tag creates the item from the block's first line instead of being deleted; open tags stay. Without --apply it only reports, exit 0.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_7 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 7u32, "2026-10-17T20:21Z", "main-thread", "16:21 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: an item created by agile todos --apply for an unknown tag gets triage defaults: Story, P4, D3, Planned, tags todo,triage, title cut from the block's first line to 3-7 words; the TODO stays in code as an open tag.")
inl note_of_todo_as_source_8 () = Note ("TODO-AS-SOURCE", 8u32, "2026-10-17T20:47Z", "main-thread", "16:47 main-thread done (unstaged): tag format // TODO: ID text, with a single upper-case word accepted too; todo_id lives once in agile_todo_lines and is exported (eoie_agile_todo_id) so agile_todo_scan stays under the 1k gate (it hit 1,079 when the parser was copied into it); the status join prints stale/unknown lines and stale=N unknown=M; both existing tags migrated (agile_mutation EOIE-MEMORY-LINKS, cells fsi_chain DIB-MODERNIZE). Release build: 197 Rust + 12 Spiral tests, topology ok. Next: agile todos --apply (remove Done blocks into notes, create triage items for unknown tags).")
inl disk_cleanup () = Task ("DISK-CLEANUP", "Periodic disk cleanup for this machine: measure build outputs (spiral-bin bin/obj/runs/scratch/native, compile-memo, cargo target dirs, Kino builds, job tmp caches, Downloads bundles) and prune what's provably regenerable on a schedule, with a report of what was freed. The memo stores already prune themselves (compile 2 days, native 14 days)", Story, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "disk,ops", "current", "current")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_1 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 1u32, "2026-10-17T19:13Z", "main-thread", "15:13 main-thread inventory (read-only, directory level; a full per-file walk of spiral-bin took over 25 min and was stopped, so the tool must use directory-level stamps or the ledger): spiral-bin has 12 bin-pack*-backup compiler snapshots from 10-15/10-16 holding 6.5 GB (only the newest blessed pack and its predecessor are useful); runs has 1,842 run folders since 09-26, 894 older than 7 days, about 2.1 GB (sampled 50 = 121 MB), and the fast-tier fold cache and cost-report read results.tsv, so prune keeps results.tsv and drops the rest; compile-memo (53k files) and native-memo prune themselves. Earlier totals: eoie/src/target 16.8 GB, spiral/target 10.1 GB, toolchains 5.1 GB. C: had 15.3 GB free at 14:54. Design: an eoie verb with a dry-run report (path, size, last use, rule that made it prunable), rules as a typed union, never deleting tracked files.")
inl note_of_disk_cleanup_2 () = Note ("DISK-CLEANUP", 2u32, "2026-10-17T19:55Z", "main-thread", "15:55 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: pack backups keep the newest 2 (bin-pack1x/1y); the 10 older ones (1f..1w) were deleted with approval, freeing 5.35 GB (C: 19.8 GB free). That is the first retention rule for the tool: compiler pack backups keep 2.")
inl bundle_wisdom () = Task ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", "Wisdom mined from the 2026 zip bundles and bootstrap_llm files (user 2026-10-17)", Story, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,history", "current", "current")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_1 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:20Z", "bundle-wisdom", "14:20 [1/12 sources] Era map. Bootstrap era Jan 2026: Downloads\\files\\code\\bootstrap_llm_compiler_v88..v101.00.md (01-04..01-05; v101 is byte-identical to espiral-comum\\bootstrap_llm_coder_v2.md), bootstrap_llm_ps1.md v1.0 (01-05, two identical copies), bootstrap_llm_*.toml 01-05..01-08 (newest espiral-comum\\bootstrap_llm_v631-alpha1.toml 01-08; bootstrap_llm_eoie.toml 01-05). Zip era Jan-Sep 2026: the root index (527 zips) misses the newest bundles. Latest per branch: Downloads\\bundle-cleanup-backup-20260926\\ holds spiral-compiler-parallelization alpha3006, spiral-samples alpha705, spiral-bin alpha417 (all 09-26), spiral-brzozowski alpha54 (09-19), eoie alpha70462 (09-26; no agile file, has state\\bootstrap.spi); dev1\\ holds workspace_spiral-compiler-parallelization alpha2891 and bundle_eoie alpha70377 (08-17); files-2026-05\\ has 2,565 older zips (Jan-Jun). agile_eoie.md exists in 5 places: the 4 Sep-26 zips (compiler 29 KB, samples 103 KB, bin 35 KB, brzozowski 81 KB, also nested in samples under contracts\\megaprojects\\mega_brzozowski_derivatives\\ledgers) plus dev1\\eoie\\agile_eoie.md (08-11, an oracle-to-ansi ERP-consolidation lane, not Spiral). AGILE.md files (13 zips, core alpha9036..9103, June) are tiny task tables for stroma/tritcritnet/mechahaze. Notes 2-12 below carry what today's backlog lacks; tasks to resume went on the matching items as bundle-wisdom notes.")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_2 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 2u32, "2026-10-17T18:21Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[2/12 bootstrap protocol, bootstrap_llm_v631-alpha1.toml 2026-01-08 + bootstrap_llm_eoie.toml 01-05] The session was a typed state machine BOOT->LEASE->WORK->WRAP->BUNDLE->REPORT->DONE with named illegal states (long text before eoie status, shell probe while the eoie entry exists, work continued after should_yield=1, files outside the roots); on an illegal state the agent goes back to BOOT. Invariants I001-I005: one entry point for all actions, status before any substantial text, after the budget only WRAP/BUNDLE/REPORT, publish requested artifacts immediately, prefer receipt-leaving commands. Anti-shell rule was deny-by-default with a substitute: a missing eoie verb becomes a P0 agile task (pause, patch eoie, rebuild, resume), never a silent bypass; the only allowed escapes were eoie missing/broken and bootstrap extraction, both logged. Every action maps to an agile event or an eoie command, nothing lives only in chat. Each turn ended with a benchmark table and two 0..1000 ratings (fruitfulness = how re-executable the state became, satisfaction = budget kept + artifacts delivered). Retention kept current + 2 versions, dry-run before verify, never deleting bootstrap files. Today's memories eoie-first and use-full-timebox are this rule set re-found; the state machine, the illegal-state list and the per-turn ratings are not in today's backlog.")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_3 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 3u32, "2026-10-17T18:21Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[3/12 one-call provenance] The one-call requirement predates eoie's first commit: bootstrap_llm_v631-alpha1.toml (2026-01-08) [eoie] parallelism says to prefer CPU-saturating commands and 'if you feel you would run 3+ commands in a row, try to collapse them into 1', and patching batched 'patch, rebuild and bench in a single call'. bootstrap_llm_eoie.toml (01-05) [tools] pipe allowed 2 stages with persisted intermediates. So EOIE-ONE-CALL's note that the requirement 'never reached eoie's README' is right about the repo, but the rule existed in the bootstrap files; cite these as its origin.")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_4 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 4u32, "2026-10-17T18:21Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[4/12 lease and WRAP lessons; spiral-bin alpha417 + spiral-samples alpha705 agile_eoie.md, Jun-Sep 2026] Today's eoie still has agile begin/lease (the 75+10 lease), so these defects probably still apply. (a) WRAP blocks the closeout writes it requires (handoff, agile, ratings, LOC files), which forced out-of-band writes about 8 times; it needs a WRAP-safe closeout write class limited to a whitelist of state/report files, hash-receipted, unable to touch source. (b) Stale lease across user turns (seen 5+ times): a new turn inherits the previous turn's expired or WRAP lease and cannot even write the successor lease. Fix shape: a host-supplied new-turn epoch/nonce that archives the old lease and opens exactly one successor, with no way to extend within the same turn. (c) Dual authority: mutation admission said should_wrap=1 while lease status said 0 with 10 min left. Status and admission must share one lease token and a monotonic clock. (d) Rehydrated or control trees import their historical lease, so lease-neutral sandboxes are needed. (e) A resumed chat needs begin-at/adopt-clock, not a fresh 85 min. (f) A green pass is not done: v639 sealed early after one green run; the fix was a lease mode that tells version_available from session_saturated (today's use-full-timebox). Proposal: one item EOIE-LEASE (or fold into EOIE-JOBS) owning the time box, WRAP class and turn epochs.")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_5 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 5u32, "2026-10-17T18:21Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[5/12 process lifecycle; brzozowski alpha54, spiral-bin alpha417, dev1 agile_eoie.md] Mostly for EOIE-JOBS (a short copy is noted there). (1) Receipt-first launch: write the job record before spawn, so a killed supervisor still leaves typed timeout|yielded|child_continues|no_child instead of no receipt. (2) Own the process group and reap all descendants on timeout. A timed-out compiler facade left an orphan using 3.8 cores and made known-good compiles look like regressions. (3) Commit-aware timeout: re-read the child's own completion receipts before reporting timed_out (the wrapper timed out at 25 s after the child had committed). (4) Typed outcomes semantic_failure | time_budget | yield | environment_failure, kept all the way into reports, never collapsed into one fail bit. (5) Expected-exit sets: rc5 typecheck negatives, diff rc1 = different, installer rc75 = resumable yield. (6) On a semantic failure, cancel undispatched work, let running siblings finish, and record the first-failure frontier; retry only the timed-out cells with a larger budget. (7) A stop path that is not starved: a saturated MSBuild pool blocked status/stop itself, so the parent needs reserved capacity. (8) Liveness means material progress: checkpoint churn must not outrank a zero-material-progress signal. (9) Receipts persist normalized argv, the exact toolchain paths and the first diagnostic from both stdout and stderr.")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_6 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 6u32, "2026-10-17T18:21Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[6/12 benchmarking and A/B; spiral-samples alpha705 + brzozowski alpha54] Measured traps that are absent from today's backlog. A stale warm compiler session turned 2 s compiles into 8-24 s until it was cleaned up. Exact-graph cache hits (13-70 ms) are identity evidence only, never performance evidence. A cache key without the source SHA gave a false fresh hit after the source changed (the fix bound path + source SHA). Honest fresh pairs need a unique graph identity AND session teardown before every measured compile, both orders, and a symmetric cache nonce: a predecessor SHA that hit the cache made a Zeta candidate look +4.7% while fresh-vs-fresh was -14%. Four concurrent compiler supervisors contended and timed out, so the compiler family runs serially and parallelism goes inside the work. If both residual hashes are equal, classify a wall delta as lifecycle noise. Ratchet against the sealed control AND the best state promoted in the same wave. Put workload cardinality (active_calls) in the receipt. Rehydrating under a versioned path lost path-keyed incremental state, so keep shared build paths. A gate must never mutate canonical source: the burn-in gate restored its control and erased the candidate, so gates run in a sandbox copy.")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_7 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 7u32, "2026-10-17T18:21Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[7/12 edit and patch lessons; compiler alpha3006, samples alpha705, brzozowski alpha54, workspace alpha2891] For EOIE-EDIT-BATCH (also noted there). (1) Payloads come from files or stdin, not argv: a 497 KiB fs-write hit E2BIG, and escaped JSON telemetry lines could not be matched. (2) An exact-N repeated-match mode: text-replace is exact-one, so migrating one prefix across 19 rows needed script glue. (3) Row-local idempotency: the old apply-spec marked a row resolved when its replacement text existed anywhere in the file while the needle was still present, which left 3 needles in place. (4) Refuse an empty payload over a non-empty file unless expected-old-hash/--allow-empty: a failed upstream step wiped a Spiral source to 0 bytes. (5) Content-addressed inverse-patch and rollback receipts plus restore-from-seal --expect-hash (rollbacks kept tempting an external cp). (6) A text-only plan must not need the compiler: patch check/apply still route plans through a compiled provider that loses --compiler on re-entry (still true in today's help: patch apply takes --compiler, check does not). (7) Hash-checked append (journals were rewritten whole). (8) fs-write --parents. (9) A gated patch can run a cold-compile ratchet and roll back when the time delta crosses a threshold.")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_8 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 8u32, "2026-10-17T18:21Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[8/12 read/query/census lessons] For EOIE-READ-QUERY and EOIE-TABLE-QUERY. Piping search into head made eoie panic on EPIPE: a closed pipe is a normal early stop. The root '.' was accepted by fs-list but rejected by fs-search/parallel-search/parallel-manifest, so one root sentinel should work for every verb. Native per-file line counts were the most repeated gap: per-file LOC needed wc in about 15 rounds. source-stats counted evidence/probe .spi snapshots as own code and reported a bogus empty cargo_crate_max while source-topology said 98 LOC, so one classifier is needed (own/generated/vendor/reference/evidence). Typed JSONL window/join queries (first terminal failure -> owning job -> preceding events -> run_end) replaced jq/python in every compiler diagnosis round. A typed TSV upsert keyed by column avoids tab/escape corruption (a literal backslash-t got into agile rows). Help and dispatch should come from one registry: many advertised proxy verbs had no help schema. Also wanted: bounded tail, numbered slices, executable discovery, and a workspace lineage diff before diagnosis (a recovered tree looked like a stale binary until a diff showed interrupted next-round edits).")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_9 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 9u32, "2026-10-17T18:22Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[9/12 static authority audits, a new idea; compiler alpha3006 agile_eoie.md, Sep 2026] Each compiler bug in that lane was an authority inversion that a cheap compilerless audit could have flagged. (a) A field documented as a retry hint (progressYieldCount) took part in a semantic closure predicate, so the hint vetoed completion. Classify each field as authority/evidence/projection/hint and report every predicate where a hint mints or vetoes authority. (b) One boolean gated two different authority domains, so propose a DU split. (c) A DU or option was flattened into bools/strings and recombined in a later match, which is anti-drift; keep one sum type up to the serialization boundary. (d) Comparisons used rendered type text or nominal names where an exact interned identity existed (two operands both printed as file_type). (e) A fallback scan weakened identity (exact owner -> same backend -> same key) and borrowed another owner's body. (f) A clock or age carried across an epoch boundary without a carry-forward witness (stasis age inherited from the previous attempt). This fits the no-comments/unions direction and EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION. Proposal: one item EOIE-AUTHORITY-AUDIT (a Spiral-side lint over our own sources) instead of more prose rules.")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_10 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 10u32, "2026-10-17T18:22Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[10/12 continuity and claims; tritcritnet alpha716 docs\\vision\\readme_eoie.md (Feb 2026), core alpha9237 readmes (Jun), samples alpha705] Feeds EOIE-MEMORY. EOIE was framed as 'the body that makes the brain leave fingerprints', with memory in receipts/state, not the model. Missing today: (1) a resume compiler that builds the next-turn agenda from the previous ledger (passed = skip or verify cheaply, failed dep = block downstream, expected failure = tolerated, stale report = regenerate, partial patch = repair plan); (2) an incident-resume summary (active lease, newest savepoint, files changed since, newest receipts, toolchain identity) in one call; (3) a claim boundary on every report (passed/blocked/synthetic/oracle/proxy/tolerated) so progress words cannot outrun evidence; (4) sober regauge: 1000 means no material blockers plus external proof, with an explicit denominator (whole codebase vs local frontier); the old gauge inflated to 99x/1000; (5) a seal binds the tree fingerprint and the bundle fails if the tree changed after closeout (v664 shipped without late work); (6) reports are projections of one typed receipt (prose overwrote a planner metric: 113458 vs 113408), and reconcile-sidecars fails on contradictions; (7) self-hosting: eoie changes only through eoie verbs, with receipts.")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_11 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 11u32, "2026-10-17T18:22Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[11/12 toolchain custody and debugging method] Toolchain (brzozowski alpha54, samples alpha705): (a) compiler identity = content fingerprints + a mandatory capability probe, not a wrapper hash; identical spiral-compile wrappers sat on top of a good and a bad companion. (b) The 'latest' lane was a functional subset of the stable one, so reconcile by capability diff (superset). (c) Health is per capability and history-aware: SDK ok / compiler attested / restore smoke failing were separate states, and a stale not-attested bit outlived a later success. (d) Typed, validated env profiles: a malformed export gave SPIRAL_DOTNET=/dotnet; a gate binds its runtime profile (one-shot FSI vs the persistent daemon are not equivalent and gave false 10 s near-misses). (e) Rehydrate preserves executable bits. Method (bootstrap_llm_compiler v49-v101 Jan 2026, bootstrap_llm_ps1 v1.0 01-05): a technique enters the compendium only with an error signature, a canonical patch, a verification command and an artifact, and is deprecated, not deleted, when obsolete; write the next artifact to disk before patching; fix one class of diff per iteration; exact parity uses structural counters + sha/bytes + a similarity ratio that must rise monotonically; anchors must match exactly once and patches stay at most ~20 lines; for large logs count first (EJP code and site histograms, repeating frame cycles) before reading.")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_12 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 12u32, "2026-10-17T18:25Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[12/12 gate lessons from dev1\\eoie\\agile_eoie.md (2026-08-11, a Rust ERP-consolidation lane)] Generic rules that still apply here: a zero-test green run is not green (refuse compile-only success as semantic closure); green owner tests cannot erase a public API change (keep it open); a source-freshness gate before any executable is treated as authoritative (a stale auditor binary produced 5 critical rows, rebuilt in 0.48 s down to 2); the old eoie zip dropped every path segment named build/builder/target, including vendor\\clap_builder\\src\\builder, while keeping .cargo-checksum.json, so exclusions must be root-aware with a post-zip closure check against the checksums; a scoped transaction must not overwrite a wider-scope receipt (owner slice vs global audit); big sidecars keep counts plus a bounded sample (78.8 MB -> 1.7 MB with no lost counts); patch the compiled owner resolved through facade re-exports, not a same-named stale shadow file; installer success requires discovering the executable and running cargo --version, not a success-looking extraction log; prefer owner-filtered incremental gates and persist timeout receipts instead of reading a monolithic timeout as a semantic failure.")
inl note_of_bundle_wisdom_13 () = Note ("BUNDLE-WISDOM", 13u32, "2026-10-17T18:25Z", "bundle-wisdom", "[summary for the main lane] Proposed new items (not added; main decides): EOIE-LEASE (the time box as one monotonic authority, a WRAP-safe closeout write class, new-turn epoch rollover, begin-at for resumed chats; note 4), EOIE-AUTHORITY-AUDIT (hint/authority, DU-flattening and identity-weakening lint; note 9), BENCH-FRESH-PAIR (lifecycle-owned cold A/B with teardown, symmetric nonce, both orders, best-current ratchet, hash rollback; note 6), EOIE-RESUME-COMPILER (next-turn agenda from the ledger plus incident-resume; note 10). Item notes posted: EOIE-ONE-CALL, EOIE-JOBS, EOIE-EDIT-BATCH, EOIE-READ-QUERY, EOIE-TABLE-QUERY, EOIE-MEMORY, EOIE-MEMORY-BOOTSTRAP, EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION, HARNESS-RUN-ONLY-USEFUL, KINO-SOTA, CROSS-BACKEND-BENCHMARK, HOPACSOTA, BUNDLER-DEBUG-STACK-OVERFLOW, MEGA-SAMPLES-TOGETHER, LEAN-BACKEND, TOOLCHAIN-LATEST. Projects we are not advancing (listed only): stroma/pdf2stroma (PDF append-index merge, glyph spacing cmap, table reconstruction; AGILE.toml alpha518 2026-06-06, core AGILE.md alpha9080/9103 June), tritcritnet (telemetry critic fixtures), mechahaze (render smoke pack), and the ERP-consolidation lane in dev1. No Tibia material was found in the bundles. Extracted single entries live in C:\\Users\\i574n\\.claude\\jobs\\5a6e53fb\\tmp\\bundle-wisdom (scratch; Downloads untouched).")
inl eoie_lease () = Task ("EOIE-LEASE", "From the January 2026 bootstrap bundles (BUNDLE-WISDOM note 4): one time-box authority for agile begin/lease; a write class allowed during WRAP so closeout writes are never blocked; lease rollover on every new user turn (a stale lease survived into the next turn 5+ times); start-at for resumed chats; status and mutation must agree", Story, P2, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,agile,lease", "current", "current")
inl eoie_authority_audit () = Task ("EOIE-AUTHORITY-AUDIT", "Compilerless audit from the bundle era (BUNDLE-WISDOM note 9): a lint that catches fields or flags read with the wrong authority (a value trusted from a source that doesn't own it), as a one-call eoie verb with Spiral test blocks", Story, P3, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,audit", "current", "current")
inl bench_fresh_pair () = Task ("BENCH-FRESH-PAIR", "Honest benchmarking (BUNDLE-WISDOM note 6: stale warm compiler sessions and cache hits once faked regressions): one cold A/B command that tears down warm servers and memos before each compile, interleaves A and B, and rolls back automatically; fits the run ledger and CI-OFFLOAD", Story, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "bench,harness", "current", "current")
inl eoie_resume_compiler () = Task ("EOIE-RESUME-COMPILER", "Continuity from the bundle era (BUNDLE-WISDOM note 10): a 'resume compiler' verb that builds the next turn's agenda from backlog state, recent receipts and open leases, with claim boundaries on reports and seals bound to the tree state; pairs with EOIE-MEMORY recall", Story, P2, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,continuity", "current", "current")
inl gleam_try_rescue () = Task ("GLEAM-TRY-RESCUE", "Gleam try_unit and try_finally for real", Story, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "spiral,gleam,lib", "current", "current")
inl note_of_gleam_try_rescue_1 () = Note ("GLEAM-TRY-RESCUE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T18:50Z", "main-thread", "14:50 main-thread: verified lib/spiral/base.spi:695 - on Gleam, try_unit and try_finally emit only a comment (no rescue, no cleanup). Blocks KINO-WITHOUT-ELIXIR (runner deadline + tree kill need cleanup on crash). Proposed by kino-native-design: a spawn-and-monitor arm (spawn the body, monitor it, run finally on DOWN, re-raise the exit reason), Erlang built-ins only since a Spiral package cannot carry an .erl file. Also: lib/spiral/platform.spi is_windows imports gleam/erlang/os, gone in gleam_erlang 1.x; it needs an os:type external before any bump.")
inl note_of_gleam_try_rescue_2 () = Note ("GLEAM-TRY-RESCUE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T20:12Z", "main-thread", "16:12 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: use lpil's hex package exception (rescue + defer) for the Gleam arms of base.try_unit and base.try_finally.")
inl lib_canon_names () = Task ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", "Canon names for every lib file", Epic, P2, D7, 0u32, Planned, "", "lib,naming,i18n,edition", "current", "current")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_1 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 1u32, "2026-10-17T19:02Z", "main-thread", "15:02 main-thread, user 2026-10-17: the 1k-line limit is a Rust crate concern only. On the Spiral side we want the FEWEST files, fragmented by generic concept, not by dotnet or Fable jargon (the lib/apps layout was polluted by Fable hacking and mimicking .NET names). Drop base and core and every other inherited name for the canon term of each pattern, renaming or deleting lib files as needed, and apply the same to new code from rewrites in eoie, x, the renamed Kino and other repos. Without drift: each rename keeps the corpus green (lib sweep, harness, eoie tests) and lands with a reference count from LIB-USAGE-DIAGNOSTICS. The canon English term is picked with help from MULTILINGUAL-PRESENTATION (translations into native scripts inform the English choice).")
inl note_of_lib_canon_names_2 () = Note ("LIB-CANON-NAMES", 2u32, "2026-10-17T19:02Z", "main-thread", "15:02 main-thread: user motto for this epic, verbatim: we're opus 5.5 (rick's voice) [put that into eoie! hah! (hic)]")
inl cli_args_style () = Task ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", "Typed CLI arguments derived from unions", Story, P2, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "cli,pwsh-free,reflection,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_1 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 1u32, "2026-10-17T19:11Z", "styles-survey", "15:10 styles-survey [1/3 clap today] clap users: spiral apps/spiral (6 subcommands), apps/wasm spiral_wasm, alphabet apps/documents (1 command, 6 args); Cargo clap = 4.5 with default features (no derive, so no proc macros), lock 4.5.18 + 11 transitive crates (clap_builder, clap_lex, anstream, anstyle x4, colorchoice, strsim, utf8parse, is_terminal_polyfill). Binding: lib/spiral/runtime.spi lines 463-773, ~310 lines, 37 inl wrappers over the builder API (Command new/arg/subcommand/subcommand_required/args_override_self/get_matches(_from)/debug_assert, Arg required/short/long/default_value/num_args/value_names/value_parser/action/index, ValueRange, PossibleValuesParser, ArgMatches get_one/get_flag/get_many/subcommand) as nominal rust_type per clap type; arg_union already derives possible values from a union by reflection (get_union_fields_untag). Typing is stringly at the boundary: args are a record of (long, short) string tuples (get_args), subcommands matched by string equality in run, values come back as option std_string keyed by name, then union_try_pick. Cost in spiral: get_args 29 + get_command 122 lines for 6 verbs (~25 lines per verb) plus ~7 lines per extracted arg in run; generated clap get_command ~290 of the 24,280 spiral.rs lines (SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR note 2). Help/usage/suggestions come free from clap.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_2 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 2u32, "2026-10-17T19:11Z", "styles-survey", "15:11 styles-survey [2/3 hand-rolled] eoie: no clap anywhere; rust_std_env/model.spi (18 lines) wraps std::env::args().skip(2).nth(i) as argument_owned/argument_equals/args_len_is; each verb checks arity by count (task_notes author_choice: count 4 or 6, then --as at index 4), ~25 of 78 lines in agile_task_notes are arg handling; usage text is hand strings (agile_subcommand_spec: 18 cases, shape is an untyped string like <root> <id> <text> [--as <author>]); top-level dispatch and 32 proxy routes are numeric codes inside a rust_global blob (eoie_cli_routing/routing_terminal.spi); help/--json/--schema rendered from descriptor tables. Subcommands are a union only since the new agile_subcommand_domain (union_cases_fold), args are never records. x apps/client: typed hand parser, cli.spi 408 lines for 3 verbs + 12 flags: union command (5 cases), union flag (8), union switch (4), records request/draft/filters, parse_args_with over list string, flag names by if-chains, usage hand-written (28 lines); then flattened to command_fields (kind i32 + 13 scalars) for the Export boundary in args.spi (76 lines -> args.rs 994 lines, at the 1k gate). Per verb: clap ~25 decl lines + extraction, eoie ~25 parse lines spread over up to 5 synced tables, x ~130 lines incl. validation. Demand: 124 tracked .ps1 (8,181 lines) in spiral/polyglot/alphabet/dice/x/eoie/i574n.github, 112 of them with a param() block, i.e. ~112 future typed CLIs.")
inl note_of_cli_args_style_3 () = Note ("CLI-ARGS-STYLE", 3u32, "2026-10-17T19:11Z", "styles-survey", "15:12 styles-survey [3/3 recommendation: (b), clap retired after migration] Build our own typed parsing in lib, derived by reflection: a subcommand is a union case, its operands a record (string/i32/bool/option/list/union fields map to positional, valued, switch, optional, repeated, possible-values), and one exhaustive spec case -> {name; summary} gives usage, help, --json schema, completion and actionable errors (eoie style intent/usage/valid/corrected) from the same source. Use the compile-time fold of eoie union_reflection/cases.spi, not lib reflection, whose union_try_pick builds a runtime list + array per call. Why not clap: its ids and get_one results are strings, so a typed layer is needed on top anyway; clap only exists for the Rust backend; 12 crates per CLI tool (no proc macros, so the compile cost is one cached clap_builder per workspace; binary cost not measured here, typically a few hundred KB vs 6.1 MB spiral.exe / 8.4 MB eoie.exe); and the help/suggestion polish is what the derived spec renders anyway. Why not hybrid: two parsers to keep in sync is the drift eoie already shows. Gate rules: keep parse/usage helpers as let (an inl fold took agile_mutation.rs 614 -> 1567 lines), one crate per tool. Migration: x first (smallest typed parser, proves records), then eoie agile, then SPIRAL-CLI-GRANULAR drops runtime.spi clap (~310 lines). Needs EOIE-UNION-REFLECTION (case enumeration) and an Export that carries unions.")
inl hangulize_in_spiral () = Task ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", "Hangulize as a Spiral transcription library", Story, P3, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "alphabet,transcription,lib,typefeatureset", "current", "current")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_1 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 1u32, "2026-10-17T19:12Z", "styles-survey", "15:16 styles-survey [1/3 where and what] alphabet apps/documents (documents.spi hangul, lines 118-196) writes <name>.hangul.md (+ .html/.pdf/.epub): it pipes the trimmed text to the hangulize binary as a shell command string (deps/hangulize/cmd/hangulize/hangulize.exe <spec>), spec por-br by default or eng when the text contains the and and, then interleaves each transcribed line with the original (transcribe_only drops the original; code fences and URL lines kept). Dependency: not vendored, not a submodule; scripts/dep_hangulize.ps1 (called by init.ps1) git-clones the i574n/hangulize fork into gitignored deps/hangulize, installs Go via winget/apt and runs go build. The fork = upstream hangulize/hangulize (go 1.19; deps cobra, kagome.ipadic, go-pinyin, go_hangul, x/text) + 2 own commits (2025-06-22/28): an English spec via CMU dict (translit/english, cmudict.dict 134,429 lines / 3.9 MB, specs/eng.hsl ~300 lines, jamo.go and procedure.go tweaks). Size: 92 Go files / 7,513 lines (57 non-test / 5,148: pkg/hre regex dialect 1,321, pkg/hsl spec lexer+parser 761, translit furigana/pinyin/cyrillic/english 689, core procedure/rule/spec/script), 41 .hsl specs / 12,243 lines with 4,202 inline test examples, web/ UI 125 files. Only consumer in our repos: alphabet documents.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_2 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 2u32, "2026-10-17T19:12Z", "styles-survey", "15:17 styles-survey [2/3 the hangul experiment] alphabet apps/hangul is an F# port of suapapa/go_hangul (sibling clone C:\\home\\git\\go_hangul, 746 Go lines, last upstream 2024-01-08): hangul.fs 494 lines (jamo constants, syllable compose/split, hanjaConvert, isHanja), hanja.spi 4,586 lines (one hanja_to_hangul_table array, 477 KB), cp949_from.fs + cp949_to.fs 17,051 + 17,053 lines (733 KB each), hangul.livemd 931 lines, package.spiproj on deps/polyglot/deps/spiral/lib. 15 commits since 2025-06-20; last code change 2025-11-19 (84-line .dib edit); 2026-10-06/07 only the dib -> livemd migration and build.ps1 edits. Nothing consumes it: documents calls the Go binary, scripts/build.ps1 only runs its notebook export. It is F#/dotnet-only, so it conflicts with the native direction; worth keeping is the jamo compose/split logic (~200 lines) as the seed of the Hangul output layer, and the hanja table as data; cp949 tables are unnecessary in a UTF-8 pipeline.")
inl note_of_hangulize_in_spiral_3 () = Note ("HANGULIZE-IN-SPIRAL", 3u32, "2026-10-17T19:12Z", "styles-survey", "15:18 styles-survey [3/3 assessment + name] Concrete gains of a Spiral rewrite: (1) specs as typed data instead of runtime-parsed text: hangulize lexes/parses every .hsl (761 lines) and compiles HRE patterns to Go regexp at load (1,321 lines); in Spiral a spec is a record of vars/macros/normalize/rewrite/transcribe rules and inl partial evaluation specializes the rewrite engine per language at compile time (first Futamura projection), so no regex engine or spec parser ships. (2) GADT rule AST indexed by phase (normalize -> rewrite -> transcribe), so a rule cannot run in the wrong phase; existentials for per-language pre-transliterators (pinyin, furigana, cyrillic, CMU English) as packs of state + step; a prototype transcriber over output scripts (Hangul first, others later); reflection derives the language union, the CLI choice (CLI-ARGS-STYLE) and docs. (3) One codebase for every backend: documents stops shelling out to Go and needs no Go toolchain (dep_hangulize.ps1 retires, PWSH-FREE); the same lib serves wasm/JS for a web page. (4) Tests: the 4,202 spec examples become inline expectations / Spiral test blocks, with the Go binary as differential oracle until parity. Costs: dictionaries (kagome ipadic, pinyin, 3.9 MB cmudict) need Rust crates or embedded data; scope first only por-br + eng (the two used). Proposed generic lib name: transcription (lib/spiral/transcription: rules, hangul compose, specs/por_br, specs/eng); transliteration is the wrong word, the specs map sounds, not letters.")
inl eoie_uses_lib () = Task ("EOIE-USES-LIB", "eoie builds on lib like alphabet", Feature, P1, D6, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,lib,dogfood", "current", "current")
inl note_of_eoie_uses_lib_1 () = Note ("EOIE-USES-LIB", 1u32, "2026-10-17T19:44Z", "main-thread", "15:44 main-thread root cause (verified): alphabet reaches lib through packageDir ../../deps/polyglot/deps/spiral/lib with |core- and spiral-, a copy inside its own tree. eoie regenerates in a hermetic staging copy (compiler-contracts/test-regeneration.ps1 copies only the eoie tree, rejects links as Unexpected workspace link, skips vendor), so a packageDir into the sibling spiral repo would dangle; plus every eoie package is no-core. That is why eoie re-grew reflection (pipe tables), sm' (rust_std_string), core operators (i32_eq helpers) and option (validation_result). Plan: (1) a pinned lib snapshot staged with eoie (a deps/spiral-lib copy refreshed by a verb, or the staging step copying a hash-pinned lib), (2) a core-only probe crate to measure the 1k-gate cost of core inlining, (3) move eoie's generic pieces into lib (union_cases_fold is now in lib reflection.spi too), (4) switch eoie packages to |core- one owner at a time, removing the copies. User 2026-10-17: make eoie use lib like alphabet does; reuse what you can, move what is generic.")
inl note_of_eoie_uses_lib_2 () = Note ("EOIE-USES-LIB", 2u32, "2026-10-17T19:52Z", "main-thread", "15:52 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: staged pinned snapshot. eoie keeps a pin file with the hash of spiral/lib; regeneration copies that lib next to the staged eoie tree; packages point at it with packageDir and use |core- and spiral-. No committed lib copy in eoie; a verb refreshes the pin.")
inl note_of_eoie_uses_lib_3 () = Note ("EOIE-USES-LIB", 3u32, "2026-10-17T20:12Z", "main-thread", "16:12 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17 (later round): the staged lib follows the tree. Every regeneration stages the current spiral/lib (no pin file, no refresh verb).")
inl note_of_eoie_uses_lib_4 () = Note ("EOIE-USES-LIB", 4u32, "2026-10-17T20:13Z", "main-thread", "16:13 main-thread, user decision 2026-10-17: the 1,685 raw comparison ops in eoie (1,155 LT, 530 EQ, plus NEQ/LTE/Add/Mult/Sub) wait for this item: once packages get |core-, one mechanical pass per owner rewrites them to plain operators; no interim helper layer. Same for the 10 StaticStringConcat sites (6 of them added today in union_reflection and the agile domain).")
inl eoie_experiments () = Task ("EOIE-EXPERIMENTS", "Pre-registered experiment ledger, one call per step: register (question, arms, run count, primary metric, test, decision rule, timestamp) before any data; record runs from a results table; analyze exactly once at the planned count (Welch two-sided with CI, one-sample t against a prediction, critical values from the Student quantile); refuse or flag any read of the primary metric before the planned count (interim-look guard with a disclosure record); post-hoc addenda kept apart from the verdict; a rendered report. Replaces hand-written analysis scripts and markdown pre-registration tables.", Feature, P1, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,experiments,stats,one-call", "current", "current")
inl eoie_machine_windows () = Task ("EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS", "Exclusive machine windows shared by all sessions on one box, one call each: request a window class (live GUI runs, test-suite runs, compiler installs, heavy builds) with an expected duration, get granted/queued, release; conflicts between classes declared once (no suites during GUI runs, no compiler use during installs); free-memory admission; receipts readable by every client. Replaces chat messages like \"window open/done\" and the separate heavy-gate script.", Feature, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,coordination,one-call,sessions", "current", "current")
inl eoie_standards_checks () = Task ("EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS", "One-call code-standard checks per package, declared in the package (spiproj or a standards file): no comment lines (directives excepted), no floating point in modules marked integer-only, every test block names its target, line length, no CRLF where the repo uses LF; machine-readable findings; usable as a gate.", Feature, P2, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,standards,checks,one-call", "current", "current")
inl eoie_agile_list_fields () = Task ("EOIE-AGILE-LIST-FIELDS", "agile list --json lacks fields agents need for grooming without one show per item: notes count, last note time, deps, tags, created time; plus filters by id prefix and tag, and an oldest-first order (by created time). Today grooming \"oldest first\" needs guessing from title dates.", TaskKind, P2, D2, 0u32, Planned, "", "eoie,agile,json", "current", "current")
inl spiral_diagnostic_locations () = Task ("SPIRAL-DIAGNOSTIC-LOCATIONS", "Every type error carries file:line:col and the binding it is in. Seen without a location: ETY-ValueRestriction (\"Metavars that are not part of the enclosing function signature are not allowed\", from an unsuffixed literal flowing into conv) and ETY-MetavarsNotAllowedInRecordWith; the agent had to bisect by hand.", TaskKind, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "spiral,diagnostics,compiler", "current", "current")
inl spiral_record_with_inference () = Task ("SPIRAL-RECORD-WITH-INFERENCE", "Record update on an expression, {(f x) with field = v}, fails with ETY-MetavarsNotAllowedInRecordWith unless the expression is first bound with a type annotation; infer the record type from the expression when it is known.", TaskKind, P2, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "spiral,inference,records", "current", "current")
inl spiral_macro_field_splice () = Task ("SPIRAL-MACRO-FIELD-SPLICE", "In $\"...\" macros, !x.field splices x and then emits the literal text .field into the target (the C compiler then fails on a tuple member). Either splice the field access or reject it with a hint to bind the field first.", TaskKind, P2, D2, 0u32, Planned, "", "spiral,macros,diagnostics", "current", "current")
inl spiral_unforgeable_witnesses () = Task ("SPIRAL-UNFORGEABLE-WITNESSES", "Typed-domain gaps: module-private union constructors, so a proof value (readiness witness, typestate) can only be built by its module; and a nominal-preserving record update, so {r with field = v} cannot silently produce a new structural record type with a changed field type.", Feature, P2, D5, 0u32, Planned, "", "spiral,types,gadt,modeling", "current", "current")
inl spiral_literal_defaults () = Task ("SPIRAL-LITERAL-DEFAULTS", "Unsuffixed integer literals summed into a local leave a numeric metavar that later breaks conv (value restriction). Check whether per-package default-int (pack-1k) closes it; if not, default literals in local bindings or point the error at the literal.", TaskKind, P3, D2, 0u32, Planned, "", "spiral,inference,literals", "current", "current")
inl gpu_rental_readiness () = Task ("GPU-RENTAL-READINESS", "Run WGSL lane workloads on rented GPUs at scale: a headless GPU runner outside Windows (Linux, Vulkan/Dawn or wgpu), many-launch batching and multi-GPU dispatch, u64/i64 emulation for integer overflow budgets on WGSL, a storage boundary designed once over host arrays and GPU storage buffers (prototype instances per backend), a throughput-per-dollar benchmark, and a reproducible remote setup script; budget tranches need the user's approval.", Epic, P2, D6, 0u32, Planned, "", "gpu,wgsl,infra,backend", "current", "current")
inl cells_repin () = Task ("CELLS-REPIN", "A failing _assert_eq reports actual and expected as structured data in the run summary, and one call rewrites the literal in the source to the actual value (per test, with a diff to confirm). Today agents scrape the notebook output with regexes and edit long tuple literals by hand after every intended model change.", Feature, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "cells,testing,one-call", "current", "current")
inl cells_gate () = Task ("CELLS-GATE", "One-call gate over a list of files: memo off, per-file and total pass/fail/executed/memo-hit summary, failed test names with actual/expected, daemon retired at the end, deferral while GUI or live runs hold the machine (see EOIE-MACHINE-WINDOWS); multiple --test names or a pattern in one run.", Feature, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "CELLS-DAEMON-LIFECYCLE", "cells,testing,gate,one-call", "current", "current")
inl cells_daemon_lifecycle () = Task ("CELLS-DAEMON-LIFECYCLE", "Compiler daemons started by a run outlive it (about 2 GB each with their --server-tcp compilers) until someone sends retire by hand; retire on run end or after an idle timeout, and list live daemons with their owner run.", TaskKind, P1, D2, 0u32, Planned, "", "cells,daemon,memory", "current", "current")
inl cells_probes () = Task ("CELLS-PROBES", "First-class probe runs: compile and run a small main against a package's modules, optionally with text-patch variants of those modules, without copying the package into a scratch folder and without a test block; print the program output. Today experiments use a hand-made scratch project with copied sources and a variant script.", Feature, P1, D4, 0u32, Planned, "", "cells,probes,experiments", "current", "current")
inl cells_explicit_targets () = Task ("CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS", "Untargeted test blocks silently follow the global default backend, which changed (to zig); require a target per test block or a declared package default, and warn on untargeted blocks.", TaskKind, P2, D2, 0u32, Planned, "", "cells,targets,testing", "current", "current")
inl cells_new_project () = Task ("CELLS-NEW-PROJECT", "Project scaffold so a new app starts smooth: package.spiproj with package defaults, module and test-file layout, target conventions with reasons (C as oracle, WGSL for GPU, Lua for embedded hosts, Rust for host-only tests), standards checks and a gate command wired in, a scratch-probe setup, a backlog epic with a starter plan. Lessons from the first full project on test cells.", Feature, P1, D3, 0u32, Planned, "CELLS-GATE,CELLS-REPIN,CELLS-PROBES,CELLS-EXPLICIT-TARGETS,EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS", "cells,scaffold,onboarding", "current", "current")
inl cells_exhaustive_tests () = Task ("CELLS-EXHAUSTIVE-TESTS", "Bounded-domain exhaustive and property test blocks: compare two implementations over a declared domain (for example an integer reimplementation against a floating-point oracle over all inputs up to N), report the first counterexamples, run on the oracle backend only.", Feature, P3, D3, 0u32, Planned, "", "cells,testing,properties", "current", "current")
inl tibia_gpu_rental () = Task ("TIBIA-GPU-RENTAL", "Train at GPU scale on rented machines once the model ranks policies like live: needs GPU-RENTAL-READINESS and TIBIA-AGENT; local measurement 0.23 ms per arena clear per lane after ~4 s launch setup; estimate cost per million clears before asking the user for a budget tranche.", Feature, P2, D5, 0u32, Paused, "GPU-RENTAL-READINESS,TIBIA-AGENT", "tibia,gpu,training", "current", "current")
inl tibia_start_sync () = Task ("TIBIA-START-SYNC", "Harness: start every competitor's logic at the same moment after login (the policy probe acts ~2.5 s after login, bots at once); needed before the next win-seeking comparison.", TaskKind, P2, D2, 0u32, Paused, "", "tibia,harness,benchmark", "current", "current")
inl tibia_tools_to_eoie () = Task ("TIBIA-TOOLS-TO-EOIE", "Move tibia-local helper scripts onto the generic eoie verbs once they exist: pr1/pr2 analyses to EOIE-EXPERIMENTS, log and TSV questions to EOIE-TABLE-QUERY, harness and standards checks to EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS; BENCHMARK pre-registrations become experiment records (with TIBIA-DOCS-TO-CODE).", TaskKind, P2, D3, 0u32, Paused, "EOIE-EXPERIMENTS,EOIE-TABLE-QUERY,EOIE-STANDARDS-CHECKS", "tibia,eoie,dogfood", "current", "current")
